begin;
truncate entry_people, entry_related, timeline_events, entry_images, sources, people, entries, categories restart identity cascade;

Admiral Nimitz set an ambush with everything he had. Two carriers, Enterprise and Hornet, plus the Yorktown, patched up in 72 hours after Coral Sea damage the Japanese had written off as fatal. On the morning of 4 June, Nagumo''s four carriers struck Midway while American torpedo squadrons found the Japanese fleet and died almost to a man, Torpedo 8 lost all fifteen aircraft, pulling Japanese fighter cover down to sea level.

At 10:20 the American Dauntless dive-bombers arrived unopposed out of the sun and in five minutes turned three carriers into infernos. The fourth, Hiryu, was caught that afternoon. Japan lost four fleet carriers, a cruiser, and around 3,000 men, including irreplaceable veteran aircrews. America lost Yorktown and just over 300 men. The strategic initiative in the Pacific changed hands permanently.', 'Japan''s 1942 position was brilliant and brittle. The Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere supplied oil and rice, but the fleet had to defend a perimeter so vast that any single point could be overwhelmed. Yamamoto, who had studied at Harvard and knew American industry firsthand, wanted one annihilating battle before US shipyards, already launching a carrier roughly every few months, made the contest unwinnable.

America, meanwhile, was losing everywhere and reading the mail. Station HYPO''s partial breaks into JN-25 let Nimitz confirm Midway as the target through a celebrated ruse. Midway radioed in the clear that its water plant had broken, and Japanese intercepts dutifully reported the ''AF'' target short of water.', '["Yamamoto''s doctrine demanded a single decisive fleet action, and Midway, threatening Hawaii itself, was designed as bait America could not refuse.","American codebreaking gave Nimitz the Japanese plan, order of battle, and timetable, arguably the most valuable intelligence coup of the Pacific war.","Japan''s overextended perimeter forced Nagumo to split his attention between bombing Midway and guarding against a fleet that intelligence insisted was still at Pearl Harbor.","US industrial repair capacity. Yorktown''s battle damage, estimated by Japan as months of work, was patched in three days, giving Nimitz three decks instead of two."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1942-06-04","title":"Nagumo strikes Midway","description":"Japanese aircraft bomb the atoll while Nagumo rearms his reserve planes for a second strike, leaving decks full of fuel and ordnance."},{"date":"1942-06-04","title":"Torpedo squadrons annihilated","description":"Slow Devastators attack without fighter cover. Torpedo 8 loses all fifteen planes, but drags Japanese fighters to wave-top height."},{"date":"1942-06-04","title":"Five fateful minutes","description":"At 10:20 Dauntless dive-bombers from Enterprise and Yorktown fall out of the sun. Akagi, Kaga, and Soryu become burning wrecks."},{"date":"1942-06-04","title":"Hiryu''s revenge and death","description":"The surviving carrier cripples Yorktown, then is found and destroyed that afternoon, Japan''s carrier force ceases to exist."},{"date":"1942-06-07","title":"Yorktown sinks","description":"Abandoned after the air attacks, the carrier is torpedoed by a Japanese submarine while under tow."}]'::jsonb, '["Japan lost four fleet carriers and the core of its veteran naval aviators, losses its training pipeline could never replace.","The strategic initiative passed permanently to the United States, which went on the offensive at Guadalcanal within two months.","The battleship era ended in practice. No battleship gunfire decided anything, while carrier aircraft decided everything.","Codebreaking was proven as a war-winning capability, shaping intelligence services for the rest of the century."]'::jsonb, '["The water-plant ruse was real tradecraft. Midway broadcast the breakdown in the clear, and within 48 hours Japanese intercepts confirmed ''AF is short of water.''","Yorktown''s repair crews worked around the clock for 72 hours in drydock while the ship took on fuel, welders were reportedly still aboard as she sailed.","Ensign George Gay, sole survivor of Torpedo 8''s thirty aircrew, floated through the battle and watched the dive-bomber attack from the water.","Japan''s public was told of a great victory. The loss of four carriers stayed secret, and survivors were quarantined from their own families.","Both sides'' battleships, the supposed arbiters of naval power, never fired a shot at an enemy ship during the entire battle."]'::jsonb, 'Station HYPO''s triumph made signals intelligence a permanent pillar of warfare. The battle is still taught as the archetype of the ambush enabled by intelligence, and of how five minutes of vulnerability can decide a war.', 'Midway is the Pacific war''s hinge. The last battle Japan fought on its own terms and the first it fought on America''s. It validated codebreaking as a war-winning weapon, proved the carrier, not the battleship, now ruled the sea, and bought the breathing room for the Guadalcanal offensive two months later. Japan never again took the strategic offensive.', '/images/plates/battle-of-midway.svg', '["World War II","Pacific War","Naval Warfare","Codebreaking"]'::jsonb);

The invasion began just after midnight on 6 June, when British glider troops seized Pegasus Bridge and American paratroopers scattered across the Cotentin peninsula. At dawn the naval bombardment lifted and landing craft ground ashore. Four beaches fell at bearable cost. At Omaha, cliffs, intact defences, and rough seas turned the landing into a slaughter that junior officers and sergeants salvaged by improvisation.

By midnight the Allies held all five beachheads at a cost of some 10,000 casualties. The breakout took two more months of hedgerow fighting, but the lodgement never failed. Within eleven months Allied armies met the Russians in the middle of a ruined Germany.', 'After Stalingrad and the expulsion of Axis forces from Africa, the Allies faced a choice. Invade France across the Channel or push up through Italy. Churchill, haunted by the Somme and Gallipoli, preferred the Mediterranean periphery. The Americans, with their manpower and industry, insisted on the shortest road to Berlin. At Tehran in late 1943 Roosevelt and Stalin overruled Churchill and fixed the cross-Channel attack for the spring of 1944.

Meanwhile Germany fortified the coast. Rommel, given command of Army Group B, covered the beaches with millions of mines, steel obstacles, and concrete gun positions, the Atlantic Wall, and argued the invasion must be thrown back within 24 hours, before Allied air power and artillery could build up. Hitler, convinced the real landing would come at Calais, held the Panzer reserves under his personal control, a decision that would paralyze the German response.', '["Soviet pressure. Stalin demanded a western front to relieve the Eastern Front, where the USSR was absorbing the overwhelming bulk of German land power.","American industrial might finally made a cross-Channel assault feasible, landing craft, aircraft, and shipping existed in 1944 in quantities unimaginable in 1942.","The strategic failure of the Italian campaign to knock Germany out. Italy surrendered in 1943 but German armies simply occupied the peninsula, proving a peripheral strategy could not end the war.","Deception (Operation Bodyguard) convinced Hitler the blow would fall at Calais, freezing fifteen German divisions there while Normandy burned."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1944-06-06","title":"Pegasus Bridge, 00:16","description":"Three gliders land within metres of the Caen canal bridge. Major Howard''s men seize it in ten minutes, the first Allied objective taken on D-Day."},{"date":"1944-06-06","title":"Omaha Beach","description":"Intact German defences and rough seas turn the American landing into a morning-long slaughter. Small groups scale the bluffs and the beachhead holds by nightfall."},{"date":"1944-06-06","title":"Five beachheads linked","description":"Utah, Gold, Juno, and Sword fall at lower cost. By midnight some 156,000 men are ashore and the Atlantic Wall is breached for good."},{"date":"1944-07-25","title":"Operation Cobra","description":"After seven weeks stuck in the hedgerows, American bombers blast a corridor at Saint-Lô and Patton''s tanks pour through into open country."},{"date":"1944-08-19","title":"Falaise Pocket","description":"Allied armies encircle and destroy two German armies in Normandy. Paris is liberated within days."}]'::jsonb, '["Germany was forced into the two-front war it had fought since 1941 to avoid, and its western armies were ground down in eleven months.","France was liberated by Western armies rather than Soviet ones, shaping post-war Europe''s political geography.","The Soviet advance continued regardless, the second front shortened the war but did not stop Stalin from taking Eastern Europe.","Some 10,000 Allied casualties on the day itself, and roughly 200,000 military dead plus tens of thousands of French civilians across the Normandy campaign."]'::jsonb, '["Eisenhower wrote a failure note in advance, accepting sole blame in case the landings were repulsed, and misdated it 5 July instead of 5 June.","Two artificial Mulberry harbours, built in England and towed across the Channel, unloaded men and supplies until a great storm wrecked the American one on 19 June.","German commander Rommel was home in Germany celebrating his wife''s birthday on 6 June. Several key officers were away at a war game in Rennes.","Allied meteorologists predicted a brief break in Channel storms that German forecasters missed, Eisenhower launched on that single forecast.","The first casualty of D-Day is often given as Lieutenant Den Brotheridge, killed storming Pegasus Bridge minutes after midnight."]'::jsonb, 'The Normandy cemeteries and museums form Europe''s densest landscape of remembrance. 6 June is still commemorated by heads of state. Militarily, Overlord proved that sea power plus air power plus industrial logistics could project an army onto a fortified continent, a lesson studied in every staff college on earth.', 'D-Day ended Germany''s ability to fight a one-front war and guaranteed that Western Europe would be liberated by democracies rather than occupied by Stalin. The operational template, deception, airborne drops, naval gunfire, artificial harbours, and overwhelming logistics, became the textbook for every amphibious operation since, and the images of men wading ashore under fire remain the defining visual memory of the Second World War in the West.', '/images/plates/d-day.svg', '["World War II","Western Front","Amphibious Warfare"]'::jsonb);

On Christmas Eve and Christmas Day, at dozens of points along the British-German line especially, firing stopped by unspoken agreement. Men retrieved and buried the dead, singing parties from one trench answered by the other, then climbed out to meet in No Man''s Land. They exchanged food, drink, buttons, and addresses. Held joint burial services. And in several places played football. Officers mostly tolerated what they could not have stopped.

It never happened again. High commands on both sides, alarmed, issued explicit orders against fraternization, rotated units, and ensured that artillery fired through subsequent Christmases. The war went on for four more years and killed millions, which is precisely why the one day it stopped is remembered.', 'The truce grew from specific conditions of 1914. The armies were still largely volunteers and regulars who felt some chivalric kinship, the trenches in places lay barely thirty metres apart, and the war was young enough that hatred had not yet curdled. Saxon troops, historically sympathetic to the British, fraternized most readily. Prussian units, less so.

Both armies also shared the same muddy Christmas. Plum puddings and Princess Mary''s gift boxes on one side, Christmas trees the Germans mounted on their parapets on the other. Homesickness was the common language.', '["Proximity. Trenches close enough to shout across made the enemy human in a way artillery never allowed.","Shared Christian ritual. Christmas carols needed no translation, and midnight services drew both sides above the parapet.","The practical problem of the unburied dead rotting between the lines, burial truces were the thin end of the wedge.","A war still young. 1914 soldiers had not yet absorbed years of gas, attrition, and propaganda about Hun barbarity."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1914-12-24","title":"Silent Night across the lines","description":"German trees appear on parapets. Carols are traded trench to trench."},{"date":"1914-12-25","title":"No Man''s Land","description":"Burials, gift exchanges, joint services, and football in several sectors."},{"date":"1914-12-26","title":"Orders against repeat","description":"Commands on both sides forbid fraternization. Artillery is ordered to fire through future Christmases."}]'::jsonb, '["No repeat was ever permitted. Artillery fired through every subsequent Christmas of the war by explicit order.","Fraternization doctrine hardened, rotation, propaganda, and discipline designed to prevent familiarity with the enemy.","The memory became anti-war shorthand, invoked in every debate about soldiers'' humanity versus armies'' purposes.","Commercialized remembrance (notably 2014''s centenary) sparked its own debate about prettifying industrial slaughter."]'::jsonb, '["It was never universal. Many sectors fought through Christmas normally, and the French and Belgian lines saw far less fraternization.","The football matches, though real in several places, were mostly kickabouts rather than organized games, the famous 3–2 scorelines are later embroidery.","Both high commands reacted with fury. Courts-martial were threatened, units were rotated, and some officers who tolerated the truce were disciplined.","Princess Mary''s 1914 gift box, tobacco, chocolate, and a Christmas card, reached hundreds of thousands of British troops that week.","The last known participant, Alfred Anderson of the Black Watch, died in 2005 aged 109, the truce lived within living memory into this century."]'::jsonb, 'Memorialized at Ploegsteert, in films, songs, and Sainsbury''s famous 2014 advertisement, itself controversial for prettifying the mud. Military doctrine drew the opposite lesson. Keep units rotating, keep hatreds fed, and never let Christmas happen again.', 'The truce is the most cited evidence that the war''s soldiers were not the war''s hatred, ordinary men suspended industrial slaughter for a day, entirely without orders. It haunts every subsequent discussion of obedience, fraternization, and what armies are made of. The one day the trenches emptied voluntarily is remembered longer than a hundred battles.', '/images/plates/christmas-truce.svg', '["World War I","Truce","Christmas","Fraternization"]'::jsonb);

The siege lasted from 6 April to 29 May. Behind the triple Theodosian Walls stood perhaps 7,000 defenders, including 2,000 Venetians and Genoese under the Genoese commander Giustiniani. Outside camped an Ottoman host commonly estimated around 80,000 with the largest cannon ever built, Orban''s great bombard, which could hurl a stone ball half a ton in weight against walls that had stopped every previous attacker.

For weeks the walls held, the great chain barred the Golden Horn, and Venetian ships broke through to resupply the city. Then Mehmed slid his ships overland on greased logs into the Horn behind the chain, Giustiniani was wounded and evacuated against orders, and at dawn on 29 May the Janissaries found an unwatched postern gate, the Kerkoporta, reportedly left unlocked, and poured inside. Constantine XI died fighting in the streets. By noon the Sultan was praying in Hagia Sophia.', 'Constantinople had been besieged by Avars, Persians, Arabs, Bulgars, Rus, and Ottomans, and never taken by assault. Its triple walls, sea walls, Greek fire, and the great chain across the Golden Horn made it the most defensible city on earth. But the empire behind the walls had withered. The Fourth Crusade''s sack of 1204 had shattered Byzantine power permanently, the Black Death and civil wars followed, and the Ottomans spent the 1300s absorbing every Byzantine province in Anatolia and the Balkans.

Mehmed inherited a state that already surrounded the city on three sides. In 1452 he built Rumeli Hisari fortress on the Bosporus to strangle Constantinople''s supplies, hired the Hungarian cannon-founder Orban after Constantinople refused his price, and spent the winter assembling the greatest siege train the medieval world had seen.', '["A century of Ottoman expansion had reduced Byzantium to its capital plus fragments. The city was geographically indefensible in the long term once Anatolia and the Balkans were lost.","Mehmed''s personal determination. The young sultan staked his throne''s legitimacy on succeeding where six centuries of predecessors had failed.","Gunpowder artillery, Orban''s bombards, finally neutralized the Theodosian Walls, the one asset that had saved the city in every previous siege.","Western aid never came in force. Venice and Genoa sent ships and men, but the Pope''s crusade, Hungary''s armies, and meaningful relief all failed to materialize.","Byzantine manpower collapse. Some 7,000 defenders held fourteen miles of walls against roughly ten times their number."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1452-08-31","title":"Rumeli Hisari completed","description":"Mehmed''s Bosporus fortress cuts Constantinople''s Black Sea lifeline. The siege effectively begins."},{"date":"1453-04-06","title":"Bombardment opens","description":"Orban''s great guns begin pounding the Theodosian Walls, the first sustained artillery siege of a great walled city."},{"date":"1453-04-20","title":"The convoy breaks through","description":"Three Genoese ships fight past the Ottoman fleet into the Horn, reviving the defenders'' hope."},{"date":"1453-04-22","title":"Ships over land","description":"Mehmed slides some seventy vessels overland into the Golden Horn behind the great chain, outflanking the sea walls."},{"date":"1453-05-29","title":"The final assault","description":"Janissaries breach the Kerkoporta postern at dawn. Constantine XI dies in the streets and the city falls by noon."}]'::jsonb, '["The Byzantine Empire ended after eleven centuries. Its lands and imperial title passed to the Ottomans.","Constantinople became Istanbul, capital of a three-continent empire.","Ottoman control of eastern trade routes accelerated the search for ocean routes west.","Greek scholars fleeing to Italy carried classical manuscripts that fed the Renaissance."]'::jsonb, '["Orban the cannon-founder first offered his services to Constantinople, which could not afford his price, so he built his monster bombards for the besiegers instead.","The great bombard was so huge it reportedly took sixty oxen to move and could fire only seven times a day, and it eventually burst.","Mehmed''s ships crossed the land behind Galata on greased wooden rails in a single night, 22 April, the defenders woke to an Ottoman fleet inside their safe harbour.","The Kerkoporta, the small gate the Janissaries slipped through at dawn, may simply have been left unlocked in the chaos, the mightiest fortress of the age possibly fell to an open door.","Constantine XI''s body was never conclusively identified. Greek legend holds he was turned to marble and sleeps beneath the city, waiting to reclaim it."]'::jsonb, 'Istanbul remains the seat of a great city on two continents. Hagia Sophia, church, mosque, museum, mosque again, is still the architectural argument of empires. Tuesday 29 May is still considered an unlucky day in Greek folk tradition, and Constantine XI the last Roman emperor, canonized by the Greek church.', 'The fall closed the Middle Ages in the traditional telling and opened the Ottoman century. Constantinople became Istanbul, capital of a three-continent empire. Greek scholars fleeing west carried classical manuscripts to Italy, feeding the Renaissance. And the Ottoman control of east-west trade routes pushed Portugal and Spain to seek ocean routes around Africa and across the Atlantic, the age of exploration begins, indirectly, at these walls.', '/images/plates/siege-of-constantinople.svg', '["Byzantine Empire","Ottoman Empire","Siege","1453"]'::jsonb);

Egypt (1798) was a strategic failure redeemed by propaganda. The coup of 18 Brumaire (November 1799) made him First Consul, and a 1802 plebiscite made him Consul for Life. In 1804 he crowned himself Emperor of the French in Notre-Dame, taking the crown from the Pope''s hands. For the next decade he was master of Europe. Austerlitz in 1805 destroyed two empires'' armies in a day, Jena in 1806 erased Prussia, and by 1811 his empire, allies, and satellites stretched from Spain to Poland.

It collapsed with terrifying speed. The Peninsular War bled France from 1808. The 1812 invasion of Russia swallowed an army of some 600,000 men and returned a remnant. Leipzig in 1813, the Battle of the Nations, broke French power in Germany. Abdication, Elba, the Hundred Days, Waterloo, Saint Helena. He died on that Atlantic rock in 1821, aged fifty-one, dictating memoirs that shaped his legend for two centuries.', 'Revolutionary France after 1789 devoured its own governments. Constitutional monarchy, republic, Terror, Directory, each collapsing within a few years. The wars the Revolution started against Europe''s monarchies created both the emergency that justified strongmen and the mass-conscript armies a genius could wield. Napoleon understood, as his rivals did not, that the Revolution''s energies, careers open to talent, nationalism, legal equality, could be harnessed to personal power rather than merely feared.', '["Revolutionary meritocracy. Without 1789 a Corsican of minor nobility could never have commanded an army at twenty-six.","A decade of coalition warfare that never quite ended gave him an arena where battlefield victory translated directly into political capital.","The Directory''s corruption and bankruptcy created a demand for a strongman, which his Egyptian propaganda and timely bribes positioned him to fill.","Britain''s implacable hostility, funding every coalition, meant peace never lasted, and permanent war steadily concentrated power in the general who kept winning."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1793-12-19","title":"Toulon falls","description":"Captain Bonaparte''s artillery plan retakes the port. He is promoted to brigadier general at twenty-four."},{"date":"1796-04-12","title":"Italian campaign opens","description":"Montenotte begins a fourteen-month campaign of manoeuvre that destroys two Austrian armies and makes Napoleon famous."},{"date":"1799-11-09","title":"Coup of 18 Brumaire","description":"Napoleon overthrows the Directory and becomes First Consul, military dictatorship behind a republican façade."},{"date":"1804-12-02","title":"Coronation","description":"Crowned Emperor in Notre-Dame. The Napoleonic Code soon spreads legal equality across his dominions."},{"date":"1805-12-02","title":"Austerlitz","description":"His masterpiece. 68,000 French destroy 85,000 Russians and Austrians, ending the Third Coalition."},{"date":"1812-09-14","title":"Moscow burns","description":"The Grande Armée enters an empty, burning city. The retreat that follows destroys it, perhaps 500,000 men lost."},{"date":"1813-10-16","title":"Leipzig","description":"The Battle of the Nations. 500,000 men clash and French power in Germany collapses."},{"date":"1815-06-18","title":"Waterloo","description":"Final defeat. Abdication follows within days and exile to Saint Helena within weeks."}]'::jsonb, '["Some three to six million dead across twelve years of Napoleonic warfare, military and civilian combined.","The map of Europe redrawn at Vienna along lines Napoleon himself had sketched, centralized states replacing the old mosaic.","Nationalism ignited as a mass ideology, first against French occupation and then everywhere else for a century.","The Napoleonic Code planted legal equality, secular law, and meritocratic administration across the Continent."]'::jsonb, '["He sold Louisiana to the United States in 1803 for $15 million, roughly four cents an acre, to fund the coming war with Britain.","His reported height of 5''2\" is English propaganda confusion. In French feet he was 5''7\", average for the era, and his guardsmen were simply taller.","He once wrote a romance novella, Clisson et Eugénie, in his youth, it was published, from fragments, nearly two centuries later.","The word ''sabotage'' and the tin can both trace to his era. He offered 12,000 francs for a food-preservation method, and canning won the prize.","At Waterloo he fought with painful illness, most historians now believe acute hemorrhoids, that kept him from the saddle for much of the day."]'::jsonb, 'The Napoleonic Code still underlies civil law from Louisiana to Latin America. Metrication, centralized administration, secular education, and the modern general staff all carry his imprint. He remains the benchmark against which every subsequent would-be hegemon, and every coalition formed to stop one, is measured.', 'Napoleon both spread and betrayed the Revolution. His Code carried legal equality, religious toleration, and meritocracy across Europe, while his empire crushed the republics it claimed to liberate. Nationalism, the force that would remake Europe, was largely his unintended creation, forged in resistance to French occupation from Spain to Prussia.', '/images/plates/napoleon-bonaparte.svg', '["Napoleonic Wars","France","Empire","Leadership"]'::jsonb);

Roman builders learned the recipe empirically. Mixed with seawater, the ash-lime mortar triggers a rare crystallization, aluminum tobermorite grows through the concrete, binding it ever tighter. Cracks that would doom a modern pier instead fill with new mineral growth. The concrete heals itself. Vitruvius described the method around 30 BC. Engineers from the Pantheon (c. AD 125) to Caesarea Maritima''s harbour used it at civilization scale.

Modern science only caught up recently. Microscopic studies in the 2010s identified the tobermorite mechanism, and a 2023 MIT study found Roman masons ''hot-mixed'' quicklime directly, leaving reactive lime clasts that dissolve into cracks and re-cement them. The Romans, without chemistry, had engineered self-healing infrastructure two millennia before the term existed.', 'Rome''s expansion demanded infrastructure at unprecedented scale. Aqueducts across valleys, harbours where no natural ones existed, domes and vaults that spanned spaces Greek post-and-lintel architecture never attempted. Ordinary lime mortar could not set underwater and weakened in damp, useless for the maritime empire Augustus was building.

The answer lay literally underfoot around Vesuvius. Greek colonists had used volcanic ash mortars for centuries. Roman engineers systematized the recipe, standardized it across the empire, and deployed it in projects, Trajan''s Markets, the Pantheon, port works from Italy to Israel, that still stand.', '["Imperial scale. Harbours, aqueducts, and domes required a binder stronger and more versatile than traditional lime mortar.","Geology. The Bay of Naples'' pozzolana deposits sat beside the capital of a maritime empire, material and demand in one place.","Roman engineering culture, which tested, codified (Vitruvius), and replicated techniques across provinces instead of guarding them as craft secrets.","The empire''s fall. When maintenance regimes and ash supply chains collapsed, the recipe survived only in texts nobody could operationalize."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"-0030-01-01","title":"Vitruvius codifies it","description":"De architectura describes ash-lime mortar for harbours and damp works."},{"date":"0125-01-01","title":"The Pantheon","description":"The unreinforced concrete dome, still the world''s largest, is poured."},{"date":"2017-07-03","title":"Tobermorite identified","description":"Microscopy reveals the self-healing mineral mechanism in ancient samples."},{"date":"2023-01-05","title":"Hot mixing discovered","description":"MIT finds quicklime was mixed hot, leaving self-healing lime clasts."}]'::jsonb, '["Monumental Rome became possible. Domes, vaults, harbours, and aqueducts at unprecedented scale.","The recipe''s loss with the empire left a 1,500-year gap filled by inferior mortars.","Modern rediscovery points toward lower-carbon, self-healing concrete for seawalls and infrastructure.","A standing lesson that technologies survive only inside civilizations that maintain them."]'::jsonb, '["The Pantheon''s dome gets lighter toward the top. Aggregate grades from heavy basalt at the base to featherweight pumice near the oculus.","Caesarea Maritima''s harbour was built by sinking wooden forms and pouring concrete directly into seawater, it set underwater.","Vitruvius specified ash from specific Bay of Naples beds, the Romans had effectively graded their pozzolana by source.","Modern concrete''s steel rebar is its Achilles heel. Roman concrete used none, which is precisely why the sea cannot rust it apart.","The 2023 hot-mixing finding came from examining lime clasts long dismissed as sloppy workmanship, they turned out to be the mechanism."]'::jsonb, 'Every modern seawall project studying Roman samples is an attempt to recover what was lost. If self-healing concrete scales, the Romans will have solved, two millennia early, infrastructure''s hardest problem. Building things that outlast their builders.', 'Roman concrete made the monumental empire possible, and its loss is a parable of fragile knowledge. A superior technology vanished for 1,500 years because the civilization maintaining it fell. Its rediscovery now points toward greener, longer-lived modern concrete, since Roman mixes need far less carbon-intensive cement.', '/images/plates/roman-concrete-mystery.svg', '["Ancient Rome","Engineering","Materials","Pantheon"]'::jsonb);

What followed was four years of industrial stalemate. Machine guns, barbed wire, and artillery made attack suicidal and defence impregnable. Verdun bled both armies white through most of 1916. The Somme cost Britain 57,000 casualties on its first day. New weapons arrived faster than tactics could absorb them, poison gas in 1915, tanks in 1916, strategic bombing and submarines throughout, each horrible, none decisive.

The deadlock broke in 1918. Germany''s last gamble, the spring offensives, outran its supplies, while fresh American troops, British tanks, and a Royal Navy blockade that was starving German cities tilted the balance irreversibly. Bulgaria, the Ottoman Empire, and Austria-Hungary collapsed in quick succession. Sailors mutinied at Kiel, the Kaiser fled to Holland, and Germany signed the armistice at Compiègne on 11 November 1918.', 'Europe in 1914 was a continent of rival empires armed to the teeth and bound by interlocking promises. Germany, unified only in 1871, had overtaken Britain industrially and demanded a colonial empire and a battlefleet to match. France nursed the loss of Alsace-Lorraine from 1871. Austria-Hungary, a mosaic of eleven nationalities, was decaying from within while Russia posed as protector of the Slavs in the Balkans.

Two alliance blocs faced each other. The Triple Entente of France, Russia, and Britain against the Triple Alliance of Germany, Austria-Hungary, and Italy. Each general staff had exactly one war plan, and each plan began with mobilization timetables measured in hours. The Balkans, where Serbian nationalism threatened Austria-Hungary and Ottoman retreat had left a vacuum, provided the spark both alliance systems were primed to catch.', '["Entangling alliances turned a Balkan assassination into a continental war. Russia mobilized for Serbia, Germany for Austria, France for Russia, each step automatic once started.","Militarism and the naval arms race, especially Germany''s challenge to British sea power, had normalized the expectation that a great war was coming and soon.","Nationalism inside multi-ethnic empires. Serbian, Czech, Polish, and Arab movements each threatened Austria-Hungary, Russia, or the Ottomans, making the Balkans a permanent tinderbox.","The rigid cult of the offensive. Every general staff believed the next war would be short and decided by attack, so all plans demanded instant mobilization with no room for diplomacy.","Germany''s blank cheque to Austria-Hungary in July 1914, a promise of unconditional support, emboldened Vienna to issue Serbia an ultimatum designed to be refused."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1914-08-04","title":"Germany invades Belgium","description":"The Schlieffen Plan rolls through neutral Belgium. Britain declares war the same day and the BEF races to France."},{"date":"1914-09-06","title":"Miracle of the Marne","description":"French and British armies halt the German advance twenty miles from Paris. The war of movement ends and the trenches begin."},{"date":"1915-04-22","title":"Gas at Ypres","description":"Germany releases chlorine gas, the first large-scale chemical attack, opening a new dimension of industrial horror."},{"date":"1916-02-21","title":"Verdun","description":"Germany attacks the French fortress ring on a narrow front, declaring the goal is to bleed France white. Ten months cost both sides some 700,000 casualties."},{"date":"1916-07-01","title":"The Somme","description":"Britain suffers 57,000 casualties on the first day, still the bloodiest day in its army''s history, and tanks debut in September."},{"date":"1917-04-06","title":"America enters","description":"Unrestricted submarine warfare and the Zimmermann Telegram bring the United States into the war, promising endless fresh manpower."},{"date":"1918-03-21","title":"Spring offensives","description":"Freed by Russia''s collapse, Germany throws everything westward, advances forty miles, and outruns its artillery, food, and reserves."},{"date":"1918-11-11","title":"Armistice","description":"With allies collapsing, sailors mutinying, and revolution spreading, Germany signs at Compiègne. The guns stop at the eleventh hour."}]'::jsonb, '["Four empires fell. Germany became a republic, Austria-Hungary dissolved into new states, the Tsar was overthrown and murdered, and the Ottoman Empire was partitioned.","The Treaty of Versailles imposed war guilt, disarmament, and reparations on Germany, a settlement whose humiliation extremists would exploit within fifteen years.","The map of Eastern Europe and the Middle East was redrawn. Poland, Czechoslovakia, and Yugoslavia appeared, while Britain and France took Ottoman Arab lands under League mandates.","More than fifteen million dead and some twenty million wounded, plus a flu pandemic spread by troop movements that killed many millions more.","Women''s suffrage, the League of Nations, and Soviet Russia all emerged directly from the war''s political wreckage."]'::jsonb, '["The Christmas Truce of 1914 was never ordered, thousands of soldiers simply stopped shooting, exchanged gifts, and played football before officers on both sides banned any repeat.","Britain''s first-day losses on the Somme, 57,000 casualties, 19,000 dead, remain the bloodiest single day in British military history.","Carrier pigeons won medals. Cher Ami, shot through the breast at Verdun, still delivered the message that saved 194 trapped American soldiers.","The German army that marched in 1914 moved largely on foot and horseback, only the wealthiest armies of 1918 had trucks, tanks, and aircraft in quantity.","The 1918 influenza pandemic, spread along troop movements, killed several times more people than the war''s battles did."]'::jsonb, 'The League of Nations, women''s suffrage across much of Europe, the Soviet Union, the modern Middle East''s borders, Remembrance Day, and the very concept of a world war all date from 1914–18. Every subsequent conflict has been fought in the shadow of its demonstration that industrial powers can destroy a generation in four years.', 'The war destroyed four empires, German, Austro-Hungarian, Russian, and Ottoman, and with them the old European order. It redrew the map from Poland to Palestine, introduced total war against civilians through blockade and bombing, toppled the Tsar and midwifed both Soviet communism and, through a punitive peace, the conditions for Nazism. The twentieth century''s shape was poured in these trenches.', '/images/plates/world-war-i.svg', '["Global War","Trenches","Empires","Treaty of Versailles"]'::jsonb);

By 1942 the Axis held territory from Norway to New Guinea. The tide turned almost simultaneously at three points on the map. Soviet armies encircled the Germans at Stalingrad, American carriers sank four Japanese carriers at Midway, and British forces stopped Rommel at El Alamein. From 1943 the Allies advanced on every front while strategic bombing reduced German and Japanese cities to rubble.

The war ended in 1945 with Germany crushed between converging armies and Japan surrendering after the atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki. More than sixty million people had died, the majority of them civilians. Europe lay in ruins, and two superpowers, the United States and the Soviet Union, faced each other across a divided continent.', 'The war grew directly out of the settlement of 1918. The Treaty of Versailles stripped Germany of territory, colonies, and merchant shipping, imposed disarmament, assigned sole war guilt, and demanded reparations on a scale that poisoned German politics for a generation. The Austro-Hungarian and Ottoman empires were carved into new states whose borders satisfied nobody.

The Great Depression that began in 1929 then discredited parliamentary governments across the world. In Germany it destroyed the middle-class savings that anchored the Weimar Republic and drove voters toward extremes. In Japan it empowered officers who argued that only a conquered empire in Asia could guarantee rice, oil, and markets. Italy, humiliated by its meagre gains in 1918, invaded Ethiopia in 1935 while the League of Nations proved powerless to stop it. By the late 1930s three revisionist powers, Germany, Italy, and Japan, had each concluded that the existing order could only be changed by force.', '["The Versailles settlement left Germany humiliated, disarmed, and burdened with reparations, creating a permanent constituency for any leader who promised to overturn it.","The Great Depression radicalized politics worldwide, collapsing trade, toppling moderate governments, and making extremist promises of jobs and national revival irresistible.","Expansionist ideologies in all three Axis powers, Lebensraum in the east for Germany, a new Roman empire for Italy, and the Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere for Japan, each required conquest by definition.","The League of Nations failed every test of the 1930s. Manchuria in 1931, Ethiopia in 1935, and the remilitarization of the Rhineland in 1936 all went unpunished, teaching aggressors that aggression worked.","The Nazi-Soviet pact of August 1939 removed Germany''s fear of a two-front war and gave Hitler a free hand to invade Poland, which triggered the British and French declarations of war."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1939-09-01","title":"Invasion of Poland","description":"German armies and aircraft crush Poland in five weeks while the Soviet Union seizes the east under the secret protocol of the Nazi-Soviet pact."},{"date":"1940-05-10","title":"Fall of France","description":"German armour punches through the Ardennes, outflanks the Maginot Line, and forces France to surrender within six weeks. Britain evacuates 338,000 men from Dunkirk."},{"date":"1941-06-22","title":"Operation Barbarossa","description":"Three million Axis troops invade the Soviet Union along a 2,900-kilometre front, the largest invasion in history, and reach the gates of Moscow by December."},{"date":"1941-12-07","title":"Pearl Harbor","description":"Japanese carrier aircraft sink or damage eight American battleships in Hawaii, bringing the United States into the war the following day."},{"date":"1942-08-23","title":"Stalingrad begins","description":"Five months of street-by-street fighting end with an entire German army encircled and surrendering, the psychological turning point of the European war."},{"date":"1944-06-06","title":"D-Day","description":"156,000 Allied troops land in Normandy in the largest seaborne invasion ever attempted, opening the long-awaited second front in the west."},{"date":"1945-05-08","title":"Fall of Berlin","description":"Soviet troops fight street by street into the German capital. Hitler kills himself on 30 April and Germany surrenders unconditionally a week later."},{"date":"1945-08-06","title":"Hiroshima and Nagasaki","description":"Single atomic bombs destroy two Japanese cities. The Soviet Union invades Manchuria the same week, and Japan surrenders on 15 August."}]'::jsonb, '["More than sixty million dead, the majority civilians, the Soviet Union alone lost over twenty million people, and China lost millions more.","Germany was divided into occupation zones that hardened into East and West Germany, and Europe split along the Iron Curtain for nearly half a century.","The United Nations, the Nuremberg trials, and the Universal Declaration of Human Rights created the first enforceable framework of international law.","Britain and France emerged bankrupt and soon lost their Asian and African empires. India, Indonesia, and dozens of new states followed within two decades.","The atomic bombings opened the nuclear age, and within four years both superpowers held the bomb, the foundation of Cold War deterrence."]'::jsonb, '["Denmark surrendered to Germany within hours of the invasion on 9 April 1940, after the government concluded resistance would only destroy Copenhagen for nothing.","The Soviet Union physically relocated more than 1,500 factories east of the Urals in 1941, reassembling entire industries in Siberian snow while the front collapsed.","The Navajo code talkers'' radio code, based on their unwritten language, was never broken by Japan during the entire Pacific war.","A Syrian brown bear named Wojtek, adopted by Polish artillerymen, carried ammunition crates at Monte Cassino and was later promoted to corporal.","British deception planners dressed a corpse as a drowned officer carrying fake invasion plans, Operation Mincemeat, and convinced Germany the Sicily landings would fall elsewhere."]'::jsonb, 'The Cold War, the welfare state, European integration beginning with the Coal and Steel Community of 1951, the Geneva Conventions, decolonization, and nuclear deterrence all trace back to this war. Its moral vocabulary, genocide, crimes against humanity, never again, still frames how the world judges atrocity.', 'The war redrew the map and the rules at once. It created the United Nations, outlawed aggressive war at Nuremberg, split Europe into rival blocs for half a century, accelerated the end of European colonial empires, and opened the nuclear age, every major institution of the post-1945 world is a direct answer to something that happened between 1939 and 1945.', '/images/plates/world-war-ii.svg', '["Global War","Europe","Pacific","Eastern Front"]'::jsonb);

The Americans owned the daylight. Helicopters, B-52s, napalm, defoliants. The communists owned the night. Tunnels at Cu Chi, the Ho Chi Minh Trail through Laos and Cambodia, political cadres in every village. The Tet Offensive of January 1968 was a military defeat for Hanoi, some 45,000 communist dead, and a political catastrophe for Washington. Broadcast into living rooms, it destroyed the official story that victory was near.

Nixon''s Vietnamization withdrew US ground troops while bombing expanded into Cambodia and Laos. The 1973 Paris Accords left American prisoners home and the North''s armies in the South. In April 1975 they took Saigon, and the last helicopters lifted off the embassy roof. Some 58,000 Americans and perhaps two million Vietnamese had died. Vietnam was unified under Hanoi, and within four years was at war with Cambodia and China.', 'Vietnam had resisted China for a thousand years and France for nearly a century when Ho Chi Minh, a Comintern agent turned nationalist icon, declared independence in Hanoi in September 1945, quoting the American Declaration. Washington, needing France in NATO, backed the French return instead. Dien Bien Phu in 1954 ended that, and Geneva split Vietnam at the 17th parallel pending elections that were never held.

Ngo Dinh Diem''s southern regime, Catholic and nepotist in a Buddhist majority, alienated the countryside while American doctrine, honed against Germany and Korea, assumed firepower could substitute for legitimacy. It could not, and every escalation deepened the trap.', '["Cold War domino theory. Eisenhower, Kennedy, and Johnson each believed losing Vietnam would topple all of Southeast Asia.","The South''s illegitimacy. Diem''s persecution of Buddhists and landlords'' corruption made the Viet Cong''s parallel government the credible authority in much of the countryside.","Mission creep without strategy. Advisers became combat battalions became half a million troops, each step justified as the last one needed.","North Vietnamese endurance. Hanoi accepted any casualty level, correctly judging that American patience, televised nightly, would break first."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1964-08-07","title":"Gulf of Tonkin Resolution","description":"A disputed second attack gives Johnson a blank cheque for war."},{"date":"1965-03-08","title":"Marines land at Da Nang","description":"The first US ground combat troops come ashore. Escalation becomes open-ended."},{"date":"1968-01-30","title":"Tet Offensive","description":"Coordinated attacks nationwide shatter Washington''s victory narrative."},{"date":"1968-03-16","title":"My Lai","description":"US troops massacre hundreds of civilians, exposed a year later, it poisons the war at home."},{"date":"1973-01-27","title":"Paris Accords","description":"America exits. The North''s armies stay in the South."},{"date":"1975-04-30","title":"Fall of Saigon","description":"Tanks crash the presidential palace gates. The war ends."}]'::jsonb, '["Vietnam unified under Hanoi at a cost of perhaps two million Vietnamese and 58,000 American dead.","American interventionism froze for a generation, the Vietnam syndrome, until the Gulf War''s managed optics.","The region did not fall like dominoes. Unified Vietnam soon fought Cambodia''s Khmer Rouge and then China itself.","The war''s fiscal cost helped end Bretton Woods and fueled the inflation and distrust that defined 1970s America."]'::jsonb, '["The US dropped more bomb tonnage on Vietnam, Laos, and Cambodia than all sides dropped in all of World War II.","The Ho Chi Minh Trail was not a trail but a 16,000-kilometre logistics network of roads, pipelines, and waystations, bombed daily, never closed.","Operation Ranch Hand sprayed some 20 million gallons of herbicides including Agent Orange. The health and ecological effects persist today.","South Vietnam held the world''s fourth-largest air force at the end, and abandoned most of it on the runways in 1975.","The last Americans left by helicopter from the embassy roof on 30 April 1975. The famous photograph shows the evacuation of the CIA station chief''s deputy and local staff."]'::jsonb, 'The Powell Doctrine, the all-volunteer force, war-powers debates, embedded journalism rules, and the memorial wall''s 58,000 names all descend from Vietnam. Hanoi''s victory remains the textbook case of strategy defeating firepower. The side with the clearer theory of victory wins, regardless of tonnage dropped.', 'Vietnam proved that firepower cannot buy legitimacy and that televised war cannot be indefinitely sustained by democracies, the ''Vietnam syndrome'' constrained American intervention for a generation. It unified Vietnam at staggering cost, discredited the domino theory''s premises even as the dominoes around it stayed upright, and made credibility itself, the gap between official claims and visible reality, a permanent American political concept.', '/images/plates/vietnam-war.svg', '["Cold War","Guerrilla War","United States","Vietnam"]'::jsonb);

Both sides expected a short war and got the first industrial one. Railroads, rifles, trenches, and telegraphs produced casualty lists that stunned a nation of 31 million. Lee''s audacity carried the South through 1862, Second Bull Run, Fredericksburg, Chancellorsville, but the arithmetic was merciless. The North had twice the population, nine-tenths of the industry, and, after 1863, 180,000 Black soldiers fighting for their own freedom.

Gettysburg and Vicksburg in July 1863 broke the Confederacy''s strategic position. Grant''s 1864 Overland campaign broke its armies by grim attrition. Sherman''s march through Georgia broke its will and its railways. Lee surrendered at Appomattox on 9 April 1865 with famous generosity, officers kept sidearms, men kept horses, all went home fed. Five days later Lincoln was murdered. Some 620,000 soldiers were dead by the traditional count, and four million enslaved people were free.', 'By 1860 the United States was two economies sharing one flag. An industrializing North of free labour and a South whose wealth rested entirely on enslaved Black labour producing cotton for world markets. Every decade''s compromise, Missouri in 1820, the Compromise of 1850, Kansas-Nebraska in 1854, bought time at rising interest, while Bleeding Kansas and John Brown''s raid showed the interest was coming due.

The Republican Party existed for one purpose. Stopping slavery''s spread. Southern leaders read Lincoln''s election as a death sentence for their system, not immediate abolition, which Lincoln disavowed, but strangulation by containment. South Carolina seceded in December 1860. Six states followed before Lincoln''s inauguration.', '["Slavery''s expansion. Every new western territory forced the question of whether it would be slave or free, and the South would not accept being fenced in.","The election of 1860 gave the presidency to an explicitly anti-expansion party, the trigger, not the underlying cause, of secession.","States'' rights as Southern doctrine. The constitutional theory that made secession seem legal, deployed in defence of slavery at every turn.","Economic divergence. A tariff, banking, and internal-improvements agenda that served Northern industry while the South saw only extraction.","The failure of compromise politics. By 1860 the centre had voted itself out of existence, Bell and Douglas split the middle while Breckinridge and Lincoln embodied the poles."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1861-04-12","title":"Fort Sumter","description":"Confederate guns open fire. Lincoln calls for volunteers and four more states secede."},{"date":"1862-09-17","title":"Antietam","description":"The bloodiest single day in American history, some 23,000 casualties, gives Lincoln the victory he needs to issue Emancipation."},{"date":"1863-01-01","title":"Emancipation Proclamation","description":"Slavery declared dead in rebel territory. The war becomes officially a war for freedom."},{"date":"1863-07-04","title":"Gettysburg and Vicksburg","description":"Lee''s invasion fails the day Grant takes the Mississippi, the Confederacy is split and turned back at once."},{"date":"1864-11-15","title":"March to the Sea","description":"Sherman cuts loose from supply lines and guts Georgia''s war economy on the way to Savannah."},{"date":"1865-04-09","title":"Appomattox","description":"Lee surrenders to Grant, who offers terms generous enough to let the armies go home."}]'::jsonb, '["Four million enslaved people freed, and slavery abolished constitutionally by the 13th Amendment within the year.","Some 620,000 soldiers dead and the Southern economy wrecked, a devastation from which parts of the South took a century to recover.","Federal supremacy established. Secession settled as illegal, and Washington, not state capitals, became the centre of American power.","Reconstruction enfranchised Black men, then collapsed into Jim Crow, freedom''s promise deferred for another century."]'::jsonb, '["More Americans died in the Civil War than in all other American wars combined, roughly 2 percent of the entire population.","The first land battle, Bull Run, was watched by Washington picnickers who brought opera glasses. Several were captured when the Union army routed.","Both sides'' soldiers were overwhelmingly farm boys who had never travelled. Disease killed roughly twice as many as bullets did.","The war''s photography, Brady, Gardner, was the first conflict documented as it happened, and the images of the dead at Antietam shocked the Northern public.","Lee''s surrender terms at Appomattox were so generous that Grant''s own officers protested, Grant insisted the war end with handshakes, not humiliation."]'::jsonb, 'Reconstruction''s promise was betrayed within a decade, and Jim Crow reimposed racial subjugation by other means, the war freed four million people and left their grandchildren to finish the job in the 1960s. The battlefield parks, the Lost Cause mythology, and the still-unfinished argument over Confederate monuments show a war whose meaning America has never stopped contesting.', 'The war settled three questions at once. The Union is perpetual, slavery is dead, and the federal government, not the states, defines American citizenship. The 13th, 14th, and 15th Amendments rewrote the Constitution, and the war''s industrial scale previewed the total wars of the next century. Every subsequent American argument about federal power descends from Appomattox.', '/images/plates/american-civil-war.svg', '["United States","Slavery","Emancipation","1861-1865"]'::jsonb);

The pattern repeated with variations. Britain, untouchable at sea after Trafalgar (1805), funded coalition after coalition while Napoleon destroyed their armies on land. Austria and Russia at Austerlitz (1805), Prussia at Jena-Auerstedt (1806), Russia again at Friedland (1807), followed by the Tilsit embrace that made the Tsar an ally. The Continental System tried to strangle British trade. Instead it strangled European commerce and drove Napoleon into the two commitments that killed him.

Spain became the ''Spanish ulcer''. From 1808 British gold, Spanish guerrillas, and Wellington''s army bled 300,000 French troops in a war Napoleon never attended. Russia in 1812 swallowed the Grande Armée whole. The Sixth Coalition''s victory at Leipzig (1813), the largest battle in European history to that point, drove Napoleon to Elba. The Seventh finished him at Waterloo. Several million soldiers and civilians died across a dozen years.', 'The French Revolutionary Wars (1792–1802) had already shown that mass conscription plus ideological fervour could overwhelm professional armies. The Peace of Amiens in 1802 paused the fighting without settling anything. Britain kept Malta, France kept its satellites, and both sides rearmed while accusing the other of bad faith.

Napoleon''s coronation as Emperor in 1804 and his annexations in Italy convinced London, Vienna, and St Petersburg that no treaty would contain him. Pitt the Younger spent Britain''s gold freely to assemble the Third Coalition in 1805, and the machine of coalition warfare, which would grind for a decade, started turning.', '["Irreconcilable aims. Britain would not accept French domination of the Low Countries and coastlines. Napoleon would not accept a Europe he did not dominate.","The Amiens settlement''s hollowness, a truce both sides treated as an armistice, with Malta, trade, and press propaganda as open sores.","British subsidies that made coalition warfare affordable for Austria, Prussia, and Russia again and again, however often their armies were destroyed.","Napoleon''s own expansionism. Each victory demanded new annexations (Hanover, Naples, Holland, Spain) that created new enemies faster than old ones submitted."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1805-10-21","title":"Trafalgar","description":"Nelson destroys the Franco-Spanish fleet at the cost of his life. Britain rules the waves unchallenged for a century."},{"date":"1805-12-02","title":"Austerlitz","description":"Napoleon''s masterpiece destroys the Third Coalition''s armies in a day."},{"date":"1806-10-14","title":"Jena-Auerstedt","description":"Prussia''s fabled army collapses in a single day. French troops enter Berlin."},{"date":"1808-05-02","title":"Peninsular War begins","description":"Spain rises against French occupation. The ulcer bleeds France for six years."},{"date":"1812-09-14","title":"Moscow","description":"The Grande Armée reaches its goal and finds ashes. The retreat destroys it."},{"date":"1813-10-16","title":"Leipzig","description":"The Battle of the Nations. Half a million men, French defeat, German liberation."},{"date":"1815-06-18","title":"Waterloo","description":"The Seventh Coalition ends it in an afternoon."}]'::jsonb, '["Several million dead across twelve years, military and civilian, Europe''s costliest wars until 1914.","The Congress of Vienna redrew the map and created a century-long framework against both revolution and hegemony.","German and Italian nationalism, forged resisting France, would unify both countries within sixty years.","Britain''s naval, colonial, and industrial supremacy was secured for the Victorian century."]'::jsonb, '["Britain''s national debt reached roughly 200 percent of GDP funding these wars, the fiscal equivalent of fighting on a credit card for a decade.","The word ''guerrilla'' entered military vocabulary from Spanish irregulars. Wellington called the Peninsula the war that taught Britain to fight Napoleon.","Trafalgar''s signal, ''England expects that every man will do his duty'', was hoisted in flag code because Nelson had no prearranged signal for it.","Napoleon''s army at its peak drew soldiers from a dozen nationalities, including Poles, Italians, Germans, Croats, and even a Mameluke squadron.","The Seventh Coalition''s Waterloo campaign lasted barely a week of major fighting, after twelve years of war, Europe''s fate turned in days."]'::jsonb, 'Guerrilla (literally ''little war,'' from Spain), the general staff system (perfected by Prussia in response), corps organization, and the concept of the nation in arms all descend from these wars. Clausewitz wrote On War to explain what he had lived through, and every strategist since has argued with him.', 'The wars ended the old European order and built the modern one. The Congress of Vienna''s map, the German and Italian nationalisms forged in resistance to France, the Napoleonic Code planted across the Continent, and Britain''s century of global supremacy. Total war, conscription, economic blockade, guerrilla resistance, national mobilization, was invented here and never uninvented.', '/images/plates/napoleonic-wars.svg', '["France","Europe","Coalitions","1803-1815"]'::jsonb);

He nearly succeeded. At Ligny on 16 June he mauled the Prussians, and at Quatre Bras Ney bloodied Wellington. But the Prussians retreated in good order toward Wavre instead of away from their allies, and Grouchy''s 33,000 men, detached to pursue them, marched on empty roads while Blücher''s columns converged on Waterloo. On 18 June, after rain delayed the French guns until midday, Napoleon hurled 72,000 men at Wellington''s ridge for nine hours.

The ridge held. Hougoumont never fell. The great cavalry charges broke against unbroken squares. When some 30,000 Prussians emerged from the woods to the east and fell on the French flank at Plancenoit, the Imperial Guard''s final assault failed and the French army dissolved into rout. Napoleon fled to Paris, abdicated four days later, and finished his days on Saint Helena.', 'The Congress of Vienna had spent nine months redesigning Europe after Napoleon''s first abdication in 1814, restoring Bourbons in France, enlarging Prussia, and surrounding France with buffer states. The settlement was barely dry when Napoleon slipped away from Elba with a thousand guardsmen. Marshal Ney, sent to arrest him, defected with his whole command. Louis XVIII fled Paris, and within three weeks Napoleon ruled France again in what became known as the Hundred Days.

Britain, Prussia, Austria, and Russia each pledged 150,000 men to finish him. Wellington and Blücher, camped in Belgium with 112,000 men between them, were the only armies in place, Napoleon moved to destroy them in detail before the eastern armies could march.', '["Napoleon''s return from Elba made a new war unavoidable. The Seventh Coalition declared him outside the law of nations, leaving him no negotiated outcome except victory.","Strategic necessity forced his hand, facing eventual odds of nearly a million coalition troops, his only chance was to defeat the nearest armies separately and then negotiate from strength.","The Prussian army''s survival after Ligny. Gneisenau''s decision to retreat parallel to Wellington rather than away from him preserved the concentration that won Waterloo.","Grouchy''s detachment of 33,000 men chased shadows toward Wavre while the battle was decided elsewhere, arguably the most expensive marching orders in military history."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1815-06-16","title":"Ligny and Quatre Bras","description":"Napoleon beats Blücher at Ligny while Ney fights Wellington to a standstill at Quatre Bras, but neither French victory destroys its target."},{"date":"1815-06-17","title":"Rain and retreat","description":"Torrential rain turns the battlefield to mud, delaying everything. Wellington withdraws to the Mont-Saint-Jean ridge and Grouchy is sent after the Prussians."},{"date":"1815-06-18","title":"Hougoumont holds","description":"A diversionary attack on the Hougoumont farm draws in French reserves all day. The farmhouse, defended by British Guards, never falls."},{"date":"1815-06-18","title":"The cavalry charges","description":"Ney hurls some 9,000 horsemen at unbroken infantry squares without artillery support, magnificent, and militarily useless."},{"date":"1815-06-18","title":"Plancenoit and the Guard","description":"Prussians storm Plancenoit on the French flank while the Imperial Guard''s final assault on the ridge breaks. The French army routs by nightfall."}]'::jsonb, '["Napoleon abdicated on 22 June and surrendered to the British. Exiled to Saint Helena, he died there in 1821.","The Bourbon monarchy was restored in France under Louis XVIII, and France''s borders were rolled back essentially to 1790.","Britain emerged as the undisputed global naval and colonial power for the next century.","The Congress of Vienna settlement, validated by victory, gave Europe its longest stretch of general peace in modern history."]'::jsonb, '["The battlefield''s famous Lion''s Mound, built by the Dutch king in the 1820s, used earth that flattened parts of the actual ridge, altering the ground historians argue over.","Lord Uxbridge, his leg shattered by canister shot beside Wellington, reportedly said ''By God, sir, I''ve lost my leg!'', to which Wellington replied, ''By God, sir, so you have!''","Some 30,000 Prussians arrived through muddy country lanes guided by a local who knew the shortcuts, staff work as decisive as any charge.","Napoleon''s army at Waterloo included thousands of veterans, but also raw conscripts. Wellington called his own polyglot force ''an infamous army'' and won with it anyway.","The battle lasted roughly nine hours, from the first cannon at about 11:35 to the French collapse near 21:00."]'::jsonb, 'The Vienna settlement Waterloo secured governed Europe until 1848 and in outline until 1914. Waterloo station, countless streets, and ABBA aside, the battle remains the staff-college archetype of coalition warfare. Wellington could not have won without Blücher, and both knew it.', 'Waterloo ended twenty-three years of French Revolutionary and Napoleonic warfare in a single afternoon. It restored Bourbon rule, confirmed Britain''s century of naval and colonial supremacy, and gave Europe, through the Congress system it validated, nearly forty years without a general war. The battle''s name entered every European language as shorthand for a decisive, career-ending defeat.', '/images/plates/waterloo.svg', '["Napoleonic Wars","Belgium","Final Battle"]'::jsonb);

For three months the battle devoured divisions block by block, Mamayev Kurgan changed hands repeatedly, Pavlov''s House held out for 58 days, the grain elevator became a fortress. Then, on 19 November, the Soviets unleashed Operation Uranus against the weak Romanian and Italian flanks and closed a ring around 300,000 Axis troops. Göring''s promised airlift delivered a fraction of the daily tonnage. The pocket starved and froze.

Paulus, promoted to field marshal the day before (no German field marshal had ever surrendered), capitulated on 2 February 1943. Some 91,000 Germans marched into captivity, of whom only a few thousand ever saw home again. Total losses on both sides approached two million, the deadliest battle in human history.', 'By 1942 Germany''s blitzkrieg economy was running on borrowed time. Without Caucasus oil the Wehrmacht would grind to a halt. The 1942 summer offensive, Case Blue, aimed south past Kharkov toward Stalingrad on the Volga, both a strategic crossing and a propaganda prize. Stalin''s Order No. 227, ''Not one step back,'' made retreat a capital crime just as the Germans arrived.

Both dictators personalized the city. Hitler diverted forces from the oil objective to take the city named for his enemy. Stalin forbade evacuating civilians, insisting the army would fight harder for an inhabited city. The result was a battle neither side could abandon.', '["Germany''s oil desperation. The 1942 offensive existed to seize the Caucasus fields, and Stalingrad guarded the Volga supply route.","Prestige on both sides turned a secondary objective into the main effort, Hitler and Stalin each made the city a test of will.","Chuikov''s close-quarter tactics neutralized German advantages in artillery, armour, and air power.","Soviet operational reserves. While the Germans bled into the city, Zhukov massed a million men unnoticed on the flanks."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1942-08-23","title":"Bombing of the city","description":"Luftwaffe raids kill tens of thousands in a day and reduce Stalingrad to defender-friendly rubble."},{"date":"1942-09-13","title":"Chuikov takes command","description":"The 62nd Army is ordered to hold at all costs, hugging German lines to smother their firepower."},{"date":"1942-11-19","title":"Operation Uranus","description":"Soviet armies smash the Romanian flanks and encircle 300,000 Axis troops within four days."},{"date":"1942-12-12","title":"Relief fails","description":"Manstein''s rescue drive stalls thirty miles short. The airlift delivers a fraction of what the pocket needs."},{"date":"1943-02-02","title":"Surrender","description":"Paulus capitulates. 91,000 starving soldiers march into captivity."}]'::jsonb, '["An entire German army group destroyed, the Wehrmacht never again held the strategic initiative in the east.","Romania, Hungary, and Italy, whose troops guarded the fatal flanks, began looking for exits from the Axis.","Soviet confidence became unshakeable. From Stalingrad the Red Army advanced, with pauses, all the way to Berlin.","Some 91,000 prisoners taken, of whom only a few thousand survived captivity to return home."]'::jsonb, '["Pavlov''s House, an apartment block held by Sergeant Pavlov''s platoon for 58 days, was marked on German maps as a fortress.","Soviet reinforcements crossed the Volga at night under fire with life expectancies sometimes measured in hours. The average survival time of a newly arrived private was reportedly under a day.","Göring promised 500 tons of daily supply by air. On the best days barely a fifth arrived, and on many days nothing did.","The battle''s total casualties, approaching two million killed, wounded, and captured, exceed the population of many modern capitals.","Paulus survived Soviet captivity, testified at Nuremberg, and lived in East Germany until 1957, the field marshal who surrendered became a Cold War symbol."]'::jsonb, 'Volgograd''s Mamayev Kurgan memorial, dominated by the 85-metre Motherland Calls statue, remains Russia''s central shrine of the war. ''Stalingrad'' entered every language as shorthand for ruinous urban battle, invoked at Hue, Grozny, Mosul, and Mariupol.', 'Stalingrad ended Germany''s ability to win the war in the east, and therefore to win the war. It destroyed an entire army group, shattered the myth of Wehrmacht invincibility, and gave the Soviet Union unshakeable confidence that it would finish the war in Berlin. Historians rank it with Midway and El Alamein as one of the three turning points of 1942–43.', '/images/plates/stalingrad.svg', '["World War II","Eastern Front","Siege"]'::jsonb);

King Leonidas of Sparta arrived first with 300 picked Spartans and perhaps 7,000 other Greeks, the main Spartan army delayed, piously, by a religious festival. For two days the pass held. Persian assaults shattered, Xerxes reportedly leaping from his throne in disbelief. On the third day a local, Ephialtes, showed the Persians a mountain path around the position.

Leonidas dismissed the allies and stayed with his 300 Spartans, 700 Thespians who refused to leave, and others, about a thousand men against an empire. They were surrounded and killed to a man. The delay bought Athens time to evacuate, and a month later the Persian fleet was destroyed at Salamis. Thermopylae lost the battle and saved the campaign.', 'Persia had tried Greece once before. Darius''s expedition ended at Marathon in 490 BC, where outnumbered Athenians ran down the Persian line and won. Xerxes spent four years preparing the answer, canals dug through headlands, depots stocked along the route, and an army drawn from across an empire spanning three continents.

Greece was thirty-odd quarrelling city-states. Sparta, the land power, distrusted Athens, the sea power. Many cities medized, submitted, rather than fight. The alliance that met Persia was fragile, outnumbered, and buying time for a naval strategy most Spartans barely understood.', '["Persian imperial logic. No great king could leave a defeat like Marathon unavenged, and Greece''s submission would secure the empire''s western frontier.","Greek geography. The pass at Thermopylae and the straits at Artemisium were the only points where a small force could cancel Persian numbers.","Spartan religious calendar. The Carneian festival delayed the main army, leaving Leonidas''s advance guard to hold alone, accident as much as heroism.","Athenian naval strategy. Themistocles needed the army to buy days while the fleet prepared. Thermopylae was always a delaying action, not a bid for victory."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"-0480-08-01","title":"The pass holds","description":"Two days of repulsed Persian assaults, including the elite Immortals."},{"date":"-0480-08-02","title":"Ephialtes betrays the path","description":"A local reveals the Anopaia track around the Greek position."},{"date":"-0480-08-03","title":"The last stand","description":"Surrounded, Leonidas, his Spartans, and the Thespians fight to the death."},{"date":"-0480-09-28","title":"Salamis","description":"The delay pays off. The Persian fleet is destroyed and Xerxes withdraws."}]'::jsonb, '["Athens was evacuated in time. A month later the Persian fleet died at Salamis and Xerxes withdrew with most of his army.","The Persian invasion''s land force remained and was destroyed the next year at Plataea, Greece was free.","Sparta''s prestige soared, setting up its fatal rivalry with Athens and the Peloponnesian War a half-century later.","The last-stand archetype entered Western culture permanently, from the Alamo to modern cinema."]'::jsonb, '["The 300 were not alone. Some 7,000 other Greeks held the pass for the first two days, and 700 Thespians chose to stay and die with the Spartans.","Herodotus''s Persian millions are ancient exaggeration, but even conservative modern estimates leave the Greeks outnumbered dozens to one.","Xerxes reportedly had the sea whipped and shackled after a storm destroyed his first Hellespont bridges, punishment as engineering management.","The pass looks nothing like the legend today. Centuries of silt have moved the sea miles away, leaving a highway-side monument in a plain.","Sparta''s vaunted 300 were all men with living sons, Leonidas selected fathers, so no household would be extinguished."]'::jsonb, 'Leonidas''s stand fixed Sparta''s image for all time, ironically, since Sparta''s actual record is mostly caution and delay. The battle gave the West its favourite story about itself. Free men choosing death over submission, whatever the messier reality of helots, festivals, and allied troops the legend edits out.', 'Thermopylae bought the month that saved Greece. Without the delay, Athens could not have been evacuated and Salamis could not have been fought. Culturally it became the archetype of the heroic last stand, invoked at the Alamo, in both World Wars, and wherever a doomed position had to be held for time. The epitaph Simonides wrote for the Spartans is still quoted. Stranger, tell the Spartans we lie here obedient to their laws.', '/images/plates/thermopylae.svg', '["Ancient Greece","Persia","Last Stand","480 BC"]'::jsonb);

On 2 July Lee hammered both Union flanks. At Little Round Top, Colonel Joshua Chamberlain''s 20th Maine, out of ammunition, fixed bayonets and charged downhill, saving the Union left. At dusk on 3 July came the war''s most famous assault. Some 12,500 men under Pickett, Pettigrew, and Trimble marched a mile across open ground into concentrated artillery and rifle fire. Roughly half fell. The survivors straggled back across the field Lee would not look at.

Combined casualties approached 50,000, the bloodiest battle ever fought in the Western Hemisphere. Lee retreated to Virginia and never invaded the North again. Four months later Lincoln dedicated the cemetery in 272 words that redefined the war as a test of whether government of the people could survive.', 'By mid-1863 the Union blockade was strangling Southern ports, Vicksburg was about to fall, and Northern morale sagged under casualty lists. Lee believed one great victory on Union soil could win British or French recognition, or at least panic Northern voters into suing for peace. His army, fresh from Chancellorsville, was confident to the point of carelessness.

Gettysburg itself mattered only because ten roads met there. Neither commander chose it. Both were drawn in by the sound of the guns on 1 July, and Meade, new to army command by three days, arrived to find his subordinates had already picked magnificent defensive ground.', '["Lee''s strategic need for a Northern victory to secure foreign recognition and break Union political will before 1864.","The chance meeting engagement. Confederate infantry seeking shoes (so the story goes) ran into Buford''s dismounted cavalry, escalating beyond either commander''s plan.","Stuart''s cavalry, Lee''s eyes, rode off raiding instead of screening, leaving Lee blind about Union positions for the critical days.","Meade''s subordinates, especially Hancock and Warren, seized Cemetery Ridge and Little Round Top before Lee could occupy them."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1863-07-01","title":"Meeting engagement","description":"Buford delays the Confederates. Union infantry retreats through town to Cemetery Ridge."},{"date":"1863-07-02","title":"Little Round Top","description":"Chamberlain''s bayonet charge saves the Union left. The right holds at Culp''s Hill."},{"date":"1863-07-03","title":"Pickett''s Charge","description":"12,500 Confederates cross open ground into massed fire. Roughly half fall and the assault collapses."},{"date":"1863-07-04","title":"Retreat","description":"Lee withdraws in rain toward Virginia. Meade, cautious to the end, lets him go."},{"date":"1863-11-19","title":"Gettysburg Address","description":"Lincoln dedicates the cemetery in 272 words that redefine the war''s meaning."}]'::jsonb, '["The Confederacy never again mounted a strategic invasion of the North. The war''s endgame moved permanently south.","Coupled with Vicksburg, the twin defeats ended any realistic hope of British or French recognition.","Lee''s army survived but bled irreplaceable officers and veterans it could not replace. Grant''s 1864 campaign exploited exactly that.","Lincoln''s address reframed the war as a test of democracy itself, setting the terms of reunion and Reconstruction."]'::jsonb, '["General Meade had commanded the Army of the Potomac for exactly three days when the battle began, appointed 28 June after Hooker''s resignation.","Jennie Wade, hit by a stray bullet while baking bread, was the only civilian killed outright during the battle.","Lee''s famous meeting with the shattered Pickett, ''this has been my fight, and upon my shoulders rests the blame'', may be partly legend, but Pickett never forgave him.","Some 7 million visitors a year walk ground where some 50,000 men fell in three days, roughly one casualty per minute, day and night.","The shoes story is likely myth. The Confederates were after the town''s supplies generally, and the ''shoe factory'' tale grew afterward."]'::jsonb, 'The battlefield is America''s most visited military park, and Pickett''s Charge remains the archetypal doomed assault, studied, reenacted, and invoked whenever courage meets hopeless arithmetic. ''The world will little note, nor long remember what we say here'' is perhaps history''s most famous failed prediction.', 'With Vicksburg falling the next day, Gettysburg marks the Confederacy''s high-water mark, never again did the South threaten the North. Combined with the Emancipation Proclamation six months earlier, it ensured Britain and France could never recognize a slaveholding republic without disgrace. And Lincoln''s address turned a cemetery dedication into the second founding document of American democracy.', '/images/plates/gettysburg.svg', '["American Civil War","United States","Turning Point","1863"]'::jsonb);

On the morning of 7 December 1941, six Japanese carriers launched 353 aircraft in two waves against Pearl Harbor. In ninety minutes they sank or beached eight battleships, destroyed 188 aircraft, and killed 2,403 Americans, the Arizona''s forward magazine exploding with the loss of 1,177 men. The attack was tactically brilliant and strategically catastrophic. The three Pacific carriers were at sea, the oil tank farms and drydocks were untouched, and Nagumo withdrew without ordering the third wave that might have wrecked the base itself.

The next day Roosevelt called it a date which will live in infamy, Congress declared war with one dissenting vote, and Germany, gratuitously, declared war on America four days later. The sleeping giant Yamamoto is said to have feared was now awake, and its shipyards would launch more carrier tonnage in 1943 alone than Japan built in the entire war.', 'Japan''s war in China, begun in 1937, consumed men and money without end. When America froze Japanese assets and embargoed oil in response to the occupation of French Indochina, Japan, importing nearly all its petroleum, faced economic strangulation within two years. The army wanted to strike south for Dutch oil. The navy knew that meant war with Britain and America.

Negotiations in Washington ran through autumn 1941 while the Kido Butai secretly sortied under radio silence. Both sides misread the other. Washington expected the blow to fall on Malaya or the Philippines, and Pearl Harbor''s defenders, worried about sabotage, not air attack, parked their aircraft wingtip to wingtip.', '["The American oil embargo left Japan''s war machine with roughly two years of reserves, conquest or collapse were the only options on the table.","Yamamoto''s doctrine that only a short, sharp shock could deliver a negotiated settlement before American industry mobilized.","Intelligence failures on both sides. America never imagined carriers could strike Hawaii. Japan never imagined the American carriers would be at sea.","The Tripartite Pact''s logic. Japan assumed Germany''s war with Britain would keep the West divided, and Hitler''s declaration of war on America then sealed the Axis''s fate."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1941-12-07","title":"First wave, 07:48","description":"183 aircraft strike Battleship Row and the airfields. The Arizona explodes."},{"date":"1941-12-07","title":"Second wave","description":"171 more aircraft hit ships and installations. 2,403 Americans are dead by 09:45."},{"date":"1941-12-07","title":"Nagumo withdraws","description":"Fearing the missing carriers, Nagumo cancels the third wave, sparing the oil farms and drydocks."},{"date":"1941-12-08","title":"Infamy speech","description":"Roosevelt asks Congress for war. The vote is 82–0 in the Senate and 388–1 in the House."},{"date":"1941-12-11","title":"Germany declares war","description":"Hitler, unobliged by treaty, declares war on America, solving Roosevelt''s Germany-first dilemma by gift."}]'::jsonb, '["The United States entered the war with its industry intact and its public united, the decisive fact of 1941.","Eight battleships sunk or damaged, but the carriers, oil, and drydocks survived, the base was back in operation within months.","Germany''s gratuitous declaration of war gave Roosevelt the two-front war he wanted without a domestic fight.","Japan gained six months of conquest, and guaranteed eventual defeat by an economy ten times its size."]'::jsonb, '["The famous ''Tora! Tora! Tora!'' signal meant complete surprise achieved, commander Fuchida''s report back to the fleet.","Washington had broken Japanese diplomatic codes (Magic) but not the naval code, it knew war was coming, just not where.","One Japanese midget submarine got inside the harbour and fired torpedoes. Its wreck was found in 2002, ending a sixty-year argument.","The drydocks and 4.5 million gallons of fuel oil survived untouched, Nimitz later said their loss would have set the Pacific war back two years.","The lone dissenting vote against war was Jeannette Rankin, who had also voted against entering World War I."]'::jsonb, 'December 7 remains America''s original day of infamy, the template for every surprise-attack debate since, including 9/11, explicitly framed as a second Pearl Harbor. The Arizona memorial still leaks oil, drop by drop, over the entombed crew.', 'Pearl Harbor ended American isolationism in a morning and created the arsenal of democracy that won the war. The United States would outproduce all Axis powers combined. It also proved the carrier''s supremacy by sinking battleships with aircraft, the lesson both sides applied at Midway six months later, where Japan paid for it.', '/images/plates/pearl-harbor.svg', '["World War II","Pacific War","Naval Warfare","Surprise Attack"]'::jsonb);

On 14 October, Harold''s exhausted army, perhaps 7,000 men, formed a shield wall on Senlac Hill near Hastings. William''s Normans, roughly equal in number but with archers and cavalry, attacked uphill all day. The English line held for hours. Then Norman feigned retreats twice drew pursuers off the ridge to be cut down, and late in the day Harold was killed, tradition, from the Bayeux Tapestry, says by an arrow in the eye. Leaderless, the shield wall broke at dusk.

William marched on London, burned Southwark to force submission, and was crowned on Christmas Day 1066. The Anglo-Saxon aristocracy was replaced almost wholesale within twenty years. By the Domesday survey of 1086, barely a handful of English landholders remained.', 'Edward the Confessor, Norman-raised and Norman-favouring, left a succession deliberately ambiguous, or so every claimant insisted. Harold had the English witans''s vote but a weak blood claim. William had a papal banner and a dubious promise. Hardrada had a treaty with a dead king. Eleventh-century succession ran on force wearing the clothes of law.

England in 1066 was rich, centralized, and militarily old-fashioned. No standing army, no cavalry tradition, a levy (the fyrd) that served limited terms. Normandy was a military machine built for conquest, its knights and archers the most modern combined arms in the West.', '["An ambiguous succession. Three credible claimants, each with a plausible story, made war the only arbitration available.","Harold''s impossible September. Destroying Hardrada at Stamford Bridge left his army bloodied and 240 miles from the second invasion.","Norman military superiority in combination, archers to disorder, infantry to fix, cavalry to shatter, against an army with essentially one tactic.","Papal backing gave William''s invasion the status of a sanctioned enterprise, easing recruitment across France."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1066-09-25","title":"Stamford Bridge","description":"Harold destroys Hardrada''s Vikings in the north, the end of the Viking Age in England."},{"date":"1066-09-28","title":"William lands","description":"The Normans come ashore unopposed at Pevensey while Harold is still in Yorkshire."},{"date":"1066-10-14","title":"The shield wall holds","description":"Hours of uphill Norman attacks break against the English line."},{"date":"1066-10-14","title":"The wall breaks","description":"Feigned retreats lure defenders off the ridge. Harold is killed and the army dissolves by dusk."},{"date":"1066-12-25","title":"Crowned","description":"William is crowned in Westminster Abbey, England has a Norman king."}]'::jsonb, '["The Anglo-Saxon aristocracy was almost entirely replaced. By Domesday (1086) England''s landholders were overwhelmingly Norman.","Norman French became the language of court, law, and government for three centuries, permanently transforming English.","Feudal tenure, Norman castle-building, and continental monastic orders reorganized English society top to bottom.","England was tied to France dynastically, the root of four centuries of Anglo-French war."]'::jsonb, '["Harold fought two full-scale battles 240 miles apart within nineteen days, a marching feat Roman legions would have respected.","The arrow-in-the-eye comes from the Bayeux Tapestry, where a figure labelled Harold appears with a projectile near his face, scholars still argue whether it shows his death at all.","William''s flagship, the Mora, outran his own fleet overnight. He arrived off England alone and had to wait for his army to catch up.","Halley''s Comet appeared in 1066 and was taken across Europe as an omen, it is stitched into the Tapestry itself.","The English fyrd''s limited service terms meant part of Harold''s army had already gone home to gather the harvest before Hastings."]'::jsonb, 'The Bayeux Tapestry, 70 metres of embroidered propaganda, is the most famous visual record of the Middle Ages. Battle Abbey still marks the high altar on the spot where Harold fell, and ''1066'' remains the one date every English schoolchild knows.', 'Hastings reoriented England from Scandinavia toward France for four centuries. Norman French flooded the language (beef, pork, parliament, justice), Norman law and feudalism replaced Anglo-Saxon custom, and England''s ruling class spoke French for 300 years. Without it there is no Plantagenet empire, no Hundred Years'' War as we know it, and no English language as we speak it, modern English is essentially the post-1066 compromise between Germanic grammar and French vocabulary.', '/images/plates/hastings.svg', '["Medieval","England","Normans","1066"]'::jsonb);

The governorship of Gaul (58–50 BC) made him. In eight campaigning seasons he conquered everything between the Atlantic and the Rhine, invaded Britain twice, and wrote it all up in dispatches, the Gallic Wars, so crisp they are still first-year Latin. Ancient sources claim a million Gauls killed and a million enslaved. Modern historians treat the figures as propaganda, but the conquest itself permanently attached France to the Mediterranean world.

Ordered to disband and face prosecution, Caesar instead crossed the Rubicon in January 49 BC, reportedly saying the die is cast, and marched on Rome. Four years of civil war ended with his enemies dead at Pharsalus, in Egypt, and at Munda. Made dictator for life in February 44 BC, he was stabbed twenty-three times by some sixty senators on the Ides of March, 15 March 44 BC, at the foot of Pompey''s statue.', 'By 100 BC the Roman Republic was an empire run by a city council. Conquests had flooded Italy with enslaved labour and cheap grain, ruining small farmers who then crowded Rome as a volatile urban mob. Generals, Marius, then Sulla, learned that loyal veteran armies were a better route to power than elections, and Sulla''s dictatorship (82–79 BC) showed that a Roman could seize the state, murder his enemies by the thousand, and retire unpunished.

Politics had become a contest between populares, who bypassed the Senate with popular assemblies, and optimates, who defended senatorial privilege. Caesar, nephew by marriage to Marius, chose the populares, and then, like Sulla, discovered that the Republic''s unwritten rules only bound those too weak to break them.', '["A decade of humiliation and debt politics. Caesar owed millions, and only continuous office, with its immunity, kept his creditors and prosecutors at bay.","The Senate''s demand in 50 BC that he disband his army and return to Rome as a private citizen, which meant certain prosecution and probable exile.","Pompey''s drift into the optimates'' camp after 53 BC, turning an ally who commanded Spain''s legions into the Senate''s sword.","Personal rivalry elevated to constitutional crisis. Neither Caesar nor Cato''s circle would accept second place, and the Republic had no mechanism for compromise between armed equals."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"-0060-01-01","title":"First Triumvirate","description":"Caesar, Pompey, and Crassus pool money, soldiers, and influence to dominate Roman politics."},{"date":"-0058-01-01","title":"Gallic Wars begin","description":"Eight years of conquest across modern France, with two expeditions to Britain."},{"date":"-0049-01-10","title":"Crossing the Rubicon","description":"Caesar marches on Rome. The die is cast and the Republic effectively ends."},{"date":"-0048-08-09","title":"Pharsalus","description":"Caesar''s veterans destroy Pompey''s larger army in Greece. Pompey flees to Egypt and assassination."},{"date":"-0045-01-01","title":"Julian calendar","description":"Caesar reforms the chaotic Roman calendar, the 365-day system Europe used until 1582."},{"date":"-0044-02-15","title":"Dictator for life","description":"The title confirms every senator''s fear that the Republic will never return."},{"date":"-0044-03-15","title":"Assassination","description":"Stabbed 23 times in the Theatre of Pompey by conspirators including Brutus."}]'::jsonb, '["Thirteen more years of civil war ending at Actium, after which Augustus founded the Empire on Caesar''s precedent.","Gaul permanently absorbed into the Roman and then Romance world, France speaks a Latin language because of these campaigns.","The Julian calendar, debt reform, and an expanded Senate of 900 reshaped Roman administration for centuries.","The title Caesar detached from the man and became the generic word for supreme ruler across Europe and Asia."]'::jsonb, '["Kidnapped by pirates as a young man, Caesar joked that he would return and crucify them, then did exactly that after his ransom was paid.","His famous ''Veni, vidi, vici'' described a four-hour victory over Pharnaces at Zela in 47 BC, and was paraded on a placard at his triumph.","The month of July is named for him, Quintilis was renamed Julius in his honour during his lifetime.","He was famously epileptic by some ancient accounts, though modern scholars debate whether the seizures described were epilepsy at all.","His will, read after his death, left every Roman citizen 300 sesterces, Antony reading it aloud turned the funeral crowd into a mob that burned the conspirators'' houses."]'::jsonb, 'Every subsequent Roman ruler claimed Caesar''s mantle, and every republic since has feared its own Caesar, the popular general who loves the constitution to death. Shakespeare''s play fixed him in English imagination as the archetype of ambition punished, while historians still argue whether he killed a living republic or merely buried a corpse.', 'Caesar proved the Republic could not survive its own generals, and his heir Augustus used the lesson to build the Empire on Caesar''s foundations, keeping republican names while holding monarchic power. His name became the word for emperor in German (Kaiser), Russian (Tsar), and a dozen other languages. His calendar ran Europe for sixteen centuries. His prose still teaches Latin.', '/images/plates/julius-caesar.svg', '["Ancient Rome","Republic","Assassination"]'::jsonb);

What he built next was a war machine without precedent. Decimal organization, absolute discipline, total mobility, siege engineers recruited from conquered China, and a doctrine of terror, surrender and live under light tribute, resist and be annihilated, that made most cities choose wisely. Between 1211 and his death in 1227 his armies took northern China from the Jin, shattered the Khwarezmian empire of Persia in a two-year campaign of astonishing speed, and probed as far as Russia and the Caucasus.

At his death during the campaign against Western Xia, his empire stretched from the Pacific to the Caspian. His sons and grandsons finished the job. By 1279 Mongol khans ruled China, Persia, Russia, and Central Asia, the largest contiguous land empire ever assembled, and the engine of the first sustained contact between East and West.', 'The twelfth-century steppe was a chaos of feuding tribes, Tatars, Kereits, Naimans, Merkits, raiding each other for herds and brides while the Jin dynasty of northern China played them against one another with bribes and titles. No steppe confederation had ever lasted, because no leader had ever solved the succession problem or subordinated tribal loyalty to anything larger.

Temüjin''s innovation was institutional, not just personal. Promotion by merit instead of birth, spoils shared across the whole army instead of hoarded by aristocrats, a written legal code (the Yassa), religious tolerance as policy, and a guard corps drawn from every tribe as hostages and officer school at once.', '["Personal catastrophe as education. Abandonment taught Temüjin that tribal loyalty was worthless and only sworn personal bonds, anda brotherhood, could be trusted.","Jin China''s divide-and-rule subsidies, which armed the tribes and taught them siegecraft''s value while breeding resentment of the paymasters.","The charisma-merit engine. Talented outsiders, Chinese engineers, Muslim administrators, captured generals, rose higher under Genghis than birth would ever have taken them.","Khwarezmia''s fatal error. The Shah executed Mongol envoys and merchants in 1218, giving Genghis both a pretext and a fury that annihilated an empire in two years."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1206-01-01","title":"Proclaimed Khan","description":"The tribes unite under one ruler on the Onon. The Mongol military machine is formalized."},{"date":"1211-01-01","title":"War against the Jin","description":"Invasion of northern China. Zhongdu (Beijing) falls in 1215 with the help of defecting Chinese engineers."},{"date":"1219-01-01","title":"Invasion of Khwarezmia","description":"Some of history''s fastest campaigns. Samarkand and Bukhara fall, Persia is shattered."},{"date":"1223-05-31","title":"Kalka River","description":"A Mongol reconnaissance force destroys a Rus''-Cuman army, Europe''s first warning, ignored."},{"date":"1227-08-18","title":"Death","description":"Dies during the Western Xia campaign, cause unknown. His burial place remains hidden by design."}]'::jsonb, '["The largest contiguous land empire in history, connecting China, Persia, Russia, and Europe under one postal and tax system.","The Silk Road reopened under Mongol protection, carrying technologies, religions, crops, and eventually plague, across Eurasia.","Persia''s Khwarezmian civilization was effectively annihilated. Parts of Central Asia took generations to recover demographically.","Russia''s two centuries under Mongol overlordship shaped Muscovite autocracy, taxation, and military organization permanently."]'::jsonb, '["Nobody knows where he is buried. His funeral escort reportedly killed witnesses all the way home, and expeditions still hunt the grave.","He exempted religious leaders of all faiths from taxes, Buddhist, Muslim, Christian, and Taoist clergy alike served his administration.","His empire ran a continental postal relay, the Yam, with fresh horses every few dozen kilometres, Marco Polo marvelled at it, and Russia copied it.","The Khwarezmian campaign''s speed remains barely comprehensible. Armies crossed deserts deemed impassable and took cities hundreds of miles apart within weeks.","He is believed to have died in 1227 of uncertain causes, battle wounds, illness, and even assassination by a captive princess have all been proposed."]'::jsonb, 'Mongolia''s founding father (and onetime Soviet taboo, now on the currency and the vodka bottles). China''s Yuan dynasty. Russia''s centuries under the ''Tatar yoke,'' which shaped Muscovite autocracy. And the permanent demonstration that nomad organization, not just numbers, could humble every sedentary civilization it touched.', 'Genghis Khan connected the world. The Pax Mongolica reopened the Silk Road, carried gunpowder, printing, and navigational knowledge west and devastating plagues with them, and made Marco Polo''s journey possible. His meritocratic, tolerant, brutally disciplined model of empire ruled half of Eurasia within two generations, and his Y-chromosome, carried by an estimated 16 million living men, is history''s most successful genetic legacy.', '/images/plates/genghis-khan.svg', '["Mongols","Empire","Conquest","Steppe"]'::jsonb);

The 1930s made him. Out of office, half-mocked as a warmonger, he warned almost alone about Hitler while Britain disarmed. Munich in 1938 proved him right, and when Chamberlain fell in May 1940, days after Germany invaded France, Churchill became prime minister of a country about to lose its army at Dunkirk and, seemingly, the war.

What followed was his immortal year. ''We shall fight on the beaches'' (4 June), ''their finest hour'' (18 June), ''never was so much owed by so many to so few'' (20 August), speeches that told the literal truth (Britain stood alone) in language that made it bearable. He held the coalition together through the Blitz, wooed Roosevelt into Lend-Lease, survived the disasters of Singapore and Tobruk, and led Britain to victory in 1945, whereupon the voters, grateful and exhausted, threw him out. He returned as prime minister in 1951, won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1953, and died in 1965 with a state funeral no commoner had received for a century.', 'Interwar Britain was governed by men who had watched a generation die in Flanders and would pay almost any price to avoid repeating it. Baldwin and Chamberlain read Hitler as a grievance politician who could be satisfied. Churchill, who had studied Marlborough and read Mein Kampf, read him as a conqueror who could only be stopped.

Churchill''s wilderness years (1929–39) were politically barren and intellectually fertile. Writing history, warning about German rearmament with figures leaked by sympathetic officials, and building the moral and rhetorical arsenal he would spend in 1940.', '["A lifetime of vivid failure and recovery, Gallipoli, the gold standard, party-hopping, that left him uniquely unafraid of unpopularity.","Mastery of the English sentence as a political weapon, forged in journalism, history, and a thousand parliamentary speeches.","Accurate strategic reading of Hitler from the early 1930s, giving him unchallenged credibility when the catastrophe arrived.","The arithmetic of May 1940. Halifax wanted to explore terms through Mussolini. Churchill''s refusal, backed by Labour, closed the door to negotiation."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1915-05-15","title":"Gallipoli fails","description":"His Dardanelles gamble collapses. He resigns and goes to the trenches."},{"date":"1940-05-10","title":"Becomes Prime Minister","description":"Takes office as Germany invades France. Offers blood, toil, tears, and sweat."},{"date":"1940-06-04","title":"\"We shall fight on the beaches\"","description":"The Dunkirk speech turns evacuation into defiance."},{"date":"1940-08-20","title":"\"The Few\"","description":"Immortalizes the RAF pilots winning the Battle of Britain."},{"date":"1946-03-05","title":"Iron curtain speech","description":"At Fulton, Missouri, names the Cold War division he had foreseen."},{"date":"1953-12-10","title":"Nobel Prize","description":"Literature prize for histories and oratory, the only prime minister so honoured."}]'::jsonb, '["Britain stayed in the war through 1940–41, preserving the base from which Europe was later liberated.","His Fulton speech framed the Cold War for the West, hardening the division his wartime diplomacy had helped create.","His war histories shaped public memory for a generation, including generous treatment of himself.","The Bengal famine of 1943, worsened by wartime priorities he set, remains the grave charge against his record."]'::jsonb, '["He won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1953, officially for his histories and ''brilliant oratory in defending exalted human values.''","He painted some 500 canvases under a pseudonym, exhibited in Paris, and called painting his antidote to the ''black dog'' of depression.","His mother, Jennie Jerome, was American, Brooklyn-born, which made him, by blood, half the Atlantic alliance he built.","He escaped a Boer prisoner-of-war camp in 1899 by climbing a wall and hopping a freight train, then filed the story to his own newspaper.","His state funeral in 1965 drew the largest assembly of world leaders to that date, the last Victorian-style farewell Britain ever staged."]'::jsonb, 'Voted Greatest Briton in 2002, quoted by every subsequent prime minister, studied wherever leadership under existential pressure is taught. The critical literature is equally vast. Gallipoli, Tonypandy, the Bengal famine of 1943 (where his decisions worsened catastrophe), and his imperial romanticism complicate every statue, as serious history should.', 'In May 1940 Britain''s war cabinet seriously debated seeking terms. Churchill''s refusal kept the last democracy in Europe fighting through the year Hitler could have won everything. Without that decision there is no base for D-Day, no Ultra partnership at scale, and no post-war West as we know it. His histories then wrote the war''s meaning for generations, self-serving, magnificent, and largely adopted.', '/images/plates/winston-churchill.svg', '["World War II","Britain","Leadership","Oratory"]'::jsonb);

He then proceeded to lose New York, retreat across New Jersey, and watch his army melt toward expiration, before the gambles that saved the cause. The Delaware crossing and Trenton (Christmas 1776), Princeton days later, the survival winter at Valley Forge (1777–78) where von Steuben drilled professionals out of farmers, and the patient war of posts until French money, troops, and above all de Grasse''s fleet trapped Cornwallis at Yorktown in October 1781.

Then came the acts that made him Washington. In 1783 he crushed the Newburgh conspiracy of officers threatening Congress, with spectacles and a sentence about growing grey in service, and resigned his commission to Congress, astonishing a world that expected a crown. Recalled to preside over the Constitutional Convention and then the presidency (1789–97), he refused a third term, warned against parties and foreign entanglements in his Farewell Address, and died at Mount Vernon in 1799.', 'The thirteen colonies in 1775 had no army, no navy, no treasury, and no government beyond a debating Congress, facing the world''s greatest military and fiscal power over the question of Parliament''s right to tax and legislate. Previous colonial wars had been fought as auxiliaries to British regulars. This time the regulars were the enemy.

Washington''s asset was negative space. The British had to conquer and hold a continent three thousand miles from home, while he merely had to keep an army in existence. Time, distance, French aid, and British political division did the rest, provided the army survived, which for seven years was usually in doubt.', '["British coercion after 1763, Stamp Act, Townshend duties, the Intolerable Acts, converted commercial disputes into a sovereignty crisis.","Colonial military experience. A generation of officers, Washington included, had learned war as British auxiliaries and knew both the methods and the vulnerabilities.","French rivalry. Versailles needed only an excuse to avenge 1763, and an American rebellion supplied it with interest.","Washington''s own suitability, Virginian, wealthy, dignified, experienced, made a continental army politically possible where a New Englander could not."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1775-06-15","title":"Appointed commander","description":"Congress unanimously chooses the Virginian to lead the new Continental Army."},{"date":"1776-12-25","title":"Crossing the Delaware","description":"Surprise attack at Trenton revives the dying revolution."},{"date":"1777-12-19","title":"Valley Forge","description":"The army starves, freezes, trains, and emerges professional."},{"date":"1781-10-19","title":"Yorktown","description":"Cornwallis surrenders. American independence becomes a matter of negotiation."},{"date":"1783-12-23","title":"Resignation","description":"Surrenders his commission to Congress, the act that made republican legend."},{"date":"1789-04-30","title":"First inauguration","description":"Sworn in as first President in New York."}]'::jsonb, '["American independence recognized at Paris in 1783. A republic founded on Enlightenment principles amid slavery.","The presidency invented as an office, every power Washington exercised or declined set precedent.","The Farewell Address''s warnings shaped American isolationism and party suspicion for generations.","His slaveholding, and his will''s delayed manumission, left the republic''s original contradiction for the Civil War to settle."]'::jsonb, '["He was unanimously elected president by the Electoral College, twice, the only president ever so chosen, because there was no opposition to be unanimous against.","At Newburgh in 1783 he defused a potential coup by fumbling for spectacles and remarking he had grown grey and blind in service, officers wept, and the conspiracy died.","His teeth were not wood. Surviving dentures combine human teeth (possibly purchased from enslaved people), ivory, and metal.","He brewed and distilled at Mount Vernon, his whiskey distillery was among America''s largest, producing 11,000 gallons in 1799.","His Farewell Address warning against ''entangling alliances'' guided American foreign policy rhetoric into the twentieth century."]'::jsonb, 'The capital, the state, the monument, the currency, the cherry-tree myths, Washington is America''s civic religion. Serious history adds the harder parts. A slaveholder who freed his slaves only in his will, a general who ordered harsh discipline, a president whose precedents (cabinet, two terms, neutrality) governed until they didn''t.', 'Washington invented the American presidency by refusing to be a king, twice declining powers lesser men seized. His Farewell Address set isolationism and non-partisanship as American ideals for a century and a half. Militarily he proved that preserving an army can be victory enough. Politically he proved that republics can survive their founders, because he arranged to leave.', '/images/plates/george-washington.svg', '["United States","Revolution","Presidency","Founding"]'::jsonb);

Expelled by her brother''s ministers in 48 BC, she famously smuggled herself into Julius Caesar''s quarters, tradition says rolled in a carpet, and emerged as his ally, mistress, and mother of his son Caesarion. Caesar''s money and legions restored her. Caesar''s murder in 44 BC nearly destroyed her. She rebuilt her position by binding herself to Mark Antony, Rome''s eastern strongman, bearing him three children and ruling the eastern Mediterranean at his side through the 30s BC.

Octavian''s propaganda painted Antony as a besotted captive of a foreign queen, and the Donations of Alexandria (34 BC), parceling eastern kingdoms to Cleopatra''s children, gave Rome its pretext. War was declared, against Cleopatra, not Antony. At Actium on 2 September 31 BC their fleet collapsed. Both fled to Egypt. When Octavian''s troops entered Alexandria in August 30 BC, Antony fell on his sword and Cleopatra, aged thirty-nine, took her own life, by tradition, with the bite of an asp. Octavian had Caesarion strangled and annexed Egypt.', 'Ptolemaic Egypt in the first century BC was a rich prize in a Roman world. The dynasty survived by paying Roman politicians enormous bribes, Cleopatra''s father once paid Caesar''s equivalent of several tons of gold for recognition. Rome''s civil wars repeatedly spilled into Egypt because its grain could starve or feed whoever held Rome itself.

Meanwhile Roman politics had outgrown the Republic. After Caesar, power belonged to whoever commanded legions and controlled Egypt''s harvest. Cleopatra understood this perfectly. Her liaisons with Caesar and then Antony were not romances that happened to have political consequences, they were the foreign policy of a small kingdom negotiating survival between Roman warlords.', '["Dynastic civil war. Expelled by Ptolemy XIII''s ministers, Cleopatra needed an external patron to recover her throne, and Caesar arrived in Alexandria pursuing Pompey.","Egypt''s structural dependence on Rome. Debt, grain leverage, and the presence of a Roman garrison left from earlier interventions meant no Ptolemy ruled without Roman consent.","Antony''s need for Egyptian money and ships for his planned Parthian war, which made the alliance mutually indispensable through the 30s BC.","Octavian''s need for a unifying enemy in Italy. Declaring war on a foreign queen rallied Romans who would never have backed a war against fellow citizen Antony."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"-0051-03-01","title":"Takes the throne","description":"Eighteen years old, co-ruling with her child brother, and soon expelled by his ministers."},{"date":"-0048-10-01","title":"Caesar in Alexandria","description":"Cleopatra wins Caesar''s backing. The Alexandrine War restores her with Ptolemy XIV as junior co-ruler."},{"date":"-0047-06-23","title":"Caesarion born","description":"Her son by Caesar, proclaimed king of kings, and Octavian''s future motive for murder."},{"date":"-0041-01-01","title":"Meets Antony at Tarsus","description":"Arriving as Aphrodite, she secures the alliance that will define the next decade."},{"date":"-0034-01-01","title":"Donations of Alexandria","description":"Antony parcels eastern kingdoms to Cleopatra''s children, Octavian''s propaganda gift."},{"date":"-0031-09-02","title":"Actium","description":"Octavian''s admiral Agrippa destroys their fleet. Both leaders flee to Egypt."},{"date":"-0030-08-10","title":"Death in Alexandria","description":"Antony dies by his own hand. Cleopatra follows, by tradition, by asp bite."}]'::jsonb, '["Egypt became a Roman province, Augustus''s personal property, feeding Rome for six centuries.","The Hellenistic age ended. The last successor kingdom of Alexander was absorbed into Rome.","Caesarion was executed. The Ptolemaic bloodline, three centuries old, was extinguished.","Octavian''s victory became the foundation myth of the Roman Empire, Actium justified the monarchy."]'::jsonb, '["Her death date is uncertain even now, 10 or 12 August 30 BC, and the asp story comes from hostile Roman sources. Poison or a hairpin are equally possible.","She was not Egyptian by blood but Macedonian Greek, yet she presented herself in temples as the living Isis, in Egyptian dress and rites.","Antony and Cleopatra formed a drinking society called the Inimitable Livers, a piece of propaganda-proof revelry Octavian''s writers never forgave.","Caesarion, her son by Caesar, was seventeen when Octavian had him strangled with the justification that too many Caesars was no good.","Her palace quarter of Alexandria, including likely her tomb, now lies drowned offshore, earthquakes sank it, and archaeologists still dive the site."]'::jsonb, 'Two thousand years of art, from Plutarch to Shakespeare to Elizabeth Taylor, have used her as the archetype of dangerous female power, a legend that says more about each era''s anxieties than about the queen. Modern scholarship has begun recovering the strategist underneath. The only Ptolemy who spoke Egyptian, who kept her kingdom out of Roman hands longer than anyone had a right to expect.', 'Cleopatra''s defeat ended both Ptolemaic Egypt and the Hellenistic age. Actium made Octavian Augustus and the Roman Republic the Roman Empire. Egypt became Rome''s personal breadbasket for six centuries. And Cleopatra herself became the most mythologized woman of antiquity, Shakespeare''s serpent of Nile, Hollywood''s seductress, obscuring the multilingual administrator who kept a bankrupt kingdom independent for twenty-one years against Rome.', '/images/plates/cleopatra.svg', '["Ancient Egypt","Rome","Pharaoh","Ptolemaic Dynasty"]'::jsonb);

At its greatest extent under Trajan (AD 117), the empire ringed the Mediterranean from Britain to Egypt to Mesopotamia, about five million square kilometres, perhaps a fifth of humanity. It was an urban civilization of aqueducts, concrete domes, codified law, and a professional army of some thirty legions that doubled as engineers and policemen.

Rome did not fall in a day. The third century brought plague, inflation, and barracks emperors. Diocletian (284–305) saved the state by splitting it in practice, and Constantine moved the capital east to Constantinople in 330. The western half, poorer and more exposed, dissolved between 406 and 476 as Germanic kingdoms took over its provinces, the last western emperor, a boy ironically named Romulus Augustulus, was deposed in 476. The eastern half, which we call Byzantine, carried Roman law, administration, and the imperial title for another thousand years until 1453.', 'The Republic conquered the Mediterranean between 264 and 146 BC and then choked on its winnings. Slave-worked estates displaced citizen farmers, who crowded into Rome as a rent-a-mob. Generals from Marius to Sulla to Pompey to Caesar learned that loyal armies beat constitutional procedure. Caesar''s assassination in 44 BC settled nothing, it took thirteen more years of war before his adopted son Octavian defeated Antony and Cleopatra at Actium (31 BC) and stood alone.

Augustus''s genius was camouflage. He kept the Senate, the consuls, and the assemblies, accumulated their powers quietly, and called the result the restoration of the Republic. Nobody was fooled for long, but everybody, exhausted by civil war, preferred the fiction to another proscription list.', '["A century of republican civil war (133–31 BC) destroyed every alternative. By Actium, Romans would accept any government that promised no more war.","Augustus''s constitutional settlement preserved republican forms while concentrating military and financial power, monarchy that dared not speak its name.","The professional standing army. Thirty legions on the frontiers gave the empire both security and a permanent instrument of usurpation.","An administrative system, census, taxation in coin, Roman law, municipal self-government, that made conquered elites stakeholders instead of subjects."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"-0027-01-16","title":"Augustus takes power","description":"The Republic becomes an empire behind republican masks."},{"date":"0117-01-01","title":"Greatest extent","description":"Under Trajan the empire touches Britain, the Atlantic, the Sahara, and Mesopotamia."},{"date":"0212-01-01","title":"Citizenship for all","description":"Caracalla extends Roman citizenship to nearly every free man in the empire."},{"date":"0285-01-01","title":"Empire divided","description":"Diocletian formalizes the split between a Latin West and a Greek East."},{"date":"0330-05-11","title":"Constantinople founded","description":"Constantine dedicates his new eastern capital, the future thousand-year survivor."},{"date":"0476-09-04","title":"West falls","description":"Odoacer deposes the boy-emperor Romulus Augustulus. Germanic kingdoms inherit the western provinces."},{"date":"1453-05-29","title":"Constantinople falls","description":"The last Roman capital is taken. The imperial line that began with Augustus ends."}]'::jsonb, '["The Mediterranean became a single economic and legal space for the first time, and, after Rome, would never be again.","Latin evolved into French, Spanish, Italian, Portuguese, and Romanian. Roman law became the basis of every civil-law system on earth.","Christianity, legalized in 313 and established under Theodosius, inherited the empire''s roads, cities, and administrative geography.","The division of 285 hardened into the Latin West and Greek East, the ancestors, respectively, of Western Europe and of Byzantium, Russia, and Orthodox civilization."]'::jsonb, '["Roman concrete, volcanic ash, lime, and seawater, grows stronger with age. The Pantheon''s unreinforced dome, poured around AD 125, is still the world''s largest of its kind.","At its height the empire consumed so much olive oil that discarded amphorae formed Monte Testaccio in Rome, a hill 35 metres high made entirely of broken pots.","The emperor''s mail service, the cursus publicus, relayed riders up to 80 kilometres a day, unmatched in Europe until the telegraph.","Gladiatorial games were vastly outnumbered by chariot racing as mass entertainment. The Circus Maximus held perhaps 150,000 spectators.","When Odoacer deposed the last western emperor in 476, almost nobody at the time thought anything world-historical had happened, the ''fall of Rome'' is largely a later invention."]'::jsonb, 'Two millennia on, popes still use imperial titles, the US Capitol copies Roman temples, and every Western army marches in the shadow of the legion. When modern states debate citizenship, codified law, or the limits of executive power, they argue in categories Rome invented.', 'Rome supplied the operating system of the West. Civil law codes from Napoleon to Louisiana, Romance languages spoken by a billion people, the calendar, concrete engineering, urban planning, Christianity''s institutional shape, and the very vocabulary of power, senate, republic, emperor, dictator, citizen. The European Union''s founders consciously invoked Rome as precedent for unity without conquest.', '/images/plates/roman-empire.svg', '["Ancient Rome","Empire","Mediterranean"]'::jsonb);

Constantinople''s fall in 1453 made the Ottomans an empire. Suleiman the Magnificent (1520–66) made it the terror of Europe, Belgrade, Rhodes, Hungary at Mohács (1526), the siege of Vienna (1529), and a navy that contested the entire Mediterranean. At its 1683 peak the empire held the Balkans, the Middle East, Egypt, North Africa''s coast, Crimea, and western Arabia. Three continents, dozens of peoples, one sultan-caliph.

The failed second siege of Vienna in 1683 marked the turn. Slowly, then suddenly, the empire became the ''sick man of Europe''. Losing Hungary, then Greece, then the Balkans, then, in World War I, everything. The sultanate was abolished in 1922. Atatürk''s Turkish Republic rose from the Anatolian core in 1923.', 'Anatolia in 1300 was a vacuum. The Byzantine frontier had collapsed, the Seljuk sultanate had fragmented under Mongol pressure, and dozens of Turkish beyliks competed for land and plunder. Osman''s stood out for one reason, position on the Byzantine border meant endless holy war (gaza) recruitment, attracting ambitious fighters from across the Islamic world.

The Ottomans also inherited the best of both worlds. Byzantine administrative geography and Persian-Islamic statecraft, fused with steppe military organization. While Christian Europe fragmented into competing kingdoms, the Ottomans built the early modern world''s most centralized state.', '["Frontier position on a collapsing border, supplying endless recruits motivated by faith and plunder.","The devshirme system. Harvesting Christian youth for the Janissaries and administration created a loyal, meritocratic elite dependent solely on the sultan.","Early adoption of gunpowder weapons, Janissary muskets and great bombards, against feudal cavalry armies.","Pragmatic tolerance. Millets (religious communities) governed themselves, making Ottoman rule preferable to many subjects than the alternative."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1299-01-01","title":"Osman''s beylik","description":"A frontier chieftaincy begins six centuries of dynasty."},{"date":"1453-05-29","title":"Constantinople taken","description":"Mehmed II makes the city his imperial capital."},{"date":"1526-08-29","title":"Mohács","description":"Medieval Hungary destroyed in a day. Suleiman takes Buda."},{"date":"1683-09-11","title":"Vienna holds","description":"The second siege fails. Ottoman expansion into Europe ends."},{"date":"1922-11-01","title":"Sultanate abolished","description":"Six centuries end by parliamentary vote. The Republic follows."}]'::jsonb, '["The Balkans and Middle East inherited Ottoman administrative borders, sectarian maps, and unresolved national questions.","The empire''s WWI collapse created the mandates and nation-states whose conflicts define the modern Middle East.","Millions of Muslims expelled from lost Balkan territories reshaped Anatolia''s demography, and its politics.","Turkey emerged as a secular republic explicitly rejecting imperial and theocratic rule."]'::jsonb, '["Mehmed II spoke six languages and collected Greek manuscripts, the conqueror of Constantinople fancied himself a Renaissance prince as well as a ghazi.","The devshirme produced grand viziers of Balkan Christian origin, including Mehmed Sokollu, who effectively ran the empire at its height.","Vienna''s coffee houses legendarily descend from sacks of beans abandoned by the retreating Ottomans in 1683.","The empire abolished the death penalty for many crimes and ran guilds, soup kitchens, and hospitals that impressed European travellers.","Suleiman''s title at home was not ''the Magnificent'' but Kanuni, the Lawgiver, for systematizing Ottoman law."]'::jsonb, 'Istanbul''s skyline, the millet tradition behind the region''s sectarian map, Turkish as a lingua franca from the Balkans to Arabia, coffee culture carried to Vienna and thence the world, and Atatürk''s secular republic, built explicitly as the empire''s antithesis.', 'The Ottomans held southeastern Europe, the Middle East, and North Africa together for longer than any modern state has existed, and their collapse drew the region''s modern borders, from the Balkans'' nation-states to the mandates that became Israel, Syria, Iraq, and Lebanon. The ''Eastern Question'', what replaces Ottoman power, drove European diplomacy for a century and its aftershocks drive Middle Eastern politics still.', '/images/plates/ottoman-empire.svg', '["Middle East","Empire","Sultans","Istanbul"]'::jsonb);

Möngke''s reign (1251–59) organized the empire into four great appanages that became permanent khanates. The Golden Horde in Russia, the Chagatai Khanate in Central Asia, the Ilkhanate in Persia, and the Yuan domain in China and Mongolia. When Kublai Khan completed the conquest of southern China in 1279 and proclaimed the Yuan dynasty, the Mongol peace, Pax Mongolica, stretched from Korea to Hungary.

Unity did not survive prosperity. Kublai''s wars against his own cousins, failed invasions of Japan (1274, 1281, defeated both times by storms the Japanese called kamikaze, divine wind) and Java, and the Black Death''s arrival along reopened trade routes fractured the system. The Yuan fell in 1368. The Golden Horde lingered until 1480. But the Ilkhanate''s Persia and the Horde''s Russia carried Mongol administrative DNA for centuries.', 'The Mongol succession combined election with heredity. A khuriltai of princes chose among Genghis''s sons, which worked exactly once. Each succession crisis (1229, 1246, 1251) nearly split the empire, and Möngke''s solution, permanent regional khanates, traded unity for manageability.

Meanwhile the sedentary world proved both rich and resilient. China absorbed its conquerors culturally within generations. Persia''s cities, once annihilated, rebuilt under Ilkhanid patronage. Russia''s princes learned autocracy, taxation, and postal relays from the Horde that ruled them. The empire''s greatest legacy may be what it taught its victims.', '["Genghis''s institutional legacy, meritocracy, the Yassa code, decimal organization, survived him and kept the machine running under lesser khans.","The wealth gap. Song China''s riches and Persia''s cities offered prizes no steppe confederation had ever sustained the discipline to take, until this one.","Europe''s and Asia''s disunity. Rus'' principalities, Polish duchies, and Song China each faced the Mongols essentially alone.","The succession system''s flexibility in the short term. Capable sons like Ögedei and Möngke could be elected over less capable elder lines."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1229-01-01","title":"Ögedei elected","description":"The empire holds together. The conquest of Jin China resumes."},{"date":"1236-01-01","title":"The western campaign","description":"Batu and Subutai conquer Rus'', Poland, and Hungary in six years."},{"date":"1241-12-11","title":"Ögedei dies","description":"The princes turn back from Europe''s edge to elect a successor."},{"date":"1258-02-10","title":"Baghdad falls","description":"Hülegü''s Ilkhanid army destroys the Abbasid capital. The caliph is rolled in a carpet and trampled."},{"date":"1279-01-01","title":"All China conquered","description":"Kublai Khan founds the Yuan dynasty. The Pax Mongolica peaks."},{"date":"1281-08-15","title":"Kamikaze","description":"A typhoon destroys Kublai''s second invasion fleet bound for Japan."}]'::jsonb, '["Eurasia connected under one postal, tax, and security system for the first time, the medieval world''s only globalization.","Baghdad''s destruction ended the Islamic Golden Age''s centre. Russia''s subjugation shaped Muscovite autocracy.","Gunpowder, printing, and navigational knowledge travelled west along Mongol-protected routes. Plague travelled with them.","The khanates'' fragmentation created the power map, Yuan China, Ilkhanid Persia, the Horde''s Russia, that early modern Eurasia inherited."]'::jsonb, '["The Mongol withdrawal from Europe in 1242 was likely about succession politics, not military defeat, the princes had to vote, and voting outranked conquering.","Kublai''s China employed Marco Polo''s father and uncle as administrators years before Marco himself arrived, the family were Mongol civil servants.","The Ilkhan Ghazan''s conversion to Islam in 1295 made Persia officially Muslim under Mongol rule, conquerors absorbed by the conquered.","Mongol census-takers counted households across Russia for taxation. The word for the levy, dan'', persisted in Russian fiscal vocabulary.","The Golden Horde''s capital Sarai, on the lower Volga, was among the largest cities on earth in the 1300s, now barely an archaeological site."]'::jsonb, 'The Yuan dynasty''s China, the Ilkhanate''s Persia (where Rashid al-Din''s world history was written), Muscovy''s autocracy learned under the Horde, and the Mughals, Babur was a Genghisid on his mother''s side. ''Tatar yoke'' and ''Pax Mongolica'' remain opposite shorthand for the same two centuries.', 'The Mongol Empire was history''s great connector. Under its protection Marco Polo travelled, gunpowder and printing moved west, Persian astronomy reached Beijing, and the Black Death rode the same roads. It proved a nomad empire could administer sedentary civilizations, and proved equally that no empire built on personal loyalty survives prosperity. Russia, China, Iran, and Central Asia all still live with its borders and institutions.', '/images/plates/mongol-empire.svg', '["Mongols","Empire","Silk Road","Khanates"]'::jsonb);

Its arc runs through Justinian (527–565), who rebuilt Hagia Sophia, codified Roman law into the Corpus Juris Civilis, and briefly reconquered Vandal Africa and Ostrogothic Italy. Through near-death in the 600s, when Persians and then Arabs stripped away Syria, Egypt, and North Africa forever. Through the Macedonian renaissance of Basil II, the Bulgar-Slayer, whose empire around the year 1000 stretched from Italy to Armenia.

Then the long diminuendo. Manzikert (1071) lost Anatolia to the Turks, the Fourth Crusade''s sack (1204) shattered the state into exile fragments, and the restored Palaiologan empire was a city with suburbs. It survived by diplomacy, walls, and the Ottomans'' other priorities, until 1453, when there was nowhere left to retreat.', 'Diocletian''s division recognized a reality. The empire''s wealth, population, and defensible frontiers lay east. Constantinople sat at the hinge of Europe and Asia, taxing every ship between the Mediterranean and the Black Sea. While the West depended on fragile Germanic federates, the East kept a professional army, a gold coinage (the bezant, stable for 700 years), and a bureaucracy that outlived every dynasty.

Religion both powered and plagued it. Christianity made the emperor God''s viceroy, but Christological disputes (Monophysites, iconoclasts) repeatedly split the empire against itself, and the 1054 schism with Rome divided Christendom permanently.', '["Geography and wealth. Anatolia''s taxes and the Bosporus tolls funded professional armies and diplomacy long after the West went broke.","The Theodosian Walls plus Greek fire plus the Golden Horn chain, a defensive system no enemy cracked by assault for a thousand years.","Diplomatic virtuosity. Subsidies, titles, marriages, and missionaries turned enemies into buffers (the Rus'' were baptized into alliance).","Administrative continuity. Themes, tagmata, and a literate bureaucracy survived emperors, dynasties, and catastrophes alike."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"0330-05-11","title":"Constantinople founded","description":"Constantine dedicates his new capital."},{"date":"0537-12-27","title":"Hagia Sophia consecrated","description":"Justinian''s dome, ''Solomon, I have outdone thee'', crowns the reconquest age."},{"date":"1014-07-29","title":"Kleidion","description":"Basil II annihilates the Bulgarian army and blinds 99 of every 100 prisoners."},{"date":"1071-08-19","title":"Manzikert","description":"Seljuk Turks crush the imperial army. Anatolia, the empire''s heartland, is lost."},{"date":"1204-04-13","title":"Fourth Crusade sacks the city","description":"Venetians and crusaders storm Christian Constantinople. The empire shatters into exiles."},{"date":"1453-05-29","title":"The city falls","description":"Eleven centuries of empire end under Ottoman cannon."}]'::jsonb, '["Roman law, Greek letters, and Orthodox Christianity survived antiquity''s collapse and seeded the Renaissance, Russia, and the Balkans.","The Ottoman conquest of the imperial capital created a three-continent Islamic empire and redirected world trade toward the oceans.","The 1054 schism hardened into permanent division between Catholic and Orthodox Christendom.","Moscow inherited the imperial claim as the ''Third Rome'', an idea Russian rulers invoke to this day."]'::jsonb, '["The Varangian Guard, the emperors'' axe-bearing bodyguard, was recruited from Vikings and Anglo-Saxons, including, reportedly, men who fled Norman England after 1066.","Greek fire''s recipe was a state secret so well kept that its exact composition remains unknown, the medieval equivalent of a classified weapon.","Basil II, the empire''s greatest soldier-emperor, never married and left no heir, his dynasty died with his spendthrift successors.","The Hippodrome''s chariot factions, Blues and Greens, functioned as political parties whose riots, like Nika in 532, costing some 30,000 lives, could topple governments.","Constantinople''s population fell from perhaps half a million at its height to some 50,000 by 1453, an imperial capital the size of a market town."]'::jsonb, 'The Kremlin''s architecture, Orthodox liturgy, the double-headed eagle from Moscow to Vienna, and the very word ''byzantine'' for labyrinthine politics. When Putin invokes the ''Third Rome,'' he cites this empire''s claim, transmitted through marriage and religion to Moscow.', 'Byzantium preserved Roman law (without which no Napoleonic Code), Greek literature (without which no Renaissance), and Orthodox Christianity (without which no Russia as we know it). It shielded a fractured Europe''s eastern flank for centuries, every crusade, every Russian baptism, every Venetian fortune passed through its hands or over its walls.', '/images/plates/byzantine-empire.svg', '["Constantinople","Empire","Middle Ages","Orthodoxy"]'::jsonb);

The Old Kingdom (c. 2686–2181 BC) built the pyramids. Djoser''s step pyramid at Saqqara first, then the true pyramids of Giza, including Khufu''s Great Pyramid, 146 metres high, two million blocks, the tallest human structure in the world for more than 3,800 years. After collapse and civil war came the Middle Kingdom''s literary golden age, then Hyksos invaders who introduced the horse and chariot, then the warrior-pharaohs of the New Kingdom (c. 1550–1070 BC). Thutmose III''s empire, Akhenaten''s religious revolution, Tutankhamun''s golden tomb, and Ramesses II''s colossal building program.

Egypt never fully recovered from the Bronze Age collapse around 1200 BC. Libyans, Nubians, Assyrians, and Persians took turns ruling the valley until Alexander arrived in 332 BC and his general Ptolemy founded a Greek-speaking dynasty. It ended with Cleopatra. After Actium in 31 BC she chose suicide over Octavian''s triumph, and Egypt, the breadbasket of empires, became a Roman province in 30 BC.', 'Before unification, the Nile valley held rival chiefdoms, Naqada culture towns with pottery, copper, and increasingly elaborate burials. The flood regime rewarded coordination. Basin irrigation required communal labour on dikes and canals, and whoever controlled the grain stores controlled lives. Kingship fused taxation, religion, and engineering from the start, the pharaoh was not merely a ruler but the guarantor that the flood would come.

Geography was Egypt''s fortune and its cage. Deserts east and west, cataracts to the south, and the sea to the north made invasion rare and unity natural, but also made Egypt insular, slow to adopt iron, cavalry warfare, and other innovations that eventually overwhelmed it.', '["The Nile''s predictable floods created storable surpluses, the material precondition for priests, armies, and monuments.","Geographic isolation gave unification a chance to stick. Unlike Mesopotamia''s open plains, Egypt could be held as one unit.","Control of Nubian gold, Sinai copper and turquoise, and Levantine timber funded the state and its building programs.","Divine kingship ideology made rebellion sacrilege and taxation worship, stabilizing the regime across dynastic breaks."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"-3100-01-01","title":"Unification","description":"Traditionally under Narmer/Menes. Upper and Lower Egypt become one kingdom."},{"date":"-2670-01-01","title":"Step Pyramid","description":"Imhotep stacks mastabas at Saqqara, architecture''s first great leap."},{"date":"-2560-01-01","title":"Great Pyramid built","description":"Khufu''s tomb rises at Giza, tallest structure on earth for millennia."},{"date":"-1550-01-01","title":"New Kingdom begins","description":"Ahmose expels the Hyksos. Egypt becomes a conquering empire."},{"date":"-1332-01-01","title":"Tutankhamun reigns","description":"The boy king restores the old gods. His intact tomb will electrify the modern world in 1922."},{"date":"-1274-01-01","title":"Kadesh","description":"Ramesses II fights the Hittites to a standstill in history''s largest chariot battle, then signs the first known peace treaty."},{"date":"-0332-01-01","title":"Alexander arrives","description":"Persian rule ends. Ptolemaic Greek dynasty begins."},{"date":"-0030-08-10","title":"Last pharaoh dies","description":"Cleopatra''s death ends three thousand years of pharaonic rule."}]'::jsonb, '["Egypt''s grain fed Rome for six centuries, whoever held the Nile held the empire''s food supply, which is why Augustus kept it as his personal province.","Pharaonic religion faded under Christianity, but its symbols, calendar, and administrative geography persisted into Coptic and Islamic Egypt.","The loss of independence ended native monumental building. Egyptian art froze into the forms Rome and then Byzantium preserved.","Nineteenth-century decipherment turned Egypt into the founding case study of archaeology and museum culture worldwide."]'::jsonb, '["The Great Pyramid was originally cased in polished white limestone and capped, possibly, in gold. It shone across the desert.","Egyptian workers who built the pyramids were not enslaved. Graffiti shows organized crews with names like ''the Drunkards of Menkaure'', paid in bread and beer.","Tutankhamun''s tomb survived intact largely because later builders piled huts over its entrance and forgot it, obscurity preserved what glory could not.","The Ebers Papyrus (c. 1550 BC) contains hundreds of medical remedies with rational diagnoses, alongside spells, honestly mixed.","Cleopatra, of the Greek Ptolemaic line, was reportedly the first of her dynasty to actually learn the Egyptian language."]'::jsonb, 'Obelisks stand in Rome, Paris, London, and New York. The Rosetta Stone''s decipherment in 1822 founded Egyptology. Mummies, pyramids, and Tutankhamun''s mask are the global visual shorthand for antiquity itself, and modern Egypt still lives in the pharaohs'' shadow, from national symbolism to the Aswan Dam''s relationship with the Nile.', 'Egypt invented or perfected the toolkit of civilization. Writing that recorded administration (hieroglyphs and hieratic), monumental stone architecture, centralized bureaucracy, calendar timekeeping (365 days), and medicine with diagnoses and prognoses. Greece and Rome both learned at Egypt''s feet, Herodotus called it the gift of the Nile, and its three-thousand-year run remains unmatched by any state before or since.', '/images/plates/ancient-egypt.svg', '["Egypt","Pharaohs","Antiquity"]'::jsonb);

Fewer than fifty Mark Is rattled toward Flers-Courcelette on 15 September 1916. Half broke down, but the rest walked through wire the infantry could never have crossed. Cambrai in November 1917, 476 tanks without preliminary bombardment, tore a six-mile breach and proved the concept at scale, though German counterattacks retook the ground.

The interwar years belonged to theorists. Fuller''s Plan 1919, Liddell Hart''s expanding torrent, de Gaulle''s ignored memoranda, and Guderian, who read them all and built the Panzerwaffe around radio, combined arms, and speed. In 1940 tanks did in six weeks what four years of trenches never had. The Eastern Front then staged the largest armoured battles in history, Kursk in July 1943 alone involved thousands of fighting vehicles, and settled the design argument. The Soviet T-34, built by the tens of thousands, beat Germany''s superb, scarce Tigers. Quantity with radios defeated quality without fuel.', 'Pre-1914 armies assumed the next war would be short and mobile. Instead railways delivered millions to fronts where firepower froze them in place. Every 1915 expedient, gas, mines, longer bombardments, killed more men without restoring movement. The internal-combustion engine offered the only way to carry protection and firepower across broken ground, but no army owned such a vehicle.

Naval thinking provided the template. Churchill, at the Admiralty, imagined ''land battleships,'' and the tracked Holt tractor, an American farm machine, provided the running gear. The tank was thus a naval-army-farm hybrid before it was a weapon.', '["Trench deadlock demanded a wire-crossing, bulletproof vehicle that infantry tactics could not supply.","The Admiralty''s landship concept plus the Holt caterpillar tractor supplied both the idea and the mechanism.","Industrial capacity. Only Britain and France could mass-produce engines, armour plate, and tracks simultaneously.","Interwar theorists, Fuller, Liddell Hart, Guderian, Tukhachevsky, converted a support weapon into the core of operational doctrine."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1916-09-15","title":"First tank action","description":"Mark I tanks debut at Flers-Courcelette. Few work, all terrify."},{"date":"1917-11-20","title":"Cambrai","description":"476 tanks tear a breach without bombardment, the concept proven at scale."},{"date":"1940-05-13","title":"Sedan","description":"Guderian''s Panzers cross the Meuse and race for the Channel. France falls in weeks."},{"date":"1943-07-12","title":"Prokhorovka","description":"Hundreds of tanks clash at Kursk in history''s largest armoured engagement."}]'::jsonb, '["Trench deadlock broken permanently. No great-power war since has frozen into static lines for years.","Armoured doctrine, combined arms, radio command, operational depth, became the universal grammar of land warfare.","Industrial production decided design. The producible T-34 and Sherman beat the exquisite Tiger.","The tank''s dominance forced every subsequent anti-tank revolution, from shaped charges to attack helicopters to drones."]'::jsonb, '["The first tanks had no suspension worth the name. Crews wore goggles and chain masks against spalling metal, and carbon-monoxide poisoning was routine.","A homing pigeon carried the first tank-attack report from Flers, wireless sets were too bulky for the Mark I.","Germany built exactly twenty A7V tanks in WWI, versus thousands of Allied machines, and still managed history''s first tank-vs-tank clash at Villers-Bretonneux in 1918.","The word ''tank'' stuck so well that Russian and German both borrowed it, while French went its own way with ''char'' (chariot).","T-34s were sometimes driven straight from the Stalingrad factory floor, unpainted, into the battle raging outside."]'::jsonb, 'Every modern army still organizes around the main battle tank, however often its obituary is written, drones in Ukraine are merely the latest challengers. Tank design''s triangle (firepower, protection, mobility, pick two) remains the iron law of armoured engineering.', 'The tank ended static warfare as the default condition of great-power conflict and made speed, Blitzkrieg, Deep Battle, AirLand Battle, the grammar of land war for a century. It also settled the eternal design debate in favour of the balanced, producible machine. The T-34 and Sherman, not the Tiger, won the war of production that underlay the war of manoeuvre.', '/images/plates/tanks.svg', '["Warfare","Engineering","World War I","Armour"]'::jsonb);

The dreadnoughts'' great test came at Jutland on 31 May 1916. 250 ships, the only full-fleet clash of the battleship age. Tactically Germany won, British battlecruisers blew up with shocking ease, but strategically the High Seas Fleet never sortied in force again, and blockade strangled Germany while the fleet mutinied in 1918.

Aircraft ended the argument. At Taranto (1940) and Pearl Harbor (1941) torpedo bombers sank battleships in harbour. At Midway (1942) carriers dueled hundreds of miles apart while battleships escorted. The two largest ever built, Japan''s Yamato and Musashi, were both sunk by aircraft without ever engaging enemy battleships. The last battleship duel, Surigao Strait in October 1944, was an execution of an already-obsolete force. The war ended on a battleship''s deck, Missouri in Tokyo Bay, but carriers had won it.', 'Mahan''s 1890 gospel, sea power decides history, and sea power means battleships, Conrad every admiralty. Tirpitz''s Risk Theory promised Germany that a fleet two-thirds Britain''s size would deter London. Instead it guaranteed British hostility and an arms race neither treasury could sustain.

Dreadnought herself was Jackie Fisher''s gamble. Render Britain''s own vast fleet obsolete in order to leap a generation ahead. It worked technically and failed strategically, Germany simply started its own dreadnought program, and the race ran until 1914.', '["Mahanian doctrine made battleship supremacy the universal measure of naval power.","Tirpitz''s fleet laws committed Germany to a battleship navy explicitly aimed at Britain.","Fisher''s all-big-gun leap reset the competition, forcing everyone to build or accept irrelevance.","Industrial capacity. Only Britain and Germany could afford dreadnought fleets, which is precisely why the race defined their rivalry."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1906-02-10","title":"HMS Dreadnought launched","description":"All-big-gun design resets naval power worldwide."},{"date":"1916-05-31","title":"Jutland","description":"250 ships clash. Germany wins tactically, Britain strategically."},{"date":"1941-05-27","title":"Bismarck sunk","description":"Britain hunts down Germany''s pride after it sinks HMS Hood."},{"date":"1941-12-07","title":"Pearl Harbor","description":"Aircraft sink battleships in harbour. The carrier age is undeniable."},{"date":"1945-04-07","title":"Yamato sunk","description":"The largest battleship ever built dies under waves of carrier aircraft."}]'::jsonb, '["The dreadnought race poisoned Anglo-German relations and helped make 1914 imaginable.","Jutland''s indecisive slaughter entrenched blockade as Britain''s winning weapon, and starved Germany into revolution.","Carriers replaced battleships as the capital ship in every navy within a decade of Pearl Harbor.","Naval arms-control treaties (Washington 1922, London 1930) became the first modern disarmament regimes, built around a dying weapon."]'::jsonb, '["Dreadnought was built in 366 days, a speed that stunned the world and panicked Berlin into matching it.","At Jutland, three British battlecruisers exploded because cordite charges were stacked outside magazines for rapid fire, doctrine, not design, killed the crews.","The Bismarck''s rudder was jammed by a single torpedo from an obsolete Swordfish biplane, aviation''s slowest aircraft crippling naval engineering''s fastest ship.","Yamato''s final mission was effectively a suicide cruise with only enough fuel for one way, Operation Ten-Go expected no survivors.","The US Navy kept battleships in reserve into the 1990s, firing their 16-inch guns in Korea, Vietnam, Lebanon, and the Gulf War."]'::jsonb, 'Museum ships, Missouri, Mikasa, Belfast, and the phrase ''dreadnought'' itself, now meaning anything that obsoletes its predecessors. Naval strategy still argues about the battleship''s heirs. Carriers today face drones and missiles the way dreadnoughts faced the torpedo bomber.', 'The battleship drove the pre-1914 arms race that helped cause one world war, then proved irrelevant to winning the next. Both world wars were decided by blockade, submarines, and carriers. Its forty-year reign is the classic case of a weapons system perfected just as the next one arrives, and of admirals fighting the last war at Jutland while aviators won the current one.', '/images/plates/battleships.svg', '["Naval Warfare","Engineering","Arms Race","Dreadnought"]'::jsonb);

World War II replayed it at higher stakes. Dönitz''s wolfpacks nearly won the Battle of the Atlantic in 1942, sinking ships faster than ever. Then 1943''s Black May. Centimetric radar, Ultra decrypts, long-range aircraft, and Hedgehog mortars suddenly made the ocean transparent, and U-boat losses became unsustainable. Germany lost 781 of 1,150 commissioned U-boats and 28,000 of 40,000 submariners, the highest casualty rate of any service in the war.

After 1945 the submarine escaped the surface entirely. Nuclear propulsion (Nautilus, 1954) meant unlimited range and months submerged. Ballistic missiles (Polaris, first launched submerged in July 1960) meant a retaliatory arsenal no first strike could find. The SSBN became the ultimate deterrent, and remains, under Arctic ice and ocean basins, the least visible and most stable weapon ever built.', 'Before 1914 every navy treated submarines as defensive novelties. Slow, blind, fragile, useful for harbour defence at most. International prize law assumed commerce raiders would stop and search merchants, rules written for surface cruisers that submarines physically could not follow without dying.

Germany, outbuilt on the surface, had the motive to think differently. Britain, importing most of its food and all of its oil, was uniquely vulnerable to a weapon that attacked trade itself rather than fleets.', '["Economic asymmetry. A cheap submarine could sink merchant tonnage worth orders of magnitude more, making blockade-by-sinking affordable for the weaker naval power.","Britain''s import dependence turned trade protection from a secondary task into the decisive campaign of both world wars.","Prize law''s impossibility for submarines pushed Germany, twice, into unrestricted warfare, militarily effective and politically catastrophic.","Nuclear propulsion and ballistic missiles after 1945 converted the submarine from a commerce raider into an undetectable second-strike platform."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1914-09-22","title":"U-9''s triple kill","description":"One U-boat sinks three cruisers in an hour. The submarine age begins."},{"date":"1917-02-01","title":"Unrestricted warfare","description":"Germany sinks everything on sight, and brings America into the war."},{"date":"1943-05-01","title":"Black May","description":"Radar, Ultra, and air cover destroy the U-boat offensive in weeks."},{"date":"1954-01-21","title":"Nautilus launched","description":"Nuclear propulsion frees submarines from the surface forever."},{"date":"1960-07-20","title":"First Polaris launch","description":"Missiles fire from beneath the sea. Deterrence goes invisible."}]'::jsonb, '["Twice nearly starved Britain. Twice failed, and twice the political cost (America''s entry) outweighed the military gain.","Convoy, sonar, radar, and signals intelligence matured into the permanent toolkit of trade protection.","28,000 of 40,000 German submariners died, the costliest service record of the war, and a national trauma.","The SSBN made surprise disarming attack effectively impossible, stabilizing nuclear deterrence ever since."]'::jsonb, '["U-boat crews suffered around 70 percent fatalities in WWII, volunteers to the end, in a service where survival was statistically unlikely.","The convoy system, resisted by the Admiralty as defensive, cut shipping losses catastrophically the moment it was adopted in 1917.","Breaking naval Enigma at Bletchley Park, and the capture of code materials from U-110 in 1941, turned the Atlantic battle into an intelligence war.","Germany''s Type XXI ''electro-boat,'' too late for the war, was the true ancestor of the modern submarine, streamlined, fast submerged, snorkel-equipped.","A ballistic-missile submarine on patrol is among the quietest machines ever built, quieter, at patrol speed, than the surrounding ocean."]'::jsonb, 'Hunter-killer and missile submarines remain the capital ships of every great navy. The Atlantic convoy battles are still the textbook for trade protection. And the SSBN patrol, months of silence under the ice, continues uninterrupted since 1960, the longest continuous military operation in history.', 'The submarine twice came closer than any other weapon to defeating Britain, and twice its misuse (unrestricted warfare, atrocity headlines) helped summon the American power that doomed Germany. Then it transcended its origins. The ballistic-missile submarine underwrites every nuclear peace since 1960, the one weapon whose entire purpose is never to be used.', '/images/plates/submarines.svg', '["Naval Warfare","Cold War","Deterrence","U-boats"]'::jsonb);

The technology escaped immediately. By 1500 presses operated in more than 250 European towns and had produced an estimated twenty million books, more than all the scribes of history combined. Luther''s theses in 1517 spread across Germany in weeks because print shops competed to sell them. Erasmus became Europe''s first media celebrity. Scientific findings could be checked against identical copies hundreds of miles apart.

Print did not merely spread old knowledge faster, it changed what counted as knowledge. Identical diagrams made cumulative science possible, vernacular Bibles broke the clergy''s monopoly on scripture, newspapers created the first simultaneous public, and every authority from popes to kings discovered that censorship could no longer scale.', 'Europe in 1400 was literate at the top and oral below. Perhaps a few million books existed on the entire continent, nearly all in Latin, nearly all owned by clergy and princes. China had printed with woodblocks for centuries, the Diamond Sutra of 868 is the oldest dated printed book, and Korea cast metal type before Gutenberg, but neither system met an alphabetic script and a commercial book market.

Gutenberg''s Mainz offered both. A Rhineland of merchant cities hungry for legal texts, grammars, and indulgences (the Church''s printed get-out-of-purgatory slips were early bestsellers), plus metalworkers who could cut precise, reusable type.', '["An alphabetic script with two dozen characters made reusable type economical, thousands of Chinese characters never could be.","A commercial market. Lawyers, clergy, students, and merchants who would buy identical texts in bulk.","Metalworking skill from goldsmithing and coinage, applied to punch-cutting and type-founding.","Paper mills spreading up the Rhine, cutting material costs just as the press cut labour costs."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1440-01-01","title":"Movable type perfected","description":"Gutenberg combines press, ink, and type."},{"date":"1455-01-01","title":"Gutenberg Bible","description":"The first major printed book appears."},{"date":"1517-10-31","title":"Luther''s theses","description":"Printed copies carry the Reformation across Germany in weeks."},{"date":"1605-01-01","title":"First newspapers","description":"Regular printed news begins in Strasbourg and Antwerp."}]'::jsonb, '["Book production exploded from thousands to tens of millions within fifty years. Prices collapsed and literacy followed.","Religious monopoly broken. Vernacular scripture and pamphlet warfare made the Reformation unstoppable.","Standardized texts made cumulative science possible, identical diagrams, tables, and maps across Europe.","Censorship invented in response. Indexes, imprimaturs, and press controls that every regime has imitated since."]'::jsonb, '["Gutenberg died essentially bankrupt. His financier foreclosed, took the workshop, and finished the Bible business without him.","The Catholic Church was print''s first mass customer, indulgences, printed by the thousand, funded presses before Luther attacked them.","By 1500 Venice alone had printed more books than the rest of Europe combined had owned in manuscript a century earlier.","Korea''s Jikji, printed with metal type in 1377, decades before Gutenberg, survives in Paris, looted during the colonial era.","Erasmus''s books sold hundreds of thousands of copies in his lifetime, the first author famous across borders purely through print."]'::jsonb, 'Every subsequent media panic, novels corrupting youth, newspapers inciting mobs, the internet dissolving truth, replays arguments first made about print. Copyright, censorship law, libraries, and the newspaper habit are all Gutenberg''s grandchildren.', 'The press was the first information revolution. It broke every monopoly on texts, priestly, scholarly, and royal, and created the conditions for Reformation, Scientific Revolution, and Enlightenment alike. Mass literacy, standard languages, and the very idea of public opinion all descend from Mainz in the 1440s.', '/images/plates/printing-press.svg', '["Information","Reformation","Innovation","Printing"]'::jsonb);

On 18 April 1942 sixteen B-25s roared off the USS Hornet, launched early, some 600 miles out, after a Japanese picket boat spotted the task force. They bombed Tokyo, Yokohama, Kobe, and Nagoya in daylight, then ran for China on fumes. Fifteen crashed or ditched. One diverted to the Soviet Union. Of eighty airmen, three died, eight fell into Japanese hands (three of them later executed), and the rest were smuggled through occupied China by Chinese civilians and missionaries.

Material damage was trivial. Strategic effect was enormous. Japan''s high command, humiliated that the homeland could be bombed, pulled fighter squadrons home for defence and resolved to destroy the American carriers, the decision that led straight to the Midway trap six weeks later.', 'American strategy in early 1942 was pure defence. The Atlantic lifeline, Australia''s shield, and time for industry to mobilize. Roosevelt demanded, in his words, that Japan be bombed, partly for revenge, partly to reassure allies, mostly to prove the war could be taken to the enemy.

The technical problem was range. No American bomber could reach Japan from any available base. The carrier Hornet, commissioned only months earlier, plus Doolittle''s insight that B-25s could lift off a deck at the cost of never landing on one, turned impossibility into a one-way mission volunteers lined up for.', '["Political necessity. Four months of unbroken defeat demanded a visible blow for American and Allied morale.","Roosevelt''s personal insistence on bombing Japan, overruling planners who called it premature.","Doolittle''s unique combination, stunt pilot, MIT-trained engineer, and leader men would follow on a suicide-adjacent mission.","Japanese overconfidence. The home islands were essentially undefended, with air defence an afterthought."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1942-04-18","title":"Early launch","description":"Spotted by a picket boat, Hornet launches 600 miles out, the margin for China shrinks to fumes."},{"date":"1942-04-18","title":"Tokyo bombed","description":"Sixteen bombers strike five cities in daylight and run west."},{"date":"1942-04-18","title":"China coast","description":"Out of fuel at night in storms. Crashes, bailouts, and one diversion to the USSR."},{"date":"1942-04-19","title":"Rescue","description":"Chinese civilians hide and smuggle the surviving crews past Japanese patrols."}]'::jsonb, '["Japan pulled home defences and resolved to destroy the US carriers, the decision chain to Midway.","American morale transformed. The raid proved the homeland reachable and the war winnable.","Japanese retaliation in Zhejiang and Jiangxi killed an estimated quarter of a million Chinese civilians.","Carrier aviation''s prestige soared. The raid''s success accelerated the Pacific''s shift to carrier warfare."]'::jsonb, '["Doolittle himself nearly didn''t go. He expected demotion for the early launch''s fuel cost, and instead received the Medal of Honor and a promotion to brigadier general.","The crews trained for weeks at Eglin Field taking off in chalk-marked carrier lengths, with Doolittle personally demonstrating every takeoff.","One crew, out of fuel, landed intact at Vladivostok. The Soviets interned them, then quietly let them ''escape'' into Iran a year later.","Three captured airmen were executed by the Japanese in October 1942. A fourth died of disease in captivity.","The task force commander, Admiral Halsey, offered Hornet''s deck with the signal. To the raiders'' luck, then watched sixteen bombers vanish westward."]'::jsonb, 'The Hornet''s deck launch remains the archetype of the do-the-impossible mission, invoked for every audacious air operation since. In China the raid is remembered darker. Japanese retaliation against the regions that sheltered Americans killed an estimated quarter of a million civilians, a cost rarely counted in the legend.', 'The raid''s military scorecard reads almost zero, a few dozen casualties, minor damage, and its strategic ledger reads Midway. It forced Japan onto the offensive at the time and place of America''s choosing. It also established the pattern of American airpower as morale weapon, and Doolittle''s Medal of Honor made him the war''s most famous airman.', '/images/plates/doolittle-raid.svg', '["World War II","Pacific War","Daring Raid","Aviation"]'::jsonb);

There was no crater, no meteorite fragments worth speaking of, and no expedition for nearly twenty years. The Tsar fell, civil war followed, and the forest stood in roadless wilderness. When Leonid Kulik finally reached the site in 1927, he found exactly what the Evenki witnesses described, a devastated forest with trees still standing, stripped and branchless, at the very epicentre.

Modern consensus reads the evidence as a stony asteroid some 50 metres across bursting 5–10 kilometres up, releasing roughly 10–15 megatons, the largest impact event in recorded history, and one that hit, by pure luck, the emptiest forest on earth rather than a city.', 'Tunguska fell in a scientific blind spot. In 1908 no one understood airbursts. Meteors were supposed to hit the ground and leave craters and iron. Russian science was thin on the ground in Siberia, and within a decade revolution and civil war buried the event in archives. Kulik, a meteorite enthusiast, spent the 1920s lobbying a Soviet state that eventually funded him as much forńorthern development as for science.

Abroad, the event barely registered. A few observatory notes about bright Arctic nights (dust in the upper atmosphere), then obscurity until science-fiction writers, and later, Cold War nuclear physicists who recognized the blast pattern, revived it.', '["Orbital mechanics. Earth crosses cometary and asteroidal debris streams constantly. Tunguska was a routine intersection with an unlucky rock.","A stony (rather than iron) body, which disintegrated under atmospheric stress instead of surviving to the ground.","Entry angle and speed that converted kinetic energy into a high-altitude fireball rather than a ground impact.","Geographic luck. The same burst over London, St Petersburg, or Tokyo would have been the deadliest single day in human history."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1908-06-30","title":"The fireball","description":"Witnesses see the sky split 60 miles away. Trees fall radially across 2,000 square kilometres."},{"date":"1908-07-01","title":"Bright nights","description":"Dust in the upper atmosphere lights Arctic skies from London to Tashkent."},{"date":"1927-01-01","title":"First expedition","description":"Kulik finds flattened forest, standing dead trunks at ground zero, and no crater."},{"date":"2013-02-15","title":"Chelyabinsk","description":"A smaller airburst over a Russian city injures 1,500, Tunguska''s warning, miniatured."}]'::jsonb, '["The founding case for planetary defence. Proof that major impacts arrive without craters or warnings.","A century of fringe theories, Tesla rays to alien ships, each eventually collapsed by ordinary evidence.","Modern asteroid surveys and deflection missions (including 2022''s DART) trace their rationale to Tunguska.","UN Asteroid Day on 30 June keeps the warning on the calendar."]'::jsonb, '["Semen Semyonov, 60 kilometres away, was blown off his porch by heat and blast, his 1920s testimony remains the classic eyewitness account.","Trees at the exact epicentre stayed standing, stripped of branches, the blast came from directly above, snapping limbs downward rather than sideways.","Lake Cheko, a few kilometres off, is either an impact fragment crater or an old oxbow lake, expeditions still argue, with sediment cores inconclusive.","The 2013 Chelyabinsk airburst, same physics, smaller rock, populated target, injured some 1,500 people mostly with flying glass.","Kulik died in a Nazi POW camp in 1942, still petitioning for one more Tunguska expedition to find the meteorite that was never there."]'::jsonb, 'Asteroid Day (30 June, UN-recognized) commemorates Tunguska annually. The event anchors every risk calculation about near-Earth objects, and every reminder that the map of impacts is drawn by luck, with cities occupying most of the paper.', 'Tunguska is the case study for planetary defence. Proof that civilization-ending-scale impacts need no crater, no warning, and no permission. Every asteroid survey, every deflection mission (including NASA''s 2022 DART impact), descends intellectually from that Siberian morning. It also fixed the template for a century of fringe theories, Tesla death rays, black holes, alien spacecraft, each collapsed by the patient accumulation of ordinary evidence.', '/images/plates/tunguska-event.svg', '["Mystery","Siberia","Astronomy","Impact Event"]'::jsonb);

Supplies ran short almost immediately, and White sailed back to England for relief, the worst possible timing. England mobilized against the Spanish Armada, every seaworthy ship was embargoed, and three years passed before White could return. When he finally landed on 18 August 1590, the settlement was empty. Houses dismantled, not destroyed. No graves, no sign of battle. And carved on a palisade post, the single word CROATOAN, plus CRO on a tree. The colonists had agreed that a distress message would include a cross. There was none.

Storms prevented searching Croatoan Island itself (modern Hatteras, home of the friendly Croatoan tribe), and White never returned. Four centuries of archaeology, DNA testing, and legend, including the Dare Stones, a sensational 1930s–40s hoax, have produced hypotheses but no verdict. Assimilation with local tribes, massacre, starvation and dispersal, or some compound of all three.', 'Elizabethan England was late to empire. Spain had been looting the Americas for nearly a century while England managed one failed Newfoundland claim. Raleigh''s Roanoke ventures (1584 scouting, 1585 military colony that ate its welcome and fled) were speculative plays for a privateering base and, hopefully, gold.

The 1587 colony differed. Families, not soldiers, an attempt at permanence. But it sailed into a deteriorating relationship with the Algonquian peoples, a pilot who refused to take them to their intended Chesapeake site, and a supply chain exactly one ocean wide.', '["A fragile site choice. Roanoke''s shallow sounds starved shipping while the settlers alienated the mainland tribes who controlled food.","Supply dependence on a single patron (Raleigh) and a single ocean crossing, both hostage to the Spanish war.","The Armada crisis. White''s relief was delayed three years by England''s mobilization, the exact window in which a struggling colony would fail.","Deteriorating native relations. The 1585 colony had killed the chief Wingina, poisoning the ground the 1587 families inherited."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1587-08-01","title":"Colony founded","description":"115 settlers land on Roanoke Island."},{"date":"1587-08-18","title":"Virginia Dare born","description":"First English child born in the Americas."},{"date":"1587-08-27","title":"White sails for help","description":"Leaves for England. War with Spain strands him for three years."},{"date":"1590-08-18","title":"The empty fort","description":"White finds CROATOAN carved on a palisade, and no colonists."},{"date":"1937-01-01","title":"Dare Stones surface","description":"Carved stones purporting to tell the colonists'' fate. Exposed as mostly fraud."}]'::jsonb, '["England''s colonization paused for nearly twenty years. Jamestown (1607) was planned with Roanoke''s failure explicitly in mind.","The mystery became America''s oldest cold case, generating four centuries of archaeology, fraud, and fiction.","Algonquian-European contact history lost its earliest chapter, whatever happened at Roanoke happened off the record.","Modern Hatteras excavations continue, making this a live investigation rather than a closed file."]'::jsonb, '["The colonists'' agreed distress symbol was a Maltese cross carved above any message, its absence is the strongest evidence the departure was orderly, not panicked.","White''s own maps, re-examined with modern imaging, show a hidden fort symbol under a patch at the mainland site now called Site X.","The Dare Stones, 48 inscribed rocks ''telling'' Eleanor Dare''s story, fooled scholars for years. Most are now considered the work of a Georgia stonecutter.","Algonquian oral traditions recorded centuries later describe assimilation of Europeans into coastal tribes, unprovable, but consistent with the evidence.","Hatteras Island digs have found English gun hardware, sword hilts, and pottery contemporary with the colony, the most promising lead in four centuries."]'::jsonb, 'Virginia Dare as folk heroine (statues, festivals, a county named Dare). CROATOAN in horror fiction from Harlan Ellison to American Horror Story. And a permanent caution that colonization''s archive preserves the organizers'' paper, never the disappeared.', 'Roanoke is America''s founding mystery. The first English attempt at a permanent New World settlement, vanished so completely that Jamestown''s founders (1607) arrived knowing only a carved word. It set the template for every lost-colony story since and remains the active archaeological question of early American history, excavations on Hatteras continue to turn up contemporary English material.', '/images/plates/roanoke-lost-colony.svg', '["Mystery","Colonization","Unsolved","1587"]'::jsonb);

Theodore Roosevelt picked up the pieces with gunboats and lawyers. Backing Panama''s secession from Colombia in 1903, taking the Canal Zone in perpetuity, and handing the project to engineers who understood the real enemy was the mosquito. William Gorgas''s sanitation campaign, draining, screening, fumigating, killed yellow fever in the Zone. John Stevens solved the digging with railroads and a lock design. George Goethals drove it to completion.

The SS Ancon made the first official transit on 15 August 1914, the same month Europe went to war, which is why almost nobody noticed. Ships now climb 26 metres through the Gatun locks, cross an artificial lake, and descend to the Pacific. Roughly 8,000 miles saved, and the United States became a two-ocean naval power.', 'Panama in the 1800s was a Colombian backwater crossed by fortune-seekers. The California gold rush sent tens of thousands overland, and a US-built railway (1855) proved the route''s value while killing thousands of its own builders. Suez''s 1869 opening made a Central American canal seem inevitable, de Lesseps simply assumed Panama''s mountains and rivers would yield as Egypt''s sand had.

They did not. The Chagres River flooded catastrophically, the cut at Culebra slid endlessly, and tropical disease, whose mosquito vector no one yet understood, killed faster than the company could recruit. When the money ran out in 1889, 800,000 French small investors were ruined and the word ''Panama'' entered French as shorthand for scandal.', '["Geography and gold. The Cape Horn route''s 13,000 miles made any shortcut worth a fortune in freight and naval strategy.","De Lesseps''s prestige after Suez, which raised millions on reputation and then applied a sea-level design to mountains.","Disease ecology. Panama''s mosquitoes killed 22,000 French workers because germ theory had not yet reached tropical medicine.","American naval strategy. Mahan''s doctrine demanded a two-ocean fleet, and only a canal could shuttle battleships between coasts."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1881-01-01","title":"French works begin","description":"De Lesseps breaks ground. Disease begins killing faster than digging advances."},{"date":"1889-01-01","title":"French collapse","description":"De Lesseps'' company goes bankrupt."},{"date":"1903-11-03","title":"Panama secedes","description":"US-backed independence. The Hay–Bunau-Varilla Treaty grants the Zone."},{"date":"1904-05-04","title":"Americans take over","description":"Gorgas begins the sanitation campaign. Stevens switches to a lock design."},{"date":"1914-08-15","title":"Canal opens","description":"The SS Ancon makes the first transit."}]'::jsonb, '["Some 8,000 miles saved between Atlantic and Pacific ports. World shipping patterns permanently altered.","US two-ocean naval power became possible, the strategic fact behind a century of American Pacific dominance.","Panama''s existence as a state, the Zone''s century of US control, and a long shadow of interventionism.","Tropical medicine proven as engineering. Sanitation campaigns worldwide copied Gorgas''s methods."]'::jsonb, '["The French effort''s death toll, around 22,000, mostly disease, exceeded the American toll (~5,600) several times over.","Gorgas''s men oiled or drained every stand of water in the Zone weekly. Mosquito brigades inspected homes with fines for larvae found.","The Culebra Cut slides were so relentless that excavated spoil areas are still visible as artificial hills flanking the canal.","Ancon''s first transit carried no fanfare. With Europe at war, the opening went nearly unreported worldwide.","The canal''s water comes entirely from rainfall into Gatun Lake, droughts, worsening with climate change, now restrict transits."]'::jsonb, 'Panama took full control in 1999 under the Torrijos-Carter treaties and runs the canal as the core of its economy. The 2016 expansion added Neo-Panamax locks for ships the original builders could not have imagined. The French failure remains the classic case study in confusing one environment''s solution for another''s.', 'The canal halved the maritime world for the Americas, made US two-ocean naval power possible, and demonstrated, after Gorgas, that tropical disease was an engineering problem with an engineering solution. It also set the template for a century of American intervention in Latin America. The Zone, the treaties, and the 1989 invasion all descend from Roosevelt''s 1903 bargain.', '/images/plates/panama-canal.svg', '["Engineering","Americas","Ambition","Canal"]'::jsonb);

What followed was not one war but a system of rivalry. NATO against the Warsaw Pact, nuclear arsenals growing from a handful of bombs to some 70,000 warheads, and proxy wars from Korea to Angola where the superpowers armed clients rather than meeting in battle. Crises, Berlin in 1948 and 1961, Korea in 1950, Cuba in 1962, repeatedly brought the world near catastrophe, then settled into deterrence. Each side too destructive to attack, too distrustful to disarm.

The contest ended almost as abruptly as it began. Mikhail Gorbachev''s reforms, meant to save Soviet socialism, unraveled its authority. Eastern Europe broke away in 1989, the Baltic republics left, and on 26 December 1991 the Soviet flag came down over the Kremlin. The United States stood alone as a superpower, and the institutions built for the standoff, NATO, the EU''s precursors, the nuclear arsenals, outlived the enemy they were built against.', 'The roots ran through Yalta and Potsdam in 1945, where Roosevelt, Churchill, and Stalin divided Germany into occupation zones and Poland into a Soviet sphere in exchange for promises of free elections that Moscow never kept. By 1946 Kennan''s Long Telegram from Moscow and Churchill''s Iron Curtain speech at Fulton had framed the conflict for Washington. The Soviet Union, they argued, understood only force.

Economics hardened the split. The Marshall Plan of 1948 rebuilt Western Europe as a market for American goods. The Soviets answered with Comecon and sealed their zone. Germany became two states in 1949, China went communist the same year, and the Soviet atomic test ended the American monopoly, containment now meant a global, permanent, nuclear-armed posture.', '["The power vacuum of 1945. With Germany, Japan, Britain, and France broken, only Washington and Moscow could order their regions, and their orders collided in Germany, Poland, and the Middle East.","Ideological incompatibility elevated to state doctrine, American liberal capitalism against Soviet Marxism-Leninism, each claiming universal truth and the other''s eventual collapse.","The security dilemma of the atomic age. Each side''s defensive moves, from bases to bombs, read in the other capital as preparation for attack, driving an arms race neither fully controlled.","The division of Germany and Berlin into occupation zones that turned every local dispute into a superpower test of resolve."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1948-06-24","title":"Berlin Blockade","description":"Stalin seals West Berlin. The Allied airlift flies 278,000 sorties to keep two million people alive."},{"date":"1950-06-25","title":"Korean War begins","description":"North Korea invades the South. America intervenes under UN cover and China counter-intervenes, three years, millions dead, borders unchanged."},{"date":"1962-10-16","title":"Cuban Missile Crisis","description":"Thirteen days when Soviet missiles in Cuba brought the world closer to nuclear war than ever before or since."},{"date":"1968-08-20","title":"Prague Spring crushed","description":"Soviet tanks end Czechoslovak reform, codifying the Brezhnev Doctrine that Moscow would invade its own allies."},{"date":"1989-11-09","title":"Berlin Wall falls","description":"East Germans stream west after a bungled announcement. The Eastern bloc unravels within weeks."},{"date":"1991-12-26","title":"USSR dissolved","description":"The Supreme Soviet votes itself out of existence. Fifteen republics become independent states."}]'::jsonb, '["Germany and Korea permanently divided. Europe split by an Iron Curtain of walls, minefields, and rival armies.","Nuclear arsenals peaking near 70,000 warheads, with deterrence doctrine and arms-control treaties as permanent fixtures.","Decolonization accelerated as Washington and Moscow bid for new states with aid, arms, and ideology.","Proxy wars from Vietnam to Afghanistan killed millions while the superpowers themselves never exchanged a shot."]'::jsonb, '["The hotline between Washington and Moscow was a teletype, not a red telephone, voice was considered too emotional for nuclear messaging.","In 1983 a Soviet officer, Stanislav Petrov, judged a satellite warning of US launches a false alarm and refused to report it upward, likely preventing retaliation.","The US and USSR played each other in the 1972 Olympic basketball final decided on a replayed final three seconds, the Soviets won and America refused the silver.","West Berlin survived partly on a single air corridor''s chocolate. Pilot Gail Halvorsen dropped candy on handkerchief parachutes to children below.","Both sides seriously planned to fight on the Moon. The US Army''s Project Horizon studied a lunar military base in 1959."]'::jsonb, 'NATO''s eastward expansion, Russia''s grievance narrative, Korea''s division, and the nuclear arsenals all descend directly from this standoff. Every debate about deterrence, containment, or decoupling still borrows its vocabulary.', 'The Cold War invented the modern world order. Bipolar deterrence, permanent alliances, proxy war as standard practice, and the idea that great powers compete everywhere at once, in space, in sport, in aid budgets. It normalized the nuclear shadow over daily life and built the institutions, from NATO to the non-proliferation regime, that still structure security.', '/images/plates/cold-war.svg', '["Bipolar World","Nuclear Age","Proxy Wars","NATO"]'::jsonb);

What followed compressed a century of politics into ten years. The Bastille fell on 14 July 1789. Feudal dues were abolished overnight on 4 August. The Declaration of the Rights of Man proclaimed liberty and legal equality. Then the revolution radicalized. War with Austria and Prussia in 1792, the monarchy''s fall, the Republic, and the Terror of 1793–94, when Robespierre''s Committee of Public Safety guillotined some 17,000 people, nobles, priests, and in the end revolutionaries themselves, including Danton and then Robespierre.

Exhaustion produced the Directory, a corrupt five-man regime that survived on army contracts until a 30-year-old general, Napoleon Bonaparte, overthrew it on 9 November 1799. The Bourbons would return, but never the old order. Equality before the law, careers open to talent, the metric system, and the Napoleonic Code all descend from this decade.', 'The Enlightenment had already put the monarchy on trial in print. Voltaire mocked clerical privilege, Rousseau argued sovereignty lay with the people, and American independence proved a king could be beaten. France paid for that American war with loans it could not service, interest alone consumed half the royal budget by 1788.

Below the ideas sat hunger. Bad harvests in 1787 and 1788 sent bread prices soaring while wages stagnated. When hail ruined the 1788 harvest, Paris filled with unemployed men who could be hired for a riot or a revolution, and the nobility''s attempt to protect its tax exemptions in the Assembly of Notables destroyed the last illusion that reform would come from above.', '["Fiscal collapse. Debt service consumed around half of state revenue, and the privileged orders blocked every attempt to tax land and income fairly.","Enlightenment ideas plus the American example, which gave French officers and readers a working model of a republic founded on rights.","Subsistence crisis in 1787–89 that turned political grievance into street power in Paris.","Louis XVI''s indecision, summoning the Estates-General, then threatening troops, then recalling them, which convinced the Third Estate that force alone would decide."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1789-07-14","title":"Storming of the Bastille","description":"Paris crowds seize the royal fortress-prison. Its governor is lynched and the revolution has its founding date."},{"date":"1789-08-26","title":"Rights of Man","description":"The Assembly proclaims men free and equal in rights, press, property, and law for (male) citizens."},{"date":"1792-09-20","title":"Valmy and the Republic","description":"Citizen-soldiers stop the Prussian advance. The monarchy is abolished the next day."},{"date":"1793-01-21","title":"Louis XVI executed","description":"The king dies under the guillotine in the Place de la Révolution."},{"date":"1794-07-28","title":"Robespierre falls","description":"The Terror''s architect is guillotined without trial. The Terror ends with him."},{"date":"1799-11-09","title":"Brumaire coup","description":"Bonaparte overthrows the Directory and makes himself First Consul."}]'::jsonb, '["Feudal dues, venal offices, and clerical privilege abolished. Equality before the law proclaimed across France.","A citizen army of mass conscription that defeated the old regime''s professionals and redrew Europe''s map.","Some 17,000 guillotined in the Terror plus tens of thousands dead in the Vendée and federalist revolts.","The Napoleonic Code and centralized departments, which outlived every regime that followed."]'::jsonb, '["The Bastille held only seven prisoners when it fell, its gunpowder stores mattered more than its cells.","The revolutionary calendar renamed months after weather and crops, Thermidor, harvest-heat, and added ten-day weeks.","Women marched Versailles in October 1789 and forced the royal family back to Paris, arguably the revolution''s decisive day.","The guillotine was promoted as humanitarian. A quick, equal death replacing the executioner''s repertoire of tortures.","France''s metric system was defined during the Terror, the metre as one ten-millionth of the Paris meridian."]'::jsonb, 'The tricolour, the Marseillaise, Bastille Day, and the Napoleonic Code all survive. So does the darker lesson both left and right still quote. Revolutions devour their moderates first, and emergency powers outlive the emergency.', 'The revolution invented modern politics. Left and right, the nation in arms, universal male suffrage as an aspiration, and the idea that legitimacy flows from citizens rather than God or custom. Its code, calendar, weights, and administrative map reorganized France permanently, and its armies carried, and discredited, those ideas across Europe.', '/images/plates/french-revolution.svg', '["Revolution","Republic","Terror","Rights of Man"]'::jsonb);

Medieval medicine had no answer. Doctors in beaked masks prescribed prayer, flight, and foul air dispersed by bonfires. Towns sealed gates and still died. Modern DNA from plague pits confirms the killer as Yersinia pestis, carried by rat fleas and probably spreading pneumonically in its final form. Mortality ran around a third of Europe, twenty to twenty-five million dead, with some villages losing four in five.

The dying stopped by 1352, but plague returned roughly every decade for three centuries. The first wave''s scale broke the labour market permanently. Fields stood unharvested, rents collapsed, and surviving peasants could demand wages that custom had forbidden. The old order never quite reassembled.', 'Europe around 1300 was overfull. Population near its medieval peak, marginal land under plough, and cities dependent on grain shipped long distances. The Great Famine of 1315–17 had already killed millions and weakened resistance. Meanwhile Mongol peace stitched Asia to the Black Sea in one trading system, and Italian merchants at Caffa sat at its western tap.

The standard account, besiegers catapulting corpses into Caffa in 1346, refugees carrying infection west, captures the mechanism even where details are debated. The plague rode established grain and fur routes, and Europe''s crowded, malnourished towns gave it perfect fuel.', '["An interconnected Eurasian trade network that moved goods, rats, and fleas from Central Asia to the Mediterranean in weeks.","Dense, malnourished urban populations living alongside grain stores, livestock, and poor sanitation.","A pathogen, Yersinia pestis, to which Europeans had no acquired immunity, spreading by flea bite and likely by breath.","Flight that carried infection. Towns expelled strangers, and strangers carried the bacillus to the next town."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1347-10-01","title":"Messina landfall","description":"Plague ships reach Sicily. The contagion is ashore in Europe."},{"date":"1348-03-01","title":"Italy overwhelmed","description":"Florence loses perhaps half its people. Boccaccio''s Decameron opens with the dying."},{"date":"1348-08-01","title":"Avignon and Paris","description":"Papal and royal capitals stricken. Clement VI buys riverside land for plague pits."},{"date":"1349-02-14","title":"Strasbourg pogrom","description":"Hundreds of Jews burned on accusations of well-poisoning, scapegoating across the Rhineland."},{"date":"1350-01-01","title":"Northern peak","description":"Scandinavia, Scotland, and Russia suffer their worst mortality as the wave completes its circuit."}]'::jsonb, '["A third of Europe dead, twenty million or more, with villages abandoned and fields reverting to waste.","Labour scarcity that raised wages and eroded villeinage in Western Europe.","Pogroms that destroyed hundreds of Jewish communities on false poisoning charges.","Public-health machinery, quarantine, isolation hospitals, burial registration, invented under pressure."]'::jsonb, '["The name Black Death is modern, contemporaries called it the Great Mortality or Pestilence.","Iceland was largely spared the first wave for lack of rats and ships, then devastated by later introductions.","Pope Clement VI survived by sitting between two bonfires, on doctors'' advice that heat dispersed corrupted air.","English wages legislation, the 1351 Statute of Labourers, tried to freeze pay at pre-plague levels and failed for a century.","Plague pits under London still yield Yersinia DNA that matches Central Asian strains, confirming the eastern origin."]'::jsonb, 'Quarantine, forty days'' detention from the Venetian quarante giorni, plus lazarettos, plague orders, and parish burial registers all descend from these years. The art of the Danse Macabre and a lasting European association of rats, Jews-baiting shame, and mass pits trace here too.', 'No event killed faster at continental scale before or since. By halving the workforce it raised wages, broke serfdom''s economics in the west, and forced landlords toward leases and cash, the demographic hinge between medieval and early modern England and France.', '/images/plates/black-death.svg', '["Plague","Medieval","Demography","Pandemic"]'::jsonb);

Three interlocking shifts drove it. Abraham Darby''s coke smelting freed iron from charcoal. James Watt''s separate-condenser engine (patented 1769) turned steam from a mine pump into a universal motor. And the factory system, Arkwright''s Cromford mill of 1771 first, gathered spinners, power, and discipline under one roof and one clock. Canals cut freight costs, then Stephenson''s locomotives cut them again.

The price was a new world of shift bells, child labour, smoke, and slums, and unprecedented wealth. Real wages stagnated for decades, then rose. Population doubled. And every industrializing country after Britain copied, adapted, or rebelled against this template.', 'Britain had unusual advantages. Cheap coal near navigable water, a patent system that rewarded tinkerers, interest rates low enough to fund canals, and a navy that secured cotton from America and markets in India. Enclosure had already pushed villagers toward wage labour, and a culture of dissenting chapels and coffeehouses spread technical literacy.

Science helped less than legend claims, millwrights and instrument-makers outran theorists, but the Royal Society''s ethos of publishing and improving gave mechanics status. When French wars after 1793 closed continental competition, British manufacturers enjoyed a captive empire market at exactly the moment steam matured.', '["Coal geology. Shallow, coastal seams that made heat and iron cheap beside ship-ready ports.","Cotton demand that outran hand spinning, creating a premium for any machine that multiplied thread.","Capital and credit from trade and land that could fund canals, mills, and engines with years-long paybacks.","A legal culture of patents and partnerships that let men like Boulton and Watt scale an invention into an industry."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1769-01-05","title":"Watt''s patent","description":"The separate condenser makes steam efficient enough to power mills anywhere."},{"date":"1771-01-01","title":"Cromford mill","description":"Arkwright''s water-powered factory sets the template. Machines, shifts, and supervision."},{"date":"1804-02-21","title":"First locomotive","description":"Trevithick''s engine hauls iron at Penydarren, slow, but proof rails could replace horses."},{"date":"1825-09-27","title":"Stockton and Darlington","description":"The first public steam railway opens. Stephenson''s Locomotion No. 1 leads the way."},{"date":"1833-08-29","title":"Factory Act","description":"Parliament limits child labour after decades of agitation, the state enters the mill."}]'::jsonb, '["Factory towns of hundreds of thousands where villages had stood, with slums, cholera, and shift discipline.","Britain as workshop of the world, cotton, iron, and coal financing empire and naval supremacy.","A working class with unions, chapels, and newspapers demanding votes, schooling, and shorter hours.","Fossil-fuel dependence and urban smoke that inaugurated the modern environmental ledger."]'::jsonb, '["Manchester was nicknamed Cottonopolis, by 1860 it handled most of the world''s cotton trade.","Luddite frame-breaking (1811–16) targeted specific machines and owners, not technology in general, and ended with hangings at York.","Gas lighting, installed in mills before streets, extended the working day before it beautified the city.","The first inter-city railway timetable forced Britain onto a single railway time, the origin of standard time.","Engels researched The Condition of the Working Class in Manchester while managing his family''s mill there."]'::jsonb, 'Railways, steamships, machine tools, and joint-stock industry spread the model worldwide within a generation. The environmental and labour questions first posed in Manchester, smoke, hours, housing, remain the template for every industrial debate since.', 'The first sustained rise in output per person in human history. Everything modern, cities of millions, fossil-fuel energy, wage labour as the norm, and the expectation that children live differently from parents, starts in these Lancashire mills.', '/images/plates/industrial-revolution.svg', '["Steam","Factories","Railways","Coal"]'::jsonb);

The descent nearly aborted. A 1202 program alarm, a boulder field at the target site, and fuel running toward bingo. Armstrong took manual control and set Eagle down with an estimated 25 to 45 seconds of usable fuel left by the gauges of the day. Six hours later he descended the ladder and spoke the line a quarter of humanity would hear live. One small step for man. Aldrin followed. Together they planted the flag, deployed experiments, and gathered 21.5 kilograms of Moon rock in two and a half hours outside.

Splashdown came on 24 July in the Pacific. The crew quarantined, the rocks quarantined with them, and the Cold War''s great technological wager was settled. The nation that had trailed in space in 1957 had put men on the Moon before the decade''s end.', 'Apollo was Kennedy''s 1961 answer to Gagarin. A goal so hard it would organize American science itself. NASA''s budget peaked near 4.4 percent of federal spending. Some 400,000 people worked on Apollo across the country. The Saturn V, the lunar-orbit rendezvous method, and the integrated circuit, bought by Apollo in volumes that created the chip industry, all matured under that deadline.

The Soviets, who led early with Sputnik and Gagarin, lost the Moon on organization. Competing design bureaus, the N1 rocket''s four consecutive failures, and Korolev''s death in 1966. By 1968 Apollo 8''s Christmas Eve orbit of the Moon made the American landing a matter of when.', '["Cold War prestige competition after Sputnik (1957) and Gagarin (1961) convinced Washington that space leadership equalled ideological superiority.","Kennedy''s 1961 deadline, which converted an open research program into a scheduled engineering project with unlimited priority.","The lunar-orbit rendezvous decision, which let one small lander do the job instead of landing an entire battleship on the Moon.","Industrial scale. North American, Grumman, and thousands of subcontractors coordinated by NASA''s systems management."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1969-07-16","title":"Launch","description":"Saturn V AS-506 lifts Columbia and Eagle from Pad 39A."},{"date":"1969-07-19","title":"Lunar orbit","description":"Service propulsion burn puts the stack behind the Moon."},{"date":"1969-07-20","title":"Touchdown","description":"Eagle lands in Tranquility at 20:17 UTC with fuel nearly gone."},{"date":"1969-07-21","title":"First steps","description":"Armstrong then Aldrin walk 2.5 hours. Flag, laser reflector, and seismometer deployed."},{"date":"1969-07-24","title":"Splashdown","description":"Columbia lands 1,400 km southwest of Hawaii. Carriers recover the crew."}]'::jsonb, '["America''s Cold War prestige victory, watched by an estimated 600 million people.","A semiconductor and software push, Apollo bought the early integrated circuit industry into existence.","Planetary science founded on 382 kg of Moon rock returned across six landings.","A human-spaceflight ceiling unbroken since 1972, making Apollo both triumph and reproach."]'::jsonb, '["The 1202 alarm meant the guidance computer was overloaded, engineer Jack Garman in Houston called it go in seconds.","Armstrong''s famous line was heard as ''for man'' without the ''a''. He always insisted he said ''for a man''.","Aldrin took communion on the surface from a kit packed by his pastor, the first food eaten on the Moon.","The flag''s ripple comes from a horizontal rod. There is no wind to wave it.","Collins, alone behind the Moon, called himself the loneliest man in history, out of radio contact for 48 minutes each orbit."]'::jsonb, 'No human has left low Earth orbit since 1972, which is why Apollo still defines ambition. Its hardware museum pieces, its code on GitHub, and its management methods in every aerospace firm keep the template alive for Artemis.', 'The only crewed landing on another world, and the high-water mark of state-directed technology. It proved systems engineering could do the unprecedented on schedule, seeded the semiconductor and software industries, and fixed Earth''s fragility in mind, the Earthrise and Blue Marble images date from this program.', '/images/plates/apollo-11.svg', '["Spaceflight","Cold War","Engineering","Moon"]'::jsonb);

For thirteen days the Executive Committee of the National Security Council debated in secret while the world learned only fragments. Kennedy rejected immediate airstrikes, which could not guarantee every missile, and on 22 October announced a naval quarantine of Cuba, putting 180 ships to sea and raising Strategic Air Command to DEFCON 2, one step from war. Soviet ships approached the line, turned, or were boarded. A U-2 was shot down over Cuba on 27 October, Black Saturday, while another strayed over Siberia.

The settlement was a trade conducted half in public, half in secret. Khrushchev withdrew the missiles under UN inspection in exchange for an American pledge never to invade Cuba, plus a confidential promise, kept for 25 years, to remove Jupiter missiles from Turkey. No other crisis has carried decision-makers so close to general nuclear war by their own later admission.', 'Cuba had radicalized fast. Castro''s 1959 revolution, the 1960 nationalizations, the failed CIA-backed Bay of Pigs landing of April 1961, and the 1962 expulsion from the Organization of American States. Khrushchev, embarrassed by the Berlin Wall''s admission of failure and pressed by hardliners, saw Cuban missiles as cheap leverage, American Jupiters already sat in Turkey and Italy within range of Soviet cities.

Intelligence ran both ways and badly. Washington underestimated the Soviet garrison''s size and its delegated authority to use tactical nuclear weapons if invaded. Moscow underestimated Kennedy''s domestic need, weeks before midterms, to look unyielding.', '["The Bay of Pigs convinced both Havana and Moscow that another American invasion was coming and that only Soviet weapons would deter it.","A lopsided strategic balance, overwhelming American superiority in long-range missiles, that made Khrushchev desperate for a shortcut to parity.","Jupiter missiles in Turkey that gave Moscow a grievance of encirclement to answer in kind.","Brinkmanship as doctrine on both sides. Each leader believed showing fear would invite the next probe, from Berlin to Southeast Asia."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1962-10-14","title":"U-2 photographs","description":"Major Richard Heyser''s mission returns with unmistakable missile-transporter and launch-pad images."},{"date":"1962-10-22","title":"Quarantine speech","description":"Kennedy addresses the nation, announces the naval quarantine, and puts forces worldwide on alert."},{"date":"1962-10-24","title":"The line holds","description":"Soviet ships stop or turn before the quarantine arc. The tanker Bucharest is allowed through."},{"date":"1962-10-27","title":"Black Saturday","description":"A U-2 is shot down over Cuba. Another strays over the USSR. A Soviet submarine nearly fires a nuclear torpedo."},{"date":"1962-10-28","title":"Khrushchev backs down","description":"Moscow Radio broadcasts withdrawal. Missiles leave under inspection through November."}]'::jsonb, '["Soviet missiles withdrawn under inspection. American Jupiters quietly removed from Turkey months later.","A permanent US pledge not to invade Cuba, which secured the revolution''s survival.","The hotline, the Limited Test Ban Treaty of 1963, and standing rules against reckless naval encounters.","Khrushchev''s humiliation before his colleagues, contributing to his removal in 1964."]'::jsonb, '["Soviet forces in Cuba already had tactical nuclear warheads and delegated launch authority if America invaded.","The quarantine line was deliberately set 800 km out to give both sides hours of decision time.","Vasili Arkhipov, flotilla chief of staff aboard submarine B-59, refused to concur in a nuclear torpedo launch on 27 October.","Kennedy privately estimated the odds of war during the crisis as between one in three and even.","The Jupiter withdrawal from Turkey was kept secret so long that most Americans learned of it decades later."]'::jsonb, 'Every later crisis manual quotes the quarantine, the back channel through Robert Kennedy and Dobrynin, and the Turkey trade as the model of coercion plus face-saving exit. The crisis also entrenched Castro. The non-invasion pledge guaranteed his regime''s survival.', 'The closest the nuclear age has come to general war, and the proof that deterrence could fail through misreading rather than malice. Its machinery, the hotline, test-ban talks, and rules for naval encounters, was built directly from the fright of these two weeks.', '/images/plates/cuban-missile-crisis.svg', '["Nuclear Crisis","Cold War","Cuba","Deterrence"]'::jsonb);

The end came through Poland, Hungary, and Leipzig as much as Berlin. Solidarity''s election victory, Hungary''s opened Austrian border, and Monday demonstrations in Leipzig, 70,000 on 9 October, unshot, proved Moscow would not send tanks. When the regime offered limited travel reform, spokesman Günter Schabowski received the decree live on television unread, and at 18:53 on 9 November answered a reporter''s when with. Immediately, without delay.

East Berliners walked to the crossings. Outnumbered guards at Bornholmer Strasse, without orders and with crowds swelling, opened the barrier around 23:30. Strangers climbed the Wall at the Brandenburg Gate, hammered souvenirs free, and embraced western strangers. The concrete stayed for months. The state behind it lasted eleven more.', 'Gorbachev had already pulled the plug. Perestroika''s debts, glasnost''s newspapers, and his 1988 UN speech renouncing force in Eastern Europe told every satellite that Soviet tanks would not save its rulers. Poland negotiated its communists out of power in June 1989. Hungary cut its Austrian fence in September, letting East German holidaymakers walk west.

East Germany''s gerontocracy, Honecker was 77, answered with pageantry for the republic''s 40th anniversary in October while Leipzig''s crowds grew weekly. Honecker fell on 18 October. His successor Krenz offered travel reform to relieve pressure, and the bureaucracy''s own confusion did the rest.', '["Gorbachev''s withdrawal of the Brezhnev Doctrine, which removed the tanks that had guaranteed every Eastern regime since 1953.","The Hungarian border opening that punched a hole in the bloc and stranded thousands of East Germans in Prague and Budapest embassies.","Leipzig''s Monday demonstrations, whose restraint proved protest could be massive and survivable.","A botched travel decree read live on air, converting an administrative tweak into an opening order."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1989-09-11","title":"Hungary opens","description":"The Austrian fence comes down. East Germans stream west through Hungary."},{"date":"1989-10-09","title":"Leipzig holds","description":"70,000 march past armed police who do not fire, the regime''s spell breaks."},{"date":"1989-11-09","title":"Schabowski''s press conference","description":"The spokesman declares travel free effective immediately, without delay."},{"date":"1989-11-09","title":"Bornholm opens","description":"Guards yield to the crowd around 23:30. Berliners cross both ways weeping."},{"date":"1990-10-03","title":"Reunification","description":"East Germany accedes to the Federal Republic eleven months later."}]'::jsonb, '["Free movement overnight. Families divided 28 years reunited within hours.","Eastern European communism collapsing state by state through December 1989.","German reunification in 1990 and the Soviet withdrawal from Eastern Europe.","The Cold War''s symbolic end, reordering NATO, the EU, and every border east of the Elbe."]'::jsonb, '["Schabowski had not read the decree before the cameras. He improvised the effective-immediately line from the page.","Bornholmer commander Harald Jäger opened the barrier on his own authority after no ministry answered the phone.","David Hasselhoff''s Freedom performance at the Wall on New Year''s Eve 1989 was watched by a divided Germany singing along.","Wall segments were sold worldwide, one stands in a men''s restroom in Las Vegas, others in Seoul and Buenos Aires.","9 November was also the 1918 republic proclamation and the 1938 Kristallnacht, which is why reunification day became 3 October instead."]'::jsonb, 'The East Side Gallery preserves painted concrete. Mauermachers and Mauerspechte, wall woodpeckers, live in German vocabulary. November 9 now carries both 1938''s pogrom and 1989''s liberation, deliberately paired in remembrance.', 'The Wall''s fall ended the Cold War''s European division in a single night without a shot, the visual shorthand for 1989 as communism''s terminal year. It made German reunification and EU eastward expansion thinkable, and fixed 9 November as modern Germany''s fateful date.', '/images/plates/fall-of-berlin-wall.svg', '["1989","Germany","Cold War","Reunification"]'::jsonb);

The government answered neither. It kept Russia in the war, postponed land reform to a constituent assembly, and watched soviets, workers'' and soldiers'' councils, build a rival state in every barracks and factory. Lenin, returned from Swiss exile through Germany in a sealed train, distilled the program to three words the soviets understood. Peace, land, bread. Trotsky organized the Military Revolutionary Committee. On the night of 7–8 November its guards seized bridges, stations, and the Winter Palace with almost no resistance.

The second revolution''s cost came after. A Constituent Assembly dispersed at gunpoint in January 1918, a punitive peace at Brest-Litovsk, and a civil war of Reds against Whites, Greens, and fourteen intervening armies that killed millions before the Soviet Union was proclaimed in 1922.', 'The Tsarist state had survived 1905 by conceding a Duma it then emasculated, and Stolypin''s reforms created just enough independent peasants to fear losing everything. Rasputin''s sway over the Tsarina during Nicholas''s absence at the front discredited the throne, while the Progressive Bloc in the Duma demanded a government of confidence the Tsar refused.

By winter 1916–17 Petrograd ate sawdust bread on alternate days. The army, bled white at Tannenberg, the Masurian Lakes, and Brusilov''s offensives, no longer feared its officers more than the crowd, the precondition of every successful urban revolution.', '["Military catastrophe and supply collapse that destroyed the army''s willingness to defend the regime.","Bread and fuel shortages in Petrograd that turned a women''s-day strike into a general uprising.","The Tsar''s personal command of the army, which tied the throne directly to every defeat.","The Provisional Government''s refusal to exit the war or redistribute land, leaving its program to Lenin."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1917-03-08","title":"February days","description":"Strikes and mutinies force Nicholas II to abdicate within a week."},{"date":"1917-04-16","title":"Sealed train","description":"Lenin returns via Germany and issues the April Theses. No support for the government."},{"date":"1917-07-16","title":"July Days","description":"Premature Bolshevik rising collapses. Lenin flees to Finland."},{"date":"1917-09-14","title":"Kornilov affair","description":"The army''s coup attempt fails. Bolsheviks gain arms and credit defending the capital."},{"date":"1917-11-07","title":"October seizure","description":"Red Guards take Petrograd''s infrastructure. Ministers arrested in the Winter Palace."}]'::jsonb, '["The Provisional Government and Constituent Assembly swept away. One-party soviet rule installed.","Peace at Brest-Litovsk ceding vast western territories to Germany, reversed only by Germany''s own defeat.","A civil war of 1918–22 killing millions through battle, terror, and famine.","The Soviet Union proclaimed in 1922, anchoring world communism for seventy years."]'::jsonb, '["The Winter Palace assault was nearly bloodless, the famous storming was staged for Eisenstein''s film October.","Lenin wore a wig and shaved his beard to slip back into Petrograd in October.","Women''s Day, not a party directive, started the February rising, textile workers struck first.","The Tsar''s abdication document was signed on a train at Pskov after his generals advised him no army would march.","Russia adopted the Gregorian calendar in 1918, jumping 13 days overnight, hence two dates for everything."]'::jsonb, 'The USSR, the planned economy, one-party rule, and the Cold War all begin here. The revolution''s calendar itself moved. February and October are Old Style dates, February is March, October is November, by the modern calendar.', 'The first successful seizure of a great power in the name of Marxism, which created the Soviet state, the Comintern, and the twentieth century''s central ideological divide. Its model, a disciplined vanguard capturing a collapsed state, was imitated from China to Cuba.', '/images/plates/russian-revolution.svg', '["Revolution","Bolsheviks","Soviets","1917"]'::jsonb);

Shots at Lexington and Concord on 19 April 1775 turned protest into war. A Continental Congress appointed George Washington, a Virginian planter with militia experience, to forge an army from short-term provincials. Defeats around New York in 1776 gave way to survival at Valley Forge and the turning point at Saratoga in October 1777, which brought France, then Spain and the Netherlands, openly into the war with money, fleets, and troops.

Yorktown in October 1781 trapped Cornwallis between Washington''s siege and de Grasse''s fleet. Negotiations in Paris ended with the Treaty of Paris of 3 September 1783. Britain recognized an independent United States from the Atlantic to the Mississippi. A republic of quarrelsome states, not a loyal dominion, would now test whether self-government survived victory.', 'Colonial America by 1770 was richer per capita than Britain, literate, and used to running its own assemblies. The imperial crisis was constitutional as much as fiscal. Colonists claimed the rights of Englishmen, including consent to taxes, while Parliament claimed sovereignty unlimited and indivisible.

Enlightenment language met local grievance halfway. Locke on property, Montesquieu on balanced government, and a dissenting pulpit tradition gave merchants'' boycotts a philosophy, and Thomas Paine''s Common Sense of January 1776, selling some 100,000 copies, turned independence from treason into common sense within months.', '["Parliamentary taxation and trade restriction imposed without colonial representation, from stamps to tea.","The Coercive Acts'' collective punishment of Massachusetts, which united thirteen jealous colonies in a Continental Congress.","A colonial elite confident it could govern, assemblies, militias, and courts already American-run.","French willingness after Saratoga to fund revenge on Britain with loans, soldiers, and sea power."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1775-04-19","title":"Lexington and Concord","description":"Minutemen fire on redcoats. The siege of Boston begins."},{"date":"1776-07-04","title":"Declaration adopted","description":"Jefferson''s text proclaims self-evident truths and lists the King''s abuses."},{"date":"1777-10-17","title":"Saratoga","description":"Burgoyne surrenders an entire army. France enters the war."},{"date":"1778-02-06","title":"Franco-American alliance","description":"Treaties of commerce and alliance bind Paris to independence."},{"date":"1781-10-19","title":"Yorktown","description":"Cornwallis surrenders 8,000 men. British will for the war collapses."},{"date":"1783-09-03","title":"Treaty of Paris","description":"Britain recognizes the United States with the Mississippi as its western edge."}]'::jsonb, '["An independent United States stretching to the Mississippi, recognized by Britain and Europe.","A republican constitution and bill of rights that became the template for written constitutions.","Loyalist exile and Native dispossession as the revolution''s defeated paid its price.","French bankruptcy deepened by war costs, hastening 1789 in Paris."]'::jsonb, '["Washington lost more battles than he won and kept the army alive by retreating well.","France spent so much bankrolling America that the debt helped trigger its own revolution.","One in five colonists remained loyalist. Some 80,000 fled to Canada and Britain after the war.","The Yorktown siege was paid for with French coin, Rochambeau''s war chest funded the march south.","Treaty negotiations in Paris excluded America''s French allies from the final Anglo-American terms."]'::jsonb, 'The Fourth of July, the Constitution of 1787, and the presidency as an office all descend from this war. Its unfinished business, slavery protected, Native nations dispossessed, set the terms of every American conflict after.', 'The first successful colonial war of independence, which proved a republic could be founded by design and defended by citizen armies. Its constitution, bill of rights, and precedent of civilian command shaped liberal state-building from Latin America to France.', '/images/plates/american-revolution.svg', '["Independence","Republic","Thirteen Colonies","1776"]'::jsonb);

Firefighters arrived thinking it was a roof fire and fought graphite with water, absorbing lethal doses. The graphite burned for ten days, lofting a plume across Belarus, Scandinavia, and eventually the northern hemisphere. Moscow stayed silent for 36 hours. Sweden''s Forsmark station, detecting fallout on workers'' shoes, forced the announcement.

Some 600,000 liquidators, soldiers, miners, pilots, contained the ruin under a concrete sarcophagus by December. Pripyat, built for 50,000 plant families, was evacuated three days late and never reoccupied. Two workers died in the explosions and 28 more of acute radiation sickness within months. The wider toll from cancers remains bitterly contested, but the political fallout is not. Glasnost could not survive what Chernobyl revealed about secrecy.', 'Soviet nuclear power expanded fast in the 1970s as oil was reserved for export. The RBMK''s flaws, positive void coefficient, sluggish control rods tipped with graphite, were known inside the ministry but classified, so operators trained on manuals that omitted the reactor''s capacity to run away.

Gorbachev had been in office a year, preaching openness while the system still punished bearers of bad news. The test itself had failed before and was rescheduled onto a night shift that had not rehearsed it, every layer of defense, technical and human, aligned its holes.', '["An RBMK design whose physics could accelerate on coolant loss, compounded by control rods that briefly added reactivity on insertion.","A test run at low, unstable power after a ten-hour delay, with emergency cooling and automatic shutdowns disabled.","Operator violations of procedures, withdrawing too many control rods, inside a culture that rewarded plan fulfillment over safety questions.","Institutional secrecy that kept known design flaws from the men at the controls."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1986-04-26","title":"01:23 explosions","description":"Power excursion ruptures fuel channels. The reactor lid lifts and the roof burns."},{"date":"1986-04-26","title":"Firefighters'' night","description":"Crews fight graphite by hand through lethal radiation. First acute deaths within weeks."},{"date":"1986-04-28","title":"Sweden sounds the alarm","description":"Forsmark detects the plume. Moscow admits an accident the same evening."},{"date":"1986-05-01","title":"Kyiv parade held","description":"May Day marches proceed in fallout while evacuation of Pripyat began days late."},{"date":"1986-12-01","title":"Sarcophagus closed","description":"The concrete shelter entombs Reactor 4. A new arch follows in 2016."}]'::jsonb, '["Pripyat and dozens of villages abandoned. A 30-km zone closed indefinitely.","Soviet nuclear credibility destroyed at home and abroad. RBMKs retrofitted under international scrutiny.","Glasnost radicalized, the press learned it could report catastrophe and kept reporting.","A contested health ledger. Dozens of acute deaths, thousands of thyroid cancers, and decades of monitoring."]'::jsonb, '["The elephants foot, a lava of melted fuel and concrete, still sits beneath Reactor 4, lethally radioactive for centuries.","Miners dug a cooling tunnel under the reactor in weeks, stripping naked in heat that melted their instruments.","Three divers, Ananenko, Bezpalov, and Baranov, waded through radioactive water to drain the bubbler pools and prevent a steam explosion.","Pripyat''s amusement park was to open 1 May. Its Ferris wheel never ran and became the zone''s emblem.","Wolves, lynx, and eagles now outnumber people in the zone, an accidental rewilding reserve."]'::jsonb, 'The 30-kilometre Exclusion Zone, the New Safe Confinement arch of 2016, and thyroid-cancer screening programs persist. Every nuclear regulator since cites Chernobyl''s lesson. A test that disables safety is not a test but a gamble.', 'The worst nuclear accident ever rated, INES Level 7, and the moment Soviet secrecy became indefensible. It forced open reporting of flaws across the RBMK fleet, ended the illusion that civilian nuclear risk could be managed by decree, and accelerated glasnost into an uncontrollable audit of the system.', '/images/plates/chernobyl-disaster.svg', '["Nuclear","Soviet Union","1986","Disaster"]'::jsonb);

At 23:40 on 14 April, 600 km southeast of Newfoundland, lookout Frederick Fleet rang the crow''s-nest bell. Iceberg right ahead. First Officer Murdoch ordered hard-a-starboard and full astern, but 52,000 tons at 22 knots needs a mile to turn. The berg scraped the starboard bow, popping rivets and opening six compartments to the sea, two more than the design could survive.

By 02:20 the ship broke and went down. The Carpathia arrived at dawn to 705 survivors in boats. More than 1,500 died, mostly in freezing water within minutes. The inquiries on both sides of the Atlantic blamed speed in ice, too few lifeboats, and a wireless room buried in passenger messages, and rewrote maritime law within two years.', 'Edwardian liners competed on size and luxury, not safety. Emigrant traffic paid, first-class opulence advertised, and the Board of Trade''s lifeboat rules, written for 10,000-ton ships, had not been updated for vessels four times larger. Titanic carried 20 boats for 1,178 people because the law required 16 and Harland & Wolff''s davits could have taken more that nobody ordered.

Wireless was new and unregulated. Titanic''s Marconi men worked for the Marconi Company, prioritizing paid passenger traffic over ice warnings. The nearest ship, Californian, had shut its set for the night ten miles away.', '["Full speed through a known ice field on a moonless night to make New York headlines.","A glancing blow that buckled plates over 90 metres, flooding six compartments against a four-compartment design.","Lifeboats for barely half aboard, launched half-empty for lack of drills and muster discipline.","Wireless failures. Ignored ice warnings and a silenced Californian that could have saved hundreds."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1912-04-10","title":"Departure","description":"Titanic sails Southampton via Cherbourg and Queenstown with 2,224 aboard."},{"date":"1912-04-14","title":"23:40 impact","description":"Lookouts spot the berg. The starboard bow scrapes and compartments flood."},{"date":"1912-04-15","title":"00:45 first boat","description":"Distress rockets fire. Boats lower half-full as the band plays on."},{"date":"1912-04-15","title":"02:20 sinking","description":"The ship breaks between funnels and disappears. Screams fade within the hour."},{"date":"1912-04-15","title":"04:10 rescue","description":"Carpathia hauls 705 survivors from boats. The rest are lost to cold."}]'::jsonb, '["More than 1,500 dead, the worst peacetime liner loss to that date.","SOLAS 1914. Lifeboats for all, continuous wireless watch, mandatory drills.","The International Ice Patrol, funded by liner nations and still operating.","White Star''s reputation sunk with the ship. The line merged into Cunard within two decades."]'::jsonb, '["The band played to keep order, none of the eight musicians survived.","Millionaire John Jacob Astor IV died after reportedly joking about the lifeboat arrangements.","The Californian''s rockets were seen from Titanic''s boats, ten miles of dark water away.","Titanic''s baker, Charles Joughin, survived hours in freezing water, reportedly steadied by whisky and debris.","A first-class ticket cost up to 870 pounds, roughly a decade''s wages for a labourer."]'::jsonb, 'Wreck found in 1985 at 3,800 metres, now a protected grave decaying under iron-eating bacteria. Every safety drill, muster station, and iceberg chart in the North Atlantic descends from five April hours.', 'The disaster that ended Edwardian confidence in progress by engineering alone. It produced SOLAS, Safety of Life at Sea, still the basis of maritime law. Boats for all, 24-hour wireless watch, and the International Ice Patrol that still flies from Newfoundland.', '/images/plates/titanic.svg', '["Shipwreck","1912","Maritime Law","Atlantic"]'::jsonb);

What followed was pursuit without end. Through Persepolis, burned in unclear circumstances, across the Hindu Kush in winter, to the Indus, where his men mutinied at the Hyphasis in 326 BC and refused to march to the Ganges. The return through the Gedrosian desert cost thousands. Back in Babylon, planning an Arabian campaign, Alexander fell ill after days of drinking and died on 10 June 323 BC, thirty-two years old, without a competent heir.

His generals, the Diadochi, carved the conquests into kingdoms. Ptolemaic Egypt, Seleucid Asia, Antigonid Macedon. Greek became the language of government from the Nile to the Indus for three centuries, and the cities he planted carried it.', 'Philip II had already revolutionized warfare. The sarissa, a five-metre pike, wielded by drilled phalanxes, plus Companion cavalry that struck like a hammer on the phalanx''s anvil. Greece''s city-states, exhausted by decades of mutual war, could not resist a professional national army.

Persia, for all its size, was brittle at the top, Darius III newly enthroned, satraps jealous, and a court that expected the king to win in person. Alexander, tutored by Aristotle and raised on the Iliad, understood both the army he inherited and the heroic script his men wanted to follow.', '["Philip''s army and treasury. A professional force and full war chest purpose-built for Persia.","Persian dynastic weakness that let a single battlefield defeat unhinge whole provinces.","Alexander''s personal leadership, fighting in the front rank of the cavalry, which made mutiny unthinkable until India.","A policy of fusion, from adopting Persian dress to marrying Roxana, that co-opted eastern elites instead of merely occupying them."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"-0334-05-01","title":"Granicus","description":"First victory in Asia. Persian satraps'' army broken on a riverbank."},{"date":"-0333-11-05","title":"Issus","description":"Darius flees, abandoning family and treasure. The Levant opens."},{"date":"-0332-01-01","title":"Tyre and Egypt","description":"Seven-month siege ends in massacre. Alexandria founded on the Nile delta."},{"date":"-0331-10-01","title":"Gaugamela","description":"The Persian Empire''s field army annihilated. Babylon and Persepolis fall."},{"date":"-0326-07-01","title":"Hyphasis mutiny","description":"Exhausted troops refuse India. The eastward march ends at the Beas."},{"date":"-0323-06-10","title":"Death in Babylon","description":"Fever, malaria, typhoid, or poison in later gossip, kills him at thirty-two."}]'::jsonb, '["The Persian Empire replaced by Greek-speaking kingdoms from Egypt to India.","Greek as the Near East''s common language for administration, science, and scripture.","Some seventy foundations named Alexandria carrying Greek civic life deep into Asia.","A succession bloodbath among the Diadochi lasting forty years."]'::jsonb, '["His horse Bucephalas, bought against advice as a boy''s gamble, carried him to India and got a city named for him.","At Siwa oasis the oracle greeted him as son of Ammon, divine sonship he never discouraged afterward.","He staged mass weddings at Susa, marrying 80 officers to Persian noblewomen in one ceremony.","The Gordian Knot was cut, or untied, accounts differ, as staged proof that Asia awaited him.","His tomb in Alexandria was visited by emperors for centuries. Its location is now lost."]'::jsonb, 'Alexandrias from Egypt to Afghanistan, the Septuagint Bible in Greek, and the Diadochi kingdoms that Rome spent two centuries absorbing. His name remains the standard of military genius and of ambition that outruns institutions.', 'Alexander fused the eastern Mediterranean and western Asia into one Hellenistic world. Greek cities, Greek law-courts, and the Koine Greek that the New Testament would be written in. Rome inherited that world whole, and with it the model of the conquering hero-king every Caesar and Napoleon consciously imitated.', '/images/plates/alexander-the-great.svg', '["Conqueror","Hellenistic","Macedon","Antiquity"]'::jsonb);

Charles''s theologians at Poitiers examined her and found nothing heretical. The court, desperate, gave her armour, a banner, and a place in the relief army for Orléans, besieged since October 1428. She entered the city on 29 April, and French assaults, with Joan in the front rank, wounded by an arrow, took the English forts in four days. The Loire campaign followed, then the march to Reims, where Charles was crowned Charles VII on 17 July with Joan holding her standard beside him.

The favor curdled within a year. Captured at Compiègne on 23 May 1430 by Burgundians, sold to the English for 10,000 livres, she faced a church court at Rouen under Bishop Cauchon. Condemned for heresy, relapse, and cross-dressing, she burned on 30 May 1431, nineteen years old. A retrial in 1456 annulled the verdict. Canonization followed in 1920.', 'France in 1429 was two kingdoms. Lancastrian Normandy and Paris under Bedford''s regency, Burgundian east, and a shrinking Armagnac south loyal to a dauphin whose own legitimacy the Treaty of Troyes denied. Orléans blocked the English road south, its fall would have cut the remaining France in two.

The war was also a crisis of signs. Prophecies circulated that France would be lost by a woman and saved by a virgin from Lorraine''s marches. Joan arrived inside a story the desperate already knew, which is why a peasant girl got an audience no captain could buy.', '["A military emergency at Orléans that made the court willing to try any remedy, including a visionary teenager.","A prophetic tradition that framed her exactly as the promised maid, priming commanders and crowds to follow.","Charles VII''s need for sacral legitimacy, which a God-sent virgin supplied better than lawyers.","Her own tactical sense, aggression, artillery concentration, and refusal to plunder, which impressed professionals like Dunois."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1429-02-23","title":"Ride to Chinon","description":"Eleven days through Burgundian country to meet the dauphin."},{"date":"1429-05-08","title":"Orléans relieved","description":"English forts fall in four days. The siege lifts after seven months."},{"date":"1429-06-18","title":"Patay","description":"French cavalry destroys the English field army. The road to Reims opens."},{"date":"1429-07-17","title":"Coronation at Reims","description":"Charles crowned VII with Joan''s banner at his side."},{"date":"1430-05-23","title":"Capture","description":"Pulled from her horse at Compiègne and sold to the English."},{"date":"1431-05-30","title":"Execution","description":"Burned at Rouen marketplace after a political trial for heresy."}]'::jsonb, '["The Hundred Years'' War''s momentum reversed. France gradually expelled England by 1453.","Valois kingship sacralized at Reims after decades of disputed succession.","A show trial whose records preserved the victim''s voice better than any chronicle.","A national symbol claimed by left, right, and church across six centuries."]'::jsonb, '["She could not write, she dictated letters marked with a cross and a seal.","Her sword, found behind the altar at Sainte-Catherine-de-Fierbois as her voices promised, broke at the coronation march.","The Rouen trial''s Latin record runs thousands of pages. Her answers were sharper than her judges'' questions.","She was wounded twice, an arrow at Orléans, a crossbow bolt at Paris, and returned to the line both times.","Napoleon reportedly kept her statue from royalist cults by declaring her a national, not clerical, heroine."]'::jsonb, 'Rehabilitated in 1456, canonized 1920, patron saint of France. Her banner, armor inventories, and trial transcripts survive, the best-documented life of any medieval non-royal.', 'Orléans reversed a century of English victories and made Valois succession a sacred cause rather than a dynastic claim. Joan fixed the image of France as a nation worth dying for, invoked by every French regime since, from monarchists to republicans to the Resistance.', '/images/plates/joan-of-arc.svg', '["Hundred Years War","France","Trial","Saint"]'::jsonb);

Turing''s Hut 8 broke Naval Enigma, the hardest variant, by attacking procedure as much as mathematics. The Banburismus technique for reducing rotor possibilities, then the Bombe, an electromechanical machine, specified by Turing and built by Harold Keen, that tested wheel settings at speed. When U-boat wolf packs starved Britain in 1942, Hut 8''s breaks into Shark and Dolphin keys rerouted convoys around them. Historians debate the war-shortening at one to two years. The Admiralty at the time simply called the breaks indispensable.

After the war Turing designed the ACE computer, wrote pioneering chess and morphogenesis papers, and proposed the imitation game for machine intelligence. In 1952 he was prosecuted for homosexual acts, then a crime, and sentenced to chemical castration. He died in 1954 of cyanide poisoning, ruled suicide. The government apologized in 2009. A royal pardon followed in 2013.', 'Enigma''s military model used rotors, plugboards, and daily keys producing 159 quintillion settings for the naval version, unbreakable by hand. Poland''s Cipher Bureau had already broken early Enigma and passed its Bombes and methods to Britain and France in July 1939, weeks before the war.

Bletchley industrialized that lead. Thousands of staff, mostly women, in huts and blocks running Bombes around the clock. Turing arrived into a system ready to mechanize his mathematics, the meeting of theory and factory that defined Allied signals intelligence.', '["Polish breakthroughs of 1932–39 that gave Britain working Enigma replicas and the bomba concept.","German procedural errors, cribs like weather reports and the prohibition on a letter enciphering as itself, that gave mathematics its footholds.","Bletchley''s industrial scale, which turned Turing''s specifications into dozens of running Bombes.","Naval Enigma''s centrality to the Atlantic battle, which made Hut 8''s keys the highest-value intelligence in Britain."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1936-05-28","title":"Universal machine","description":"On Computable Numbers defines computation itself."},{"date":"1939-09-04","title":"Joins Bletchley","description":"Reports to Hut 8 the day after war is declared."},{"date":"1940-03-18","title":"First Bombe","description":"Victory, built to Turing''s specification, begins testing Enigma settings."},{"date":"1941-05-01","title":"Naval breaks","description":"Hut 8 reads U-boat keys through the crisis months of the Atlantic."},{"date":"1950-10-01","title":"Turing Test paper","description":"Computing Machinery and Intelligence poses the imitation game."},{"date":"1952-03-31","title":"Conviction","description":"Pleads guilty to gross indecency. Chemical castration imposed."}]'::jsonb, '["Atlantic convoys rerouted around wolf packs through the decisive years.","An industrial signals-intelligence complex that became GCHQ and its American counterpart.","Stored-program computing accelerated by wartime electronics and Turing''s ACE design.","A posthumous reckoning that overturned Britain''s treatment of gay veterans."]'::jsonb, '["Turing cycled to Bletchley wearing a gas mask against hay fever, a landmark colleagues navigated by.","He buried silver bars in the woods and, forgetting the map coordinates, never recovered them.","The apple beside his deathbed fed the Snow White myth. The inquest found cyanide but no tested apple.","He wrote the first chess program on paper in 1948 and played whole games simulating the computer himself.","His 1952 morphogenesis paper explained stripes and spots with reaction-diffusion equations biology confirmed decades later."]'::jsonb, 'The Turing Award, the test that bears his name, and Bletchley Park as a museum. The 2013 pardon became the legal model for pardoning thousands convicted under the same laws.', 'Turing founded computer science before computers and artificial intelligence before the term existed, then proved both abstractions decisive in war. The Bombe''s industrial codebreaking became the template for signals intelligence, and the universal machine became the definition every computer still satisfies.', '/images/plates/alan-turing.svg', '["Enigma","Computing","Bletchley Park","AI"]'::jsonb);

From that rising emerged Toussaint Louverture, a freedman, coachman, and herbalist who built a disciplined army from the insurgency, allied and un-allied with Spain and Britain in turn, and by 1801 governed the whole island under a constitution that abolished slavery but kept plantation labour compulsory. Napoleon, needing the island''s revenue for his American plans, sent 40,000 troops under Leclerc in 1802 with secret orders to restore slavery. Toussaint was seized by treachery and shipped to a Jura prison, where he died in 1803.

His lieutenants finished the work. Jean-Jacques Dessalines defeated the disease-wrecked French at Vertières on 18 November 1803, yellow fever killed more French than bullets, and proclaimed independent Haiti on 1 January 1804. It was the only state born of a slave revolt, and France made it pay. An 1825 indemnity for lost property, including human property, that strangled Haiti''s finances for a century.', 'The colony''s wealth depended on an enslaved majority outnumbering whites fifteen to one, worked to death on sugar and coffee. The 1790s added imperial war. Britain and Spain both invaded to grab the colony, and the French Republic abolished slavery in 1794 less from principle than to keep Toussaint''s armies on its side.

Every faction armed Black soldiers and then feared them. The British landed 25,000 men between 1793 and 1798 and lost most to disease. The Spanish armed auxiliaries they later abandoned. By 1801 Toussaint commanded the only functioning state on the island.', '["A plantation system of exceptional brutality and demographic imbalance that required constant imports to replace the dead.","The French Revolution''s rights language, which free coloreds and then the enslaved turned against the colonists.","Imperial war after 1793 that put muskets in Black hands and made every empire bid for their loyalty.","Napoleon''s 1802 expedition to restore slavery, which reunited a divided Black leadership against France."]'::jsonb, '[{"date":"1791-08-22","title":"Night of fire","description":"Northern plain rises. A thousand plantations burn within weeks."},{"date":"1794-02-04","title":"Abolition decreed","description":"Paris abolishes slavery. Toussaint rallies to the Republic."},{"date":"1801-07-09","title":"Toussaint''s constitution","description":"Saint-Domingue governs itself with forced labour but free status."},{"date":"1802-06-07","title":"Toussaint seized","description":"Arrested at a parley and deported to Fort de Joux."},{"date":"1803-11-18","title":"Vertières","description":"Dessalines destroys the remaining French field army."},{"date":"1804-01-01","title":"Independence","description":"Haiti proclaimed at Gonaïves. Slavery abolished permanently."}]'::jsonb, '["An independent Black republic in a hemisphere of slavery, abolishing bondage permanently.","Napoleon''s American project collapsed. The Louisiana Purchase followed within months.","Some 350,000 Haitians and tens of thousands of French, British, and Spanish dead.","An 1825 indemnity that mortgaged Haiti''s treasury for over a century."]'::jsonb, '["More British soldiers died in Saint-Domingue (1793–98) than at Waterloo, overwhelmingly of yellow fever.","Toussaint''s army wore captured uniforms sorted by size and drilled to European manuals translated aloud.","Napoleon''s sister Pauline accompanied Leclerc and reportedly carried on through the disaster in full society style.","The Citadelle Laferrière, built to repel a French return, is the largest fortress in the Americas.","France''s 1825 demand, 150 million francs for lost slaves, was collected by gunboat diplomacy."]'::jsonb, 'Haiti''s Creole, Vodou, and citadel at Laferrière all date from the revolutionary generation. The revolution''s example armed abolitionism everywhere and its indemnity burdened Haiti until 1947.', 'The only slave revolt to found a state, which destroyed Napoleon''s American empire, Louisiana was sold because Saint-Domingue was lost, and terrified every slaveholding power into tightening control. Its 1825 indemnity became the textbook case of freedom priced as debt.', '/images/plates/haitian-revolution.svg', '["Slavery","Revolution","Caribbean","Independence"]'::jsonb);
commit;
